/57e072f9-bea9-5045-9286-a2d475c04cb7', 'word_count': 2389, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57e072f9-bea9-5045-9286-a2d475c04cb7'}, {'abstract': 'The of-the-moment curator Nicola Vassell has assembled “Black Eye,” a survey featuring 26 contemporary artists of African descent.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/01/on-view-a-group-show-in-tribeca-explores-black-identity-today/', 'snippet': 'The of-the-moment curator Nicola Vassell has assembled “Black Eye,” a survey featuring 26 contemporary artists of African descent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The curator Nicola Vassell has a blue-chip art-world pedigree as a former director at both Deitch Projects and Pace. And perhaps because her gallery work positioned her in contexts where glitz and glamour are currencies of their own, media coverage of Vassell has often focused on her taste and style — her former modeling career, her wardrobe, the social goings-on at her SoHo loft. But Vassell has lately gone independent — she left Pace in 2012 to found Concept NV, which she describes as an art ideation lab — and it’s clear she’s got more pressing social concerns on her mind. “Activism is important to me,” she says. “I find that sometimes the art world is too busy with its art-world view to consider that.” Her latest show, “Black Eye,” underscores the continuing need for increased diversity in a still narrow art world. The wide-ranging exhibition, which opens tomorrow night in TriBeCa, features paintings, photographs and collage by 26 emerging and established black artists, including Wangechi Mutu, Jacolby Satterwhite, Steve McQueen and Kerry James Marshall.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Group Show in TriBeCa Explores Black Identity Today', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-05-01T22:10:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dawn Chan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dawn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f379f97-1957-5d1a-9de2-f170f8bb1e60', 'word_count': 370, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f379f97-1957-5d1a-9de2-f170f8bb1e60'}, {'abstract': 'A critical guide to exhibitions and installations in the New York area.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/02/arts/design/art-listings-for-may-2-8.html', 'snippet': 'A critical guide to exhibitions and installations in the New York area.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Museums and galleries are in Manhattan unless otherwise noted. Full reviews of recent art shows: nytimes.com/art. A searchable guide to these and many other art shows is at nytimes.com/events.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Art Listings for May 2-8', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Listings: Art', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-01T22:12:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3bcf514-00a2-5992-96e7-a803939b9e08', 'word_count': 5979, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3bcf514-00a2-5992-96e7-a803939b9e08'}, {'abstract': 'The Museum of the Moving Image will show 30 films, some rarely screened, as part of a retrospective of the films of the Japanese director Kenji Mizoguchi.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/02/movies/mizoguchi-films-in-a-museum-of-the-moving-image-series.html', 'snippet': 'The Museum of the Moving Image will show 30 films, some rarely screened, as part of a retrospective of the films of the Japanese director Kenji Mizoguchi.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ranking the great Japanese filmmakers of the mid-20th century is an easy parlor game, made convenient by the long, prolific and amazingly consistent careers of the major contenders. The big three of the Japanese golden age are traditionally acknowledged to be Akira Kurosawa, Kenji Mizoguchi and Yasujiro Ozu, born within 12 years of one another around the turn of the 20th century. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/02/arts/02SUBMIZO/02SUBMIZO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 434, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/02/arts/02SUBMIZO/02SUBMIZO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 434}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/02/arts/02SUBMIZO/02SUBMIZO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 741, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/02/arts/02SUBMIZO/02SUBMIZO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1481, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/02/arts/02SUBMIZO/02SUBMIZO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/02/arts/02SUBMIZO/02SUBMIZO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/02/arts/02SUBMIZO/02SUBMIZO-thumbLarge.jpg', 'heigh