rld, a private civilization of tenderness and warmth, population two. The novel is a triumph of the obsessed imagination thriving on a real paucity of resources.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04MCRATH/04MCRATH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 313, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04MCRATH/04MCRATH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 313}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04MCRATH/04MCRATH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 534, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04MCRATH/04MCRATH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1068, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04MCRATH/04MCRATH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04MCRATH/04MCRATH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04MCRATH/04MCRATH-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gomorrah by the Bay', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gomorrah by the Bay', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Frog Music (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Donoghue, Emma', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-02T14:38:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick McGrath', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGrath', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/20d47432-d8d0-5301-ac33-4345c9f9f2bc', 'word_count': 561, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20d47432-d8d0-5301-ac33-4345c9f9f2bc'}, {'abstract': 'An episode from World War II is brought into the present.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/04/books/review/love-and-treasure-by-ayelet-waldman.html', 'snippet': 'An episode from World War II is brought into the present.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2005, the settlement of a notorious case of postwar liability took place in a federal court in Florida. Sixty years previously, the so-called Hungarian Gold Train had arrived near Salzburg, Austria, bearing property requisitioned by the Nazis from Hungary’s estimated 800,000 Jewish citizens. Making its way to Germany as the Allies closed in, the train was intercepted by the Americans. Some of its cargo — jewelry, glassware, carpets, bed linen, cutlery, furs, furniture, gold coins, watches and much else from households whose occupants had vanished into the concentration camps — was subsequently commandeered by the generals working on the Marshall Plan, who needed to furnish their headquarters. Numerous items were irretrievably lost, never to be seen again by their rightful owners or by their owners’ heirs. It would take more than half a century for Hungarian survivors of the Holocaust to settle a lawsuit that eventually granted $21 million as compensation to a number of Jewish social service agencies.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04TAYLOR/04TAYLOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 734, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04TAYLOR/04TAYLOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 734}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04TAYLOR/04TAYLOR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 838, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04TAYLOR/04TAYLOR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1675, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04TAYLOR/04TAYLOR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04TAYLOR/04TAYLOR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04TAYLOR/04TAYLOR-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Train of Jewels', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Train of Jewels', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waldman, Ayelet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-02T14:38:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/86d048a3-7043-5aba-aead-348b45229b0d', 'word_count': 641, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86d048a3-7043-5aba-aead-348b45229b0d'}, {'abstract': 'Resilience is tested by a range of disasters, natural and otherwise, in this story collection.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/04/books/review/acts-of-god-by-ellen-gilchrist.html', 'snippet': 'Resilience is tested by a range of disasters, natural and otherwise, in this story collection.', 'lead_paragraph': '“ ‘Life on the planet Earth. . . . Wars and strife and surprises and love and children and art. . . . This is our parade and I’m marching in it.’ She squeezed my hand and we went back to writing on our postcards and eating croissants and being here, glad to be alive in the only world there is, alive and eating and still breathing and not afraid really of anything that might happen next. We were Americans, for God’s sake, we weren’t in the habit of being afraid.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04HANDLER/04HANDLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 511, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04HANDLER/04HANDLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 511}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04HANDLER/04HANDLER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 872, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04HANDLER/04HANDLER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1745, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04HANDLER/04HANDLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04HANDLER/04HANDLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/04/books/review/04HANDLER/04HANDLER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In for Nasty Weather', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In for Nasty Weather', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gilchrist, Ellen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-02T14:39:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Handler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Handler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b801f3e0-3564-55d4-9e0a-813760a96789', 'word_count': 894, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b801f3e0-3564-55d4-9e0a-813760a96789'}, {'abstract': 'Robin Roberts, whose memoir “Everybody’s Got Something” debuts at No. 3 on the hardcover nonfiction list, says: “I believe optimism is a choice — a muscle that gets stronger with use.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/11/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Robin Roberts, whose memoir “Everybody’s Got Something” debuts at No. 3 on the hardcover nonfiction list, says: “I believe optimism is a choice — a muscle that gets stronger with use.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Je Ne Sais Quoi: “Everybody’s Got Something,” a new memoir by the “Good Morning America” anchor Robin Roberts, makes its debut on the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 3. Roberts’s book is like one of those packets of heavily sweetened instant oatmeal — you can pretend it’s healthy even as you search out the sugar bombs of drama (her medical crises, her mother’s death, her ratings war with the “Today” show) and celebrity gossip (her 10-year relationship with the girlfriend she met on a blind date: “I liked the fact that she had no idea who I was”). Roberts delivers all of this in the soothing, upbeat voice that has made her so formidable a presence on morning TV. “I’m no Pollyanna,” she says in the current issue of Good Housekeeping, “but I believe optimism is a choice — a muscle that gets stronger with use. Right foot, left foot . . . just keep moving.” This spirit of levelheaded resolve is widely credited with helping Roberts topple “Today” from its perch in 2012. But she almost didn’t get the chance. “She was a woman of great personal dignity, stately beauty and, what’s most important in her mysterious genre, a certain je ne sais quoi that you can’t just order up by the ounce from human resources,” the media reporter Brian Stelter wrote of Roberts in “Top of the Morning,” his 2013 best seller about the competition for breakfast-hour viewers. “Naturally, because morning TV still comes down to a group of elite men trying to guess the needs of a mass audience of mostly women, many of the powers that were at ABC wanted to fire her” as soon as feasible.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia':