'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6131ce19-1b29-57e3-af0f-0689ecef0923', 'word_count': 33, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6131ce19-1b29-57e3-af0f-0689ecef0923'}, {'abstract': 'Welfare policy, economic incentives, and bad boyfriends.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/02/making-men-marriageable/', 'snippet': 'Welfare policy, economic incentives, and bad boyfriends.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Continuing my recent series of posts offering what I hope is constructive criticism for anti-inequality liberals, let me address\xa0this recent piece from Naomi Cahn and June Carbone, two sociologists I’ve both learned from and differed with in the past, in which they make a case for the wisdom of working-class single motherhood in an age of downscale male underemployment. Using the pseudonymous case of “Lily,” a single mom for whom the father of her baby, “Carl,” is more a burden than a helper, they argue that it’s often much more sensible for women who want children to get pregnant and raise them on their own than to hold out for a decent, productive, reliable man who may never actually materialize:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Making Men Marriageable', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food Stamps', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Liberalism (US Politics)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minimum Wage', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment Insurance', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cahn, Naomi', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murray, Charles', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-02T16:13:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b25013e-c1be-54e4-9e80-a922319fb98a', 'word_count': 1269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b25013e-c1be-54e4-9e80-a922319fb98a'}, {'abstract': 'Seen less frequently accompanying the leader in his public activities, it remained unclear whether Vice Marshal Choe Ryong-hae was removed from other key positions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/03/world/asia/korean-official-loses-a-post.html', 'snippet': 'Seen less frequently accompanying the leader in his public activities, it remained unclear whether Vice Marshal Choe Ryong-hae was removed from other key positions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEOUL, South Korea — Kim Jong-un, the North Korean leader, has removed an official who was widely considered No. 2 in his government from top military and party posts, according to reports in the country’s state-run news media over the weekend.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/02/world/KOREA/KOREA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/02/world/KOREA/KOREA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption'