d', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kimbrough', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/f7147c2b-42c4-5c4a-9dd5-8418ad62003a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/f7147c2b-42c4-5c4a-9dd5-8418ad62003a'}, {'abstract': 'William Kent Krueger won the Edgar for best novel for “Ordinary Grace.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/02/edgar-awards-handed-out-for-top-mystery-works/', 'snippet': 'William Kent Krueger won the Edgar for best novel for “Ordinary Grace.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'William Kent Krueger walked off with the award for best novel for his “Ordinary Grace” (Simon & Schuster/Ataria) on Thursday night as the Mystery Writers of America presented the 2014 Edgar Awards at their annual banquet in New York. The awards honor the best mystery writing in several categories, including fiction, nonfiction and television. Mr. Krueger’s book is about a man’s look back at the summer of 1961 in Minnesota, when he was 13, and death “assumed many forms.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Edgar Awards Handed Out for Top Mystery Works', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Edgar Awards Presented', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krueger, William Kent', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Matthews, Jason (1951- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mystery Writers of America', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-02T17:35:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e802527-2091-524c-8767-16a3b218ab5c', 'word_count': 157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e802527-2091-524c-8767-16a3b218ab5c'}, {'abstract': 'Street art made using botanical matter is sprouting in locales from Brooklyn to Budapest.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/02/graffiti-made-of-plants-not-paint-edina-tokodi-spy-julie-forand-sprout-guerrilla-anna-garforth/', 'snippet': 'Street art made using botanical matter is sprouting in locales from Brooklyn to Budapest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In recent years, city walls have become fertile artistic ground — quite literally — for a new wave of graffiti art that uses live plants instead of paint. Many such artists borrow their secretive practices from tagger tradition, jealously guarding their walls and rarely revealing their locations. But as in the spray-can graffiti world, many also accept commissions from public or private organizations to spruce up the streetscape.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'All the Rage | Graffiti Made of Plants, Not Paint', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graffiti', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trees and Shrubs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-02T17:36:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula De La Cruz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'De La Cruz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3b46717-c3c2-5f73-99ab-685992d7eaa7', 'word_count': 461, 'uri'