': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/97dc4678-13e1-5636-80e8-1b0ea3e5542a', 'word_count': 1006, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97dc4678-13e1-5636-80e8-1b0ea3e5542a'}, {'abstract': 'The celebrated young author, whose new short-story collection is out May 6, discusses a few of her favorite things with T.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/02/under-the-influence-rivka-galchen-on-comic-strips-lucille-ball-and-what-shes-reading-now/', 'snippet': 'The celebrated young author, whose new short-story collection is out May 6, discusses a few of her favorite things with T.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rivka’s Galchen’s 2009 debut novel “Atmospheric Disturbances” was filled with pleasurably twisty sentences and casually engaging intellectualism, earning her effusive praise and comparisons to Jonathen Lethem, Thomas Pynchon and Jorge Luis Borges. Her new book, “American Innovations” (out May 6 from Farrar, Straus and Giroux) gathers 10 short stories — each written from a female perspective in response to a canonical work like Gogol’s “The Nose” and Borges’s “The Aleph” — that are sharp and rich, astutely observed and wildly imaginative.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/02/t-magazine/02galchen-miller-slide-4O76/02galchen-miller-slide-4O76-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 801, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/02/t-magazine/02galchen-miller-slide-4O76/02galchen-miller-slide-4O76-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 801}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/02/t-magazine/02galchen-miller-slide-4O76/02galchen-miller-slide-4O76-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/02/t-magazine/02galchen-miller-slide-4O76/02galchen-miller-slide-4O76-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2000, 'width': 1499, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/02/t-magazine/02galchen-miller-slide-4O76/02galchen-miller-slide-4O76-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/02/t-magazine/02galchen-miller-slide-4O76/02galchen-miller-slide-4O76-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/02/t-magazine/02galchen-miller-slide-4O76/02galchen-miller-slide-4O76-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rivka Galchen on Comic Strips, Lucille Ball and What She’s Reading Now', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Galchen, Rivka', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-02T18:30:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea923527-08f2-5cdd-abdd-ed02cd064969', 'word_count': 180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea923527-08f2-5cdd-abdd-ed02cd064969'}, {'abstract': 'Geoff Dyer, Liesl Schillinger, Justin Go and Janne Teller discussed the literature of World War I at an event on Wednesday night.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/02/a-great-war-reading-list-at-pen-world-voices-festival/', 'snippet': 'Geoff Dyer, Liesl Schillinger, Justin Go and Janne Teller discussed the literature of World War I at an event on Wednesday night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'July is the centenary of the outbreak of World War I, but publishers got a major jump on the anniversary last year. Christopher Clark’s “The Sleepwalkers,” Margaret Macmillan’s “The War That Ended Peace,” Max Hastings’s “Catastrophe 1914” and Sean McMeekin’s “July 1914” were just four of the hundreds of books to mark the moment a year early. The deluge continues in 2014, with, well, “The Deluge” by Adam Tooze (coming in November), David Reynolds’s “The Long Shadow” and many more.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/02/arts/02artsbeat-somme/02artsbeat-somme-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 492, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/02/arts/02artsbeat-somme/02artsbeat-somme-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 492}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/02/arts/02artsbeat-somme/02artsbeat-somme-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 840, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/02/arts/02artsbeat-somme/02artsbeat-somme-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1681, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/02/arts/02artsbeat-somme/02artsbeat-somme-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy'