: 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Levine, Adam', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-02T19:47:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Logan Hill', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Logan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hill', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51a8cef7-8098-56a5-b182-1bd09b51269d', 'word_count': 1439, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51a8cef7-8098-56a5-b182-1bd09b51269d'}, {'abstract': 'James Naismith had strong feelings against segregation and was a mentor to a student who became the first black coach in professional basketball.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/03/upshot/choices-on-race-even-from-basketballs-beginnings.html', 'snippet': 'James Naismith had strong feelings against segregation and was a mentor to a student who became the first black coach in professional basketball.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the end of a week in which the Los Angeles Clippers owner Donald Sterling became, for a moment, the most talked-about man in basketball, let’s rewind the historical videotape and remember the Canadian-born farmer-chaplain-physician who invented the game. Here is James Naismith in 1928, holding a peach basket for his wife, Maude, to make a shot. Naismith seems now like an anachronistic figure, yet even nearly a century ago he too had to make serious choices about issues like race and money in basketball.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/02/upshot/03UP-BESCHLOSS/03UP-BESCHLOSS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 438, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/02/upshot/03UP-BESCHLOSS/03UP-BESCHLOSS-articleLarge.jpg