'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Al Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Al', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/142cf983-57a3-516e-86ef-2761da5f9bbd', 'word_count': 805, 'uri': 'nyt://article/142cf983-57a3-516e-86ef-2761da5f9bbd'}, {'abstract': 'With colleges failing to protect students, the White House and some legislators finally take steps to combat sexual assault.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/03/opinion/washington-weighs-in-on-campus-assaults.html', 'snippet': 'With colleges failing to protect students, the White House and some legislators finally take steps to combat sexual assault.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It keeps happening. One student sexually assaults another on campus. College administrators discourage the victim from opening a case against her assailant or take their time launching an investigation; meanwhile, the two keep living together in the same dorm. Eventually the victim speaks out or files a Title IX complaint and attracts national media attention. The administration pledges to improve its handling of these situations.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Washington Weighs in on Campus Assaults', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Washington Weighs in on Campus Assaults', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-03T01:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff585c67-5ac5-5bac-a7db-085494c37e1b', 'word_count': 475, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff585c67-5ac5-5bac-a7db-085494c37e1b'}, {'abstract': 'A threat to request the extradition of a Turkish preacher living in Pennsylvania is a crass attempt to exploit the law.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/03/opinion/let-mr-erdogan-fight-his-own-battles.html', 'snippet': 'A threat to request the extradition of a Turkish preacher living in Pennsylvania is a crass attempt to exploit the law.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not long ago, Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdogan of Turkey and Fethullah Gulen, a Turkish preacher in Pennsylvania, were the best of allies. Mr. Erdogan heads an Islamist government, and Mr. Gulen promotes a moderate, pro-Western brand of Sunni Islam that appeals to many well-educated and professional Turks. The two men had a common purpose in confronting and weakening the country’s once-dominant secular military and political leadership.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Let Mr. Erdogan Fight His Own Battles', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Let Mr. Erdogan Fight His Own Battles', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Erdogan, Recep Tayyip', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gulen, Fethullah', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Extradition', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'