rticle/d7fb993f-a625-5ec8-aede-aae9403bff97'}, {'abstract': '“The House That Will Not Stand” at Yale Repertory Theater in New Haven serves as something of a lesson in the custom of plaçage, in which black, American Indian or Creole women were “placed” into marriages, of sorts, with white men.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/04/nyregion/a-review-of-the-house-that-will-not-stand-in-new-haven.html', 'snippet': '“The House That Will Not Stand” at Yale Repertory Theater in New Haven serves as something of a lesson in the custom of plaçage, in which black, American Indian or Creole women were “placed” into marriages, of sorts, with white men.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Oh, look, it’s Masha, Olga and Irinia. The first time the three sisters in Marcus Gardley’s “The House That Will Not Stand” retire together to their upstairs Louisiana bedroom, those Russian sisters may come to mind. But Mr. Gardley has not written a Chekhov drama. Those who know “The House of Bernarda Alba” may think of Angustias, Martirio and Adela. And Mr. Gardley was indeed inspired by that Spanish drama, written by Federico García Lorca in 1936. But as the new work, having its world premiere at the Yale Repertory Theater in New Haven, unfolds, the young women may seem more like Marcia, Jan and Cindy.', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/04/nyregion/04ARTSCT1sub/04ARTSCT1sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 407, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/04/nyregion/04ARTSCT1sub/04ARTSCT1sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 407}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/04/nyregion/04ARTSCT1sub/04ARTSCT1sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 695, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': 