32-9763-80fad4243fc8'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/04/arts/television/whats-on-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '9 P.M. (Discovery) EVEREST AVALANCHE TRAGEDY On April 18 an NBC News team waited at the base camp on Mount Everest as it prepared to produce “Everest Jump Live,” a special about the climber Joby Ogwyn’s attempt to make the first wing-suit flight off the 29,029-foot summit of the world’s tallest mountain. That morning an avalanche struck the Khumbu Icefall, killing 13 Sherpa guides and leaving three missing — the highest death toll in a single day in the mountain’s history. This documentary covers the weeks leading up to the tragedy and the subsequent rescue and recovery efforts through real-time film footage and witness accounts. Among those interviewed are Mr. Ogwyn, above; Garrett Madison, the expedition leader; and Todd Burleson, the president of Alpine Ascents International, which lost five guides. It also pays tribute to the Sherpas and their families; the network will make a contribution to the American Himalayan Foundation’s Sherpa Family Fund, from which all donations will flow to relatives of the deceased. In “Bear Grylls: Man vs. Everest,” leading in at 8, Mr. Grylls presents stories of the greatest Everest attempts, using archival footage and interviews with climbers and support crews. In “Bear Grylls: Everest After the Avalanche,” at 10:30, he draws on his own experience — scaling the peak in 1998 at the age of 23 —to explain the allure of the mountain, and the danger.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-05-04T16:28:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'sub