'value': 'Ringling Bros and Barnum & Bailey Circus', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Falls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Providence (RI)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Circuses', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-04T21:56:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emma G. Fitzsimmons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emma', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Fitzsimmons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/817ddea8-ab2e-5dae-92a5-008aedd5389e', 'word_count': 410, 'uri': 'nyt://article/817ddea8-ab2e-5dae-92a5-008aedd5389e'}, {'abstract': '“The Dylanologists” is David Kinney’s look at archivists and others who have been obsessed with Bob Dylan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/05/books/the-dylanologists-david-kinneys-look-at-an-obsession.html', 'snippet': '“The Dylanologists” is David Kinney’s look at archivists and others who have been obsessed with Bob Dylan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bob Dylan said it best, in a 2012 Rolling Stone interview timed to the release of his “Tempest ” album: “Why is it when people talk about me, they have to go crazy?” More than 50 years after he first started attracting fanatical followers, Mr. Dylan was facing down a more brutal and dangerous species of devotee than he had seen before. The fans had social media, and the scholars had computers; en masse, they could track every move he made and every word he wrote, said or sang. “Dylanologist,” once a derisive term for the self-styled expert who sifted through the Dylan family’s garbage cans, was now a word with wide colloquial meaning, if not yet a dictionary definition.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source':