ke this: “How like a prison is my cubicle.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/06/the-american-scholar-crowdsources-a-sonnet/', 'snippet': 'The public is invited to complete a sonnet that begins like this: “How like a prison is my cubicle.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Statisticians and late-night dorm-room bull session partisans have long debated whether a monkey banging away at a typewriter for an infinite amount of time would inevitably tap out the complete works of Shakespeare. But can a swarm of people on the Internet write a plausible sonnet in 14 weeks?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The American Scholar Crowdsources a Sonnet', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crowdsourcing (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lehman, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakespeare, William', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wordsworth, William', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Scholar', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-06T16:06:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/460b3526-2bc3-54fa-8643-1fe42814c1b9', 'word_count': 219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/460b3526-2bc3-54fa-8643-1fe42814c1b9'}, {'abstract': 'The opera singer discusses her new memoir.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/06/jessye-norman-talks-about-stand-up-straight-and-sing/', 'snippet': 'The opera singer discusses her new memoir.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Stand Up Straight and Sing!,” the memoir by the soprano Jessye Norman that came out on Tuesday, takes readers from her close-knit girlhood community in Augusta, Ga., to Carnegie Hall and beyond. Ms. Norman talks about her childhood in a supportive but segregated world, singing for Rosa Parks, befriending Marian Anderson and “growing up” as an artist. Ms. Norman spoke about her book and her life during a recent interview at Carnegie Hall. Below are edited excerpts from the conversation:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/06/books/06norman/06norman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 906, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/06/books/06norman/06norman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 906}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/06/books/06norman/06norman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 679, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/06/books/06norman/06norman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1357, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/06/books/06norman/06norman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/06/books/06norman/06norman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/06/books/06norman/06norman-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 15