ommerce released 30-second television advertisements on Wednesday for 10 Republican candidates for House seats in a broadcast campaign that will cost more than $3 million to run and will last two weeks. The ads will run in the districts of six incumbents — Andy Barr of Kentucky, Dan Benishek of Michigan, Mike Coffman of Colorado, Chris Gibson of New York, Joe Heck of Nevada and David Valadao of California — and in support of the candidates Bob Dold in Illinois, Stewart Mills in Minnesota, Doug Ose in California and Richard Tisei in Massachusetts. The chamber is changing its strategy to focus on the general election. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Chamber of Commerce Begins Ad Blitz for G.O.P.', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Washington', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S. Chamber Begins Ad Blitz for G.O.P.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Chamber of Commerce', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barr, Andy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coffman, Mike', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benishek, Dan', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ose, Doug', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gibson, Christopher P', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Heck, Joseph J', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Valadao, David G', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dold, Robert James Jr', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tisei, Richard R', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-06T23:32:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/342356d8-26b6-585a-8083-16ea0f326485', 'word_count': 109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/342356d8-26b6-585a-8083-16ea0f326485'}, {'abstract': 'Long a citadel of Islamist revolt, the Red Mosque in the Pakistani capital is enjoying a revival as the government fails to offer an alternative vision of the future.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/07/world/asia/paying-homage-to-bin-laden-mosque-re-emerges-as-bastion-of-militancy.html', 'snippet': 'Long a citadel of Islamist revolt, the Red Mosque in the Pakistani capital is enjoying a revival as the government fails to offer an alternative vision of the future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Rising from the heart of Islamabad, the Red Mosque has long been a barometer of militant Islam in Pakistan. In the 1980s it funneled fighters into the anti-Soviet jihad in Afghanistan. In the ‘90s its leaders made an awe-struck pilgrimage to visit their hero, Osama bin Laden, at his farm outside Kandahar.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/07/world/REDMOSQUE/REDMOSQUE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/07/world/REDMOSQUE/REDMOSQUE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 387}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'imag