 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2e87aae-5b1f-5837-a90b-65f1450c2ef5', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2e87aae-5b1f-5837-a90b-65f1450c2ef5'}, {'abstract': 'Miami shut out the Mets for the second consecutive game, winning with a sacrifice fly in the ninth, and the Mets pitchers’ woes at bat continued: They are 0 for 58 this year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/08/sports/baseball/marlins-shut-out-mets-whose-pitchers-know-the-feeling.html', 'snippet': 'Miami shut out the Mets for the second consecutive game, winning with a sacrifice fly in the ninth, and the Mets pitchers’ woes at bat continued: They are 0 for 58 this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MIAMI — Before his team played the Marlins on Wednesday, Mets Manager Terry Collins sat in his office and discussed how his pitchers had not yet recorded a base hit this season. He did not seem particularly perturbed. If he were to start worrying about his pitchers’ getting hits — that is, if his offense were so inept that he sorely needed his pitchers to produce — then, he said, “we got a real problem.” ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/08/sports/CITY-METS/CITY-METS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 382, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/08/sports/CITY-METS/CITY-METS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 382}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/08/sports/CITY-METS/CITY-METS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 653, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 