, 'word_count': 1000, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09a99654-f721-5aae-96fd-7d3d061e8122'}, {'abstract': 'The Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center performed a revelatory Kodaly Duo, selections from Schumann and Brahms, and the American premiere of Zhou Long’s “Tales From the Nine Bells.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/08/arts/music/chamber-music-society-performs-tales-from-the-nine-bells.html', 'snippet': 'The Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center performed a revelatory Kodaly Duo, selections from Schumann and Brahms, and the American premiere of Zhou Long’s “Tales From the Nine Bells.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a rule I do not expect to leave a concert of the Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center thinking about Donald Rumsfeld. But after Tuesday evening’s brilliant “Music From Three Centuries” at Alice Tully Hall, I was put in mind of the former defense secretary’s most famous dictum about those known knowns, known unknowns and, crucially, unknown unknowns in life: “the ones we do not know we don’t know.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From Mystical Bells to a Hungarian Folk Duo, the Joys of the Unfamiliar', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'From Mystical Bells to a Hungarian Folk Duo, the Joys of the Unfamiliar', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'da Fonseca-Wollheim, Corinna', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-07T20:58:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corinna da Fonseca-Wollheim', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/81255e52-82cd-5930-990a-f0ffba8270fe', 'word_count': 407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81255e52-82cd-5930-990a-f0ffba8270fe'}, {'abstract': 'Bare, a new specialty style journal created by a bi-continental trio, takes an intimate, unretouched approach to the genre.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/07/claudia-bruno-bare-journal-fashion-magazine/', 'snippet': 'Bare, a new specialty style journal created by a bi-continental trio, takes an intimate, unretouched approach to the genre.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Looking at countless blogs and Tumblrs, the images that we were sharing were much more raw and personal than what I typically see in fashion print,” says the creative director Claudia Bruno, a co-founder of the new fashion and culture magazine Bare. Seeking to strip away the artifice of traditional fashion media, Bruno teamed up with her longtime friends, the writers Emily Lundin and Courtney Saunders, to found a fashion magazine for what Bruno calls “women like us — smarter, intellectual people who aren’t slaves to fashion, but creators of it.”', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Feeling For | A Fashion Magazine Without the Gloss', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Feeling for: Skip the Gloss', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Magazines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-07T21:00:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eviana Hartman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eviana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hartman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'r