e Festival Adds to New York’s Lively Early Music Scene', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Early Music Festival', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenleaf, Jolle', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-07T21:26:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/080188cc-64a8-54fe-b096-698a7a03d973', 'word_count': 417, 'uri': 'nyt://article/080188cc-64a8-54fe-b096-698a7a03d973'}, {'abstract': 'Luminaries from the worlds of art and fashion trekked out to Williamsburg, Brooklyn, for a seated dinner at the Domino Sugar Factory to celebrate the artist’s new work.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/07/kara-walker-creative-time-benefit-domino-factory-williamsburg/', 'snippet': 'Luminaries from the worlds of art and fashion trekked out to Williamsburg, Brooklyn, for a seated dinner at the Domino Sugar Factory to celebrate the artist’s new work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WhoKara Walker was the guest of honor at last night’s Creative Time benefit to celebrate the artist’s 75-foot-high white sphinx sculpture made of sugar in the decommissioned Domino Sugar Factory in Williamsburg, Brooklyn. More than 700 guests, most from the worlds of art and fashion, including Chuck Close, Jenna Lyons, Theaster Gates, Solange Knowles, Chloé Sévigny, André Leon Talley and Walker’s 16-year-old daughter Octavia, dined in its shadow.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Sweet Celebration of Kara Walker’s Giant Sugar Sculpture', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Close, Chuck', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walker, Kara', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-07T21:30:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By T Magazine', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/94a18028-c0de-5277-bf52-304f7546b4c3', 'word_count': 310, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94a18028-c0de-5277-bf52-304f7546b4c3'}, {'abstract': 'Bank officials said they were conducting a “third-party review” of how the problem happened, but declined to name the firm conducting the review.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/05/07/bank-of-america-shareholders-question-officials-after-4-billion-error/', 'snippet': 'Bank officials said they were conducting a “third-party review” of how the problem happened, but declined to name the firm conducting the review.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHARLOTTE, N.C. — This year’s annual meeting was supposed to be a victory lap for Bank of America, with many of its mortgage problems receding and its deposits and credit card business growing. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/08/business/Bank/Bank-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/08/business/Bank/Bank-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 640}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/0