as arrested on charges of possessing ammunition and knives as he tried to board a domestic flight.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/08/world/asia/pakistan-fbi-agent-questioned.html', 'snippet': 'The police questioned a Federal Bureau of Investigation agent under the country’s antiterrorism laws on Wednesday, two days after he was arrested on charges of possessing ammunition and knives as he tried to board a domestic flight.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The police questioned a Federal Bureau of Investigation agent under the country’s antiterrorism laws on Wednesday, two days after he was arrested on charges of possessing ammunition and knives as he tried to board a domestic flight. Several news outlets, citing American law enforcement officials, identified the man, who arrived in Pakistan on May 1, as Joel Cox. The Associated Press said that Jen Psaki, a State Department spokeswoman in Washington, confirmed that Mr. Cox was an F.B.I. employee on temporary assignment to “provide routine assistance to the legal attaché at the U.S. Mission” in Pakistan. The local news media broadcast images of a handcuffed man wearing a blue and white striped shirt as he was taken to court on Tuesday. The images were reminiscent of the arrest of Raymond A. Davis, a covert security officer for the Central Intelligence Agency, in Lahore in January 2011 after he shot two Pakistani men at a busy intersection. The killings and arrest of Mr. Davis caused a diplomatic furor, with the United States and Pakistan wrangling over American assertions that he had diplomatic immunity. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pakistan: F.B.I. Agent Is Questioned', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pakistan: F.B.I. Agent Is Questioned', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cox, Joel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-07T23:47:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Salman Masood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Salman', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Masood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a23881b-7aa9-5393-9db8-8b2a6695293e', 'word_count': 182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a23881b-7aa9-5393-9db8-8b2a6695293e'}, {'abstract': 'After her mother passes away, the filmmaker Judith Helfand struggles to pack up her things — figuring out what to keep and how to let go.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/opinion/100000002868437/love-and-stuff.html', 'snippet': 'After her mother passes away, the filmmaker Judith Helfand struggles to pack up her things — figuring out what to keep and how to let go.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After her mother passes away, the filmmaker Judith Helfand struggles to pack up her things — figuring out what to keep and how to let go.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/07/multimedia/opdoc-mother/opdoc-mother-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/07/multimedia/opdoc-mother/opdoc-mother-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/07/multimedia/opdoc-mother/opdoc-mother-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'le