_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric A. Taub', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Taub', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71c081a0-77ce-5116-8c52-99f216b3858b', 'word_count': 710, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71c081a0-77ce-5116-8c52-99f216b3858b'}, {'abstract': 'Considering that David Barron is being vetted for a lifetime post, senators have a right to see his memos that justified the use of drones to kill an American.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/08/opinion/the-lawyer-behind-the-drone-policy.html', 'snippet': 'Considering that David Barron is being vetted for a lifetime post, senators have a right to see his memos that justified the use of drones to kill an American.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the White House nominated David Barron to be a judge on the United States Court of Appeals for the First Circuit, based in Boston, it expected the usual Republican opposition. Mr. Barron, a Harvard law professor, is known as a liberal who was pointedly critical of President George W. Bush’s national security policy. What it didn’t expect — but should have — was that Democrats would have some problems with the nominee, too.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Lawyer Behind the Drone Policy', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Lawyer Behind the Drone Policy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barron, David J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Targeted Killings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drones (Pilotless Planes)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 4,