name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/154c309b-2137-5aaf-a11f-7d80ec218ff0', 'word_count': 1846, 'uri': 'nyt://article/154c309b-2137-5aaf-a11f-7d80ec218ff0'}, {'abstract': 'While holding its benchmark interest rate at its already record low, the bank indicated it might act next month to spur the euro zone’s economy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/09/business/international/european-central-bank-leaves-benchmark-rate-unchanged.html', 'snippet': 'While holding its benchmark interest rate at its already record low, the bank indicated it might act next month to spur the euro zone’s economy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRANKFURT — The European Central Bank on Thursday held interest rates steady, but signaled that it was likely to take action next month to stimulate the euro zone economy, amid growing concern about economic fallout from the crisis in Ukraine.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'European Central Bank Says Stimulus Is Likely, but Not Before June', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'European Central Bank Says Stimulus Is Likely, but Not Before June', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Central Bank', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interest Rates', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-08T11:53:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'International Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Ewing and James Kanter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ewing', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kanter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bad2d824-5867-5584-80d6-ac3e7162744c', 'word_count': 1130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bad2d824-5867-5584-80d6-ac3e7162744c'}, {'abstract': 'In “Neighbors,” a young married couple start to feel old living next to a house full of hard-partying fraternity brothers, and turf war breaks out.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/09/movies/adulthood-vs-arrested-development-in-neighbors.html', 'snippet': 'In “Neighbors,” a young married couple start to feel old living next to a house full of hard-partying fraternity brothers, and turf war breaks out.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At an especially stressful moment in their domestic life — they are in the midst of an escalating battle with the fraternity next door, about which more shortly — Mac and Kelly, a married couple with a cute (and notably quiet) baby daughter, have a fight. At issue is who has the right to be irresponsible. Although they have a kid, a mortgage and other trappings of grown-up life, neither is entirely willing to give up the wild, impulsive behavior associated with youth. Mac (Seth Rogen) thinks his gender (and perhaps also his body type) entitles him to be the self-indulgent one. “We can’t both be Kevin James,” he says. And of course, he points out, Kelly (Rose Byrne) is a wife and mother, a bit of biological typecasting she furiously resents, and one that “Neighbors” rejects. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Just Try Borrowing a Cup of Sugar', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Just Try Borrowing a Cup of Sugar', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stoller, Nicholas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Efron, Zac', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Byrne, Rose', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rogen, Seth', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Neighbors (Movie)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-08T12:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0de4b6ed-4492-59d9-ab82-dd9ff834ba4c', 'word_count': 952, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0de4b6ed-4492-59d9-ab82-dd9ff834ba4c'}, {'abstract': '“Violet,” “The Cripple of Inishmaan” and “Hedwig and the Angry Inch” are just a few of the shows that took the long road to Broadway to increase their chances of success.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/11/theater/theaterspecial/moving-your-show-to-broadway-not-so-fast.html', 'snippet': '“Violet,” “The Cripple of Inishmaan” and “Hedwig and the Angry Inch” are just a few of the shows that took the long road to Broadway to increase their chances of success.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s the most critically acclaimed musical of the New York theater season. It has endearing characters — a hungry protagonist and a colorful family — as well as clever storytelling and tuneful songs. Its creators spent years developing the show, dreaming of Broadway, but tempering their expectations, only too well aware they didn’t have the big-name stars or tourist-friendly material that can keep a multimillion-dollar musical in business. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'sub