', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f45f773-d768-5772-b226-27ffc91bf77e', 'word_count': 875, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f45f773-d768-5772-b226-27ffc91bf77e'}, {'abstract': 'Times classical music critics share what they’ve been listening to lately.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/08/classical-playlist-pavarotti-berio-shostakovich-and-more/', 'snippet': 'Times classical music critics share what they’ve been listening to lately.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BELLINI: ‘I Puritani’Joan Sutherland, Luciano Pavarotti, Piero Cappuccilli, Nicolai Ghiaurov; Richard Bonynge, conductor; London Symphony Orchestra, Chorus of the Royal Opera House, Covent Garden(Decca; three CDs)There has been almost a mini-festival of bel canto opera taking place at the Metropolitan Opera of late, with the return of Rossini’s “La Cenerentola” and revivals of two Bellini works: “La Sonnambula” and “I Puritani.” In anticipation of “I Puritani,” I took out the 1973 recording featuring Joan Sutherland and Luciano Pavarotti, with Richard Bonynge conducting the London Symphony Orchestra. I can hardly stop listening to it. No matter one’s taste in the art of bel canto, no matter one’s passion for Maria Callas (which we all share), Joan Sutherland and Luciano Pavarotti are thrilling exemplars of the tradition. This recording captures them in their glory. (Anthony Tommasini)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/09/arts/classical-pavarotti/classical-pavarotti-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 416, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/09/arts/classical-pavarotti/classical-pavarotti-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 416}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'cred