', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/55334fbf-367e-5fd2-9a45-b0c009eda152', 'word_count': 705, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55334fbf-367e-5fd2-9a45-b0c009eda152'}, {'abstract': 'Ken-David Masur has been appointed assistant conductor of the Boston Symphony Orchestra.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/08/kurt-masurs-son-named-assistant-conductor-in-boston/', 'snippet': 'Ken-David Masur has been appointed assistant conductor of the Boston Symphony Orchestra.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Besides getting a new music director when Andris Nelsons takes up his duties at the start of the 2014-15 season, the Boston Symphony Orchestra will have a new assistant conductor as well. The orchestra announced on Thursday that it had appointed Ken-David Masur to the position. In that capacity Mr. Masur will make his subscription debut during the 2015-16 season, the second year of the two-year position, but he has already led the orchestra in a non-subscription program, at Tanglewood in 2012. On that occasion, he split the conducting tasks with his father, Kurt Masur, the former music director of the New York Philharmonic.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kurt Masur’s Son Named Assistant Conductor in Boston', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Masur, Ken-David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Masur, Kurt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boston Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boston (Mass)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-08T19:56:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/208bea66-7631-5fe0-bf00-c98da04e4d33', 'word_count': 242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/208bea66-7631-5fe0-bf00-c98da04e4d33'}, {'abstract': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/08/the-gun-report-may-8-2014/', 'snippet': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The attack on prospective smart-gun purveyors continues.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/08/opinion/hunter/hunter-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 490, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/08/opinion/hunter/hunter-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 490}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/08/opinion/hunter/hunter-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 564, 'width': 690, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/08/opinion/hunter/hunter-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 564, 'width': 690, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/08/opinion/hunter/hunter-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumb