rts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alessandra Stanley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alessandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stanley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2dff2a2d-c6d6-591e-b46a-0c395999669d', 'word_count': 891, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2dff2a2d-c6d6-591e-b46a-0c395999669d'}, {'abstract': 'The new “Rosemary’s Baby,” directed by Agnieszka Holland, aims to be psychologically complex, focusing on the loss of control of pregnancy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/11/arts/television/agnieszka-hollands-post-feminist-rosemarys-baby-on-nbc.html', 'snippet': 'The new “Rosemary’s Baby,” directed by Agnieszka Holland, aims to be psychologically complex, focusing on the loss of control of pregnancy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — “Rosemary’s Baby,” Roman Polanski’s 1968 horror film about a woman impregnated by the devil, may seem somewhat campy in retrospect, but it has influenced scores of filmmakers and spawned innumerable imitators. Now, it has given birth to a remake — set in contemporary Paris, made for television and directed by the esteemed Polish-born director Agnieszka Holland. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/11/arts/11ROSEMARY1_SPAN/11ROSEMARY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/11/arts/11ROSEMARY1_SPAN/11ROSEMARY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/11/arts/11ROSEMARY1_SPAN/11ROSEMARY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 