adline': {'main': 'Today in Small Business: Birth of a Food Fad', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Entrepreneurship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-09T15:07:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gene Marks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4953ee6a-1760-50bc-bed5-55586692c274', 'word_count': 477, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4953ee6a-1760-50bc-bed5-55586692c274'}, {'abstract': 'In Philip Kerr’s “Prayer,” an F.B.I. agent investigates the curious deaths of some prominent atheists.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/11/books/review/philip-kerrs-prayer-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In Philip Kerr’s “Prayer,” an F.B.I. agent investigates the curious deaths of some prominent atheists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bernie Gunther, the sardonic German detective in Philip Kerr’s historical crime novels, survived World War II with life, limb and integrity intact. But Bernie was only up against the entire Nazi high command. In PRAYER (Putnam, $26.95), Kerr introduces us to Gil Martins, an F.B.I. agent with the domestic terrorism unit in Houston who seems to be doing battle with Almighty God himself — or whoever is murdering prominent atheists in his name. As an ex-Catholic and wavering evangelical Christian, Martins is an avid consumer of what he calls “atheist porn” by writers like Richard Dawkins. And when his friend, the worldly Bishop Eamon Coogan, asks him to look into the curious deaths of some prominent atheists, he’s drawn into a mystery that defies both faith and logic.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/11/books/11crime-sub/11crime-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 740, 'width': 599, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/11/books/11crime-sub/11crime-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 740, 'width': 599, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/11/books/11crime-sub/11crime-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/11/books/11crime-sub/11crime-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/11/books/11crime-sub/11crime-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Guilty as Sin', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Guilty as Sin', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerr, Philip', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eriksson, Kjell', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brookmyre, Christopher', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Watson, Jan Elizabeth', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-09T15:14:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1c40c02-a129-5e93-9489-6123073fa17b', 'word_count': 852, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1c40c02-a129-5e93-9489-6123073fa17b'}, {'abstract': 'What doctors are learning from the experience of two men in Germany who appear to have been cured of AIDS.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/11/books/review/cured-by-nathalia-holt.html', 'snippet': 'What doctors are learning from the experience of two men in Germany who appear to have been cured of AIDS.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the deadliest medical conditions, “cure” is a word even the most optimistic doctors try to resist. Oncologists have seen too often how a few metastatic cancer cells can endure every poison medicine throws at them, chemical or radioactive, only to lurk silently for years before staging a triumphant return. No wonder pharmaceutical companies prefer to \xaddescribe their state-of-the-art cancer drugs in terms of five-year survival rates, with no promises about what happens in Year 6.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/11/books/review/11SUBJOHNSON/11SUBJOHNSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 652, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/11/books/review/11SUBJOHNSON/11SUBJOHNSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 652}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/11/books/review/11SUBJOHNSON/11SUBJOHNSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 942, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit':