, 'headline': {'main': 'Michael Jackson Returns', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-05-09T16:11:45+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/4234fa97-2d3c-58b4-8f55-925a8ce60b62', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/4234fa97-2d3c-58b4-8f55-925a8ce60b62'}, {'abstract': 'A new policy of charging by the size of packages, not merely weight, is likely to hurt Amazon a bit, but its small rivals more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/10/upshot/fedexs-price-rise-is-a-blessing-in-disguise-for-amazon.html', 'snippet': 'A new policy of charging by the size of packages, not merely weight, is likely to hurt Amazon a bit, but its small rivals more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No. 1 on the list of chores the Internet has done away with: dragging yourself to the store to stock up on unwieldy items and carting them home. Many e-commerce companies — led by Amazon.com and the sites it owns, like Diapers.com and Soap.com — have made it easy to order even items like toilet paper and diapers without paying a cent for shipping.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/10/upshot/10UP-Fedex-copy/10UP-Fedex-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/10/upshot/10UP-Fedex-copy/10UP-Fedex-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/10/upshot/10UP-Fedex-copy/10UP-Fedex-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/10/upshot/10UP-Fedex-copy/10UP-Fedex-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1361, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/10/upshot/10UP-Fedex-copy/10UP-Fedex-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/10/upshot/10UP-Fedex-copy/10UP-Fedex-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/10/upshot/10UP-Fedex-copy/10UP-Fedex-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'FedEx’s Price Rise Is a Blessing in Disguise for Amazon', 'kicker': 'Comparison Shopping', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Delivery Services', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Commerce', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fedex Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Parcel Service Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-09T16:12:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claire Cain Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claire', 'middlename': 'Cain', 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1c5a824-1e22-5566-9994-1b1909c10b82', 'word_count': 692, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1c5a824-1e22-5566-9994-1b1909c10b82'}, {'abstract': 'Lawmakers actually asked good questions at a hearing on Comcast’s acquisition of Time Warner Cable.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/09/lawmakers-asked-good-questions-for-a-change/', 'snippet': 'Lawmakers actually asked good questions at a hearing on Comcast’s acquisition of Time Warner Cable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lawmakers on the hill, usually eager to score points and yell about imaginary scandals, have been surprisingly thoughtful about Comcast’s $45 billion acquisition of Time Warner Cable, a pressing antitrust issue.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Proposed Merger Brings Out the Thoughtful Side of Congress', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Comcast Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Communications Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Time Warner Cable Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-09T16:13:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vikas Bajaj', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vikas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bajaj', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/919a0ad7-4a15-5718-b2ae-9f3c9c03c220', 'word_count': 371, 'uri': 'nyt://article/919a0ad7-4a15-5718-b2ae-9f3c9c03c220'}, {'abstract': 'Descendants tend to succeed founders as president of some of the more prominent Christian institutions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/10/us/at-evangelical-christian-colleges-leadership-is-often-the-family-business.html', 'snippet': 'Descendants tend to succeed founders as president of some of the more prominent Christian institutions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the past school year, several leading American universities, including Yale, Princeton, Dartmouth and Carnegie-Mellon, welcomed new presidents. These men were leading scholars, and they were experienced administrators; in some cases, they held degrees from the universities they now lead. And none of them — not one — inherited the job from his father or mother.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/10/us/10BELIEFS1/10BELIEFS1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 425, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/10/us/10BELIEFS1/10BELIEFS1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 425}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/10/us/10BELIEFS1/10BELIEFS1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 726, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/10/us/10BELIEFS1/10BELIEFS1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1451, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/10/us/10BELIEFS1/10BELIEFS1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/10/us/10BELIEFS1/10BELIEFS1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/10/us/10BELIEFS1/10BELIEFS1-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At Evangelical Colleges, Leadership Is Often the Family Business', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Leadership Is Often the Family Business at Evangelical Christian Colleges', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Liberty University', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bob Jones University', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oral Roberts University', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-09T16:14:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Oppenheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Oppenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/55b1fbdf-1b15-5a36-9879-0d5c92835a70', 'word_count': 1035, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55b1fbdf-1b15-5a36-9879-0d5c92835a70'}, {'abstract': 'The process of recovering from the death of a sibling is explored in two young adult novels.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/11/books/review/love-letters-to-the-dead-and-the-summer-of-letting-go.html', 'snippet': 'The process of recovering from the death of a sibling is explored in two young adult novels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When a friend in high school told me “The Catcher in the Rye” was the funniest book he’d ever read, I picked up a copy — and sobbed my way through it. To my friend, Holden Caulfield was a hilarious smart aleck; to me, he was a bereft sibling, falling apart after his younger brother’s death.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Grief Lessons', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Grief Lessons', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Polisner, Gae', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dellaira, Ava', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-09T16:16:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth DeVita-Raeburn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'DeVita-Raeburn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c004465a-b586-5826-a35b-0c871aff992e', 'word_count': 684, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c004465a-b586-5826-a35b-0c871aff992e'}, {'abstract': 'In two middle-grade verse novels, Emily Dickinson serves as a source of inspiration and adventure.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/11/books/review/another-day-as-emily-and-miss-emily.html', 'snippet': 'In two middle-grade verse novels, Emily Dickinson serves as a source of inspiration and adventure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the daily lives of most American children today, poetry has gone missing. Almost entirely absent from elementary school curriculums, rarely chosen as bedtime reading by parents, poetry — formerly a joyful accouterment of youth, an inexhaustible gift — seems forgotten. Yet poetry and children belong together. And who, among the great American poets, could be more appropriate for childhood than Emily Dickinson?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'That Amherst Belle', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tha