01944a', 'word_count': 1361, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20c19f66-f054-5da1-afcb-9b9e0801944a'}, {'abstract': 'The stars of many farmers’ markets in spring – artichokes, spring onions and green garlic – turn out to be “prebiotic stars” as well, supporting beneficial microbes in our gut. Here are five recipes that let you put them to work for you.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/09/dishes-for-digestive-health/', 'snippet': 'The stars of many farmers’ markets in spring – artichokes, spring onions and green garlic – turn out to be “prebiotic stars” as well, supporting beneficial microbes in our gut. Here are five recipes that let you put them to work for you.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve long been aware of the term “probiotics” (is there anybody who watches television who hasn’t seen Jamie Lee Curtis touting them in those old ads for Activia yogurt?), the beneficial microbes in our guts, and also in fermented foods like miso and kimchi, that some experts believe play a significant role in gut health and in keeping our immune system robust. But I’d never heard the word “prebiotics” until I attended a talk by the Stanford nutritionist Jo Ann Hattner, who has written (with Susan Anderes) an interesting guide called “Gut Insight: Probiotics and Prebiotics for Digestive Health and Well-Being.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/03/science/recipehealthpromo/recipehealthpromo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/03/science/recipehealthpromo/recipehealthpromo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/03/science/recipehealthpromo/recipehealthpromo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo',