ct': 'At Frieze New York this weekend, several works make art out of disembodied gams.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/09/legs-frieze-new-york-guillaume-leblon-adam-mcewen-nicolai-wallner/', 'snippet': 'At Frieze New York this weekend, several works make art out of disembodied gams.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An art fair is first and foremost a playground for the senses, but for any visitor pacing Frieze New York’s 1500-foot-long SO-IL-designed tent erected on Randall’s Island today — or any gallerist standing by to explain their inscrutable wares — these long days are as much about lower-body stamina as they are about the eyes or mind. A trio of works found around the fair render legs — the pivots on which all this weekend’s deals depend — as art. At Galerie Jocelyn Wolff, Guillaume Leblon’s pair of ceramic appendages truncated at the knee are poised for action, existentially tensed upon a gnarled, painted clay blanket. Adam McEwen’s anatomical gypsum sculptures peeking out of the wall at the Modern Institute\xa0look more like hunting trophies; the feet are tucked into bright plastic buckets. And the showstopping, kinetic piece by Jonathan Monk at Galleri Nicolai Wallner\xa0takes a page from the Rockettes with candy-colored, mechanized gams that kick for the pleasure of those who choose to cool their heels there.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/09/t-magazine/09trend-mcgarry/09trend-mcgarry-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/09/t-magazine/09trend-mcgarry/09trend-mcgarry-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/09/t-magazine/09trend-mcgarry/09trend-mcgarry-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subTyp