rlborough Chelsea uses a Chevy Malibu damaged by a falling asteroid chunk as the centerpiece for a meditation on making the familiar strange.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/09/mark-hagen-guest-star-asteroid-chevy-malibu-marlborough-chelsea-gallery/', 'snippet': 'The Los Angeles artist Mark Hagen’s new solo show at Marlborough Chelsea uses a Chevy Malibu damaged by a falling asteroid chunk as the centerpiece for a meditation on making the familiar strange.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1992 in Peekskill, N.Y., a 4.4-billion-year-old, 26-pound asteroid chunk fell on a 1980 Chevy Malibu. The car soon became famous in meteorite-collecting circles (yes, they exist) and has since traveled all around the world. It will go on view tomorrow for the first time in a fine-art context, at Mark Hagen’s self-curated show at Marlborough Chelsea, “Guest Star,” a title that slyly evokes both the rock that did the damage and to Hagen’s own status in New York (the Los Angeles-based artist is unrepresented in the city). “I love the fact that it’s a man-made object that’s been sculpted by alien hands,” Hagen says of the car. “I believe in alien life.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/09/t-magazine/09hagen-baumgardner/09hagen-baumgardner-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 240, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/09/t-magazine/09hagen-baumgardner/09hagen-baumgardner-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 240}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/09/t-magazine/09hagen-baumgardner/09hagen-baumgardner-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 410, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/09/