aus', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-09T23:06:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Schwendener', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwendener', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0ed4923-008a-5a06-ace7-134a920b9f52', 'word_count': 1051, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0ed4923-008a-5a06-ace7-134a920b9f52'}, {'abstract': 'A 7.35-carat diamond that was in the possession of a New Jersey family for nine years had actually been stolen from a jeweler in 2003 by a stylist who pawned it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/10/nyregion/hot-ice-the-tortuous-tale-of-the-pear-shaped-diamond.html', 'snippet': 'A 7.35-carat diamond that was in the possession of a New Jersey family for nine years had actually been stolen from a jeweler in 2003 by a stylist who pawned it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The thought struck Steven Zaretsky during a party two years ago, when he looked over at his wife and the 7.35-carat, pear-shaped diamond hanging on a thin chain around her neck. It had been a gift from her father to her mother, and later, to her. What was it insured for? When was the last time it had been certified and appraised? ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/10/nyregion/10CRIMESCENE/10CRIMESCENE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 530, 'width': 376, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/10/nyregion/10CRIMESCENE/10CRIMESCENE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 530, 'width': 376, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype'