n residents. Some residents think the economy and a history of corruption are still hindrances.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PROVIDENCE, R.I. — A lot of people here say that Rhode Island suffers from being the smallest state in the country. Jimmy Saccoccio, manager of the Olneyville New York System, a popular diner, agrees.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/11/us/RHODE1/RHODE1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/11/us/RHODE1/RHODE1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/11/us/RHODE1/RHODE1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/11/us/RHODE1/RHODE1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/11/us/RHODE1/RHODE1-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/11/us/RHODE1/RHODE1-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/11/us/RHODE1/RHODE1-thumbLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rhode Island, a State Sized Just Right to Contain Its Pride', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A State Sized Just Right to Contain Its Pride', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rhode Island', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gallup Poll', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-10T18:36:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katharine Q. Seelye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katharine', 'middlename': 'Q.', 'lastname': 'Seelye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ddf02075-ad15-52d7-86ff-b8ddf8e07e9b', 'word_count': 1270, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ddf02075-ad15-52d7-86ff-b8ddf8e07e9b'}, {'abstract': 'Overfishing, disease and pollution are taking a toll.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/11/opinion/sunday/saving-striped-bass.html', 'snippet': 'Overfishing, disease and pollution are taking a toll.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONTAUK, N.Y. — AS summer approaches, the view from Long Island’s easternmost point transforms at dawn as anglers casting for striped bass begin taking their places along the surf. By June, the fish will be racing through these waters on the way to coastal New England as part of their northern migration, a biological spectacle akin to the movement of salmon on the West Coast.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Saving Striped Bass', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Saving Striped Bass', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bass (Fish)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fishing, Commercial', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New England States (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fishing, Sport', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Atlantic States Marine Fisheries Commission', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-10T18:41:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Crocker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crocker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c93374a-c0a7-5593-8cf2-64fac7811371', 'word_count': 821, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c93374a-c0a7-5593-8cf2-64fac7811371'}, {'abstract': 'Screening the goalie in the playoffs is a tough job that can be a key to success, but it results in a lot of abuse for forwards.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/11/sports/hockey/battles-for-the-crease-exact-a-toll-in-the-playoffs.html', 'snippet': 'Screening the goalie in the playoffs is a tough job that can be a key to success, but it results in a lot of abuse for forwards.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Boston Bruins forward Jarome Iginla knows the playoffs can be a painful experience. In every game, he is poked, slashed, crosschecked, shoved, punched, tripped, sworn at repeatedly and thrown to the ice.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/11/sports/DOG-HOCKEY/DOG-HOCKEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 822, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/11/sports/DOG-HOCKEY/DOG-HOCKEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 822}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': No