type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3426c493-5f11-56fa-ab9e-f49f03b279a4', 'word_count': 770, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3426c493-5f11-56fa-ab9e-f49f03b279a4'}, {'abstract': 'The 2012 attack on an American diplomatic compound in Benghazi, Libya, continues to dominate the conversation after House Republicans formed a committee to investigate the attacks this week.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/10/sunday-breakfast-menu-may-11/', 'snippet': 'The 2012 attack on an American diplomatic compound in Benghazi, Libya, continues to dominate the conversation after House Republicans formed a committee to investigate the attacks this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Speaker John A. Boehner appointed seven Republicans to a 12-person committee charged with investigating the 2012 attack in Benghazi, Libya, that killed four Americans. Representative Trey Gowdy, Republican of South Carolina, was tapped to lead the investigation and will appear on “Fox News Sunday,” along with Representative Xavier Becerra, Democrat of California, to debate the new committee and whether Democrats should participate.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sunday Breakfast Menu, May 11', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-05-10T18:54:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elena Schneider', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schneider', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0650a56-44c0-5331-b1a7-19b5ceb0585c', 'word_count': 404, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0650a56-44c0-5331-b1a7-19b5ceb0585c'}, {'abstract': 'An ex-gang member gave the eighth grader I mentor some valuable help.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/11/opinion/sunday/advice-for-a-troubled-teen.html', 'snippet': 'An ex-gang member gave the eighth grader I mentor some valuable help.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE eighth-grade boy I mentor had been suspended for calling his substitute teacher a bitch, loudly enough that a teacher’s aide heard. Until this incident, he had been making real progress with his behavior problems in school. He had vastly improved since sixth grade, when suspensions were a regular part of his life. He disrupted class, taunted boys and hit girls. In seventh grade, his behavior, if not his schoolwork, had slowly improved.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/11/sunday-review/11PRISONS/11PRISONS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 534, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/11/sunday-review/11PRISONS/11PRISONS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 534}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/11/sunday-review/11PRISONS/11PRISONS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 911, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/11/sunday-review/11PRISONS/11PRISONS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1821, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/11/sunday-review/11PRISONS/11PRISONS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/11/sunday-review/11PRISONS/11PRISONS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/11/sunday-review/11PRISONS/11PRISONS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Advice for a Troubled Teen', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Advice for a Troubled Teen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mentors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teenagers and Adolescence', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tutors and Tutoring', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-10T19:03:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Berler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a9d4435-44d9-58cc-b5d1-6a763b552336', 'word_count': 1169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a9d4435-44d9-58cc-b5d1-6a763b552336'}, {'abstract': 'In the Internet age, how do we discover the new?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/11/opinion/sunday/when-music-was-strange.html', 'snippet': 'In the Internet age, how do we discover the new?', 'lead_paragraph': 'BELGRADE LAKES, Me. — IN 1975 it was my friend Daryl — one of the very few African-American students in my mostly white prep school — who was the champion of the new. “Boylan,” he said one day after school. “You have to check this out.” Then he put Miles Davis’s “Bitches Brew” on the turntable. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/08/04/opinion/BOYLAN_NEW/BOYLAN_NEW-articleLarge-v6.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/08/04/opinion/BOYLAN_NEW/BOYLAN_NEW-articleLarge-v6.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/08/04/opinion/BOYLAN_NEW/BOYLAN_NEW-jumbo-v7.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/08/04/opinion/BOYLAN_NEW/BOYLAN_NEW-superJumbo-v6.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/08/04/opinion/BOYLAN_NEW/BOYLAN_NEW-thumbStandard-v7.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/08/04/opinion/BOYLAN_NEW/BOYLAN_NEW-thumbStandard-v7.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/08/04/opinion/BOYLAN_NEW/BOYLAN_NEW-thumbLarge-v6.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Music Was Strange', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Music Was Strange', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boylan, Jennifer Finney', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radio', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Audio Recordings, Downloads and Streaming', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-10T19:08:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Finney Boylan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'Finney', 'lastname': 'Boylan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/9598aefd-b3af-592b-ba0f-17e0ea636d82', 'word_count': 939, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9598aefd-b3af-592b-ba0f-17e0ea636d82'}, {'abstract': 'Readers comment on my column about the bang for the buck from educating girls worldwide.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/10/why-the-kidnapped-schoolgirls-matter/', 'snippet': 'Readers comment on my column about the bang for the buck from educating girls worldwide.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My Sunday column looks at the kidnapping of the NIgerian schoolgirls by Boko Haram, and the similar attacks on girls’ education by the Taliban and other militants, and asks why extremists are so afraid of a girl with a book. The basic answer is that nothing can be more transformative for a society than educating girls and then moving those educated women into the formal labor force. And just as Boko Haram and the Taliban are right to fear girls’ education, those of us appalled by extremism should be investing in girls’ education. That’s the way to stick it to Boko Haram.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why the Kidnapped Schoolgirls Matter', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-05-10T19:17:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d0069f5-c651-55b6-a4f9-b49791f022ab', 'word_count': 201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d0069f5-c651-55b6-a4f9-b49791f022ab'}, {'abstract': 'The pharmaceutical industry must be encouraged to develop new drugs to supplement those that are losing their effectiveness.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/11/opinion/sunday/the-rise-of-antibiotic-resistance.html', 'snippet': 'The pharmaceutical industry must be encouraged to develop new drugs to supplement those that are losing their effectiveness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The World Health Organization has surveyed the growth of antibiotic-resistant germs around the world — the first such survey it has ever conducted — and come up with disturbing findings. In a report issued late last month, the organization found that antimicrobial resistance in bacteria (the main focus of the report), fungi, viruses and parasites is an increasingly serious threat in every part of the world. “A problem so serious that it threatens the achievements of modern medicine,” the organization said. “A post-antibiotic era, in which common infections and minor injuries can kill, far from being an apocalyptic fantasy, is instead a very real possibility for the 21st century.” ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Rise of Antibiotic Resistance', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Rise of Antibiotic Resistance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, '