s the same sorry outcome for hundreds of complaints against agents who abused migrants: inaction or a lack of a resolution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If a Border Patrol agent beats, kicks, threatens or otherwise abuses you, you can file a complaint. What you can’t count on, evidently, is anything being done about it.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Impunity and the Border Patrol', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Impunity and the Border Patrol', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Customs and Border Protection (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-12T01:05:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c32b833-78da-54f0-9520-04d8d0269deb', 'word_count': 364, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c32b833-78da-54f0-9520-04d8d0269deb'}, {'abstract': 'Russia’s national obsession with fascism is playing down the darker aspects of Soviet history and obliterating objective debate over current foreign policy issues, critics say.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/12/world/europe/russia-revisits-its-history-to-nail-down-its-future.html', 'snippet': 'Russia’s national obsession with fascism is playing down the darker aspects of Soviet history and obliterating objective debate over current foreign policy issues, critics say.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — As many Russians spent a holiday weekend reveling in the annual display of military might that marks their victory over Nazi Germany, the tension in Ukraine has fueled a passionate debate over how to exalt the country’s history without distorting it.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/12/world/RUSSIA/RUSSIA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/12/world/RUSSIA/RUSSIA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/12/world/RUSSIA/RUSSIA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/12/world/RUSSIA/RUSSIA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/12/world/RUSSIA/RUSSIA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/12/world/RUSSIA/RUSSIA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/12/world/RUSSIA/RUSSIA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Russia Revisits Its History to Nail Down Its Future', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Russia Revisits Its History to Nail Down Its Future', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Propaganda', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Putin, Vladimir V', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ukraine', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-12T01:07:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil MacFarquhar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacFarquhar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d08b1a6a-4429-5a23-a8ab-9d8892e7a27d', 'word_count': 1168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d08b1a6a-4429-5a23-a8ab-9d8892e7a27d'}, {'abstract': 'The New York City Council missed an opportunity by seeking to study “member items” rather than end these stipends that pad members’ salaries.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/12/opinion/the-city-council-playing-by-better-rules.html', 'snippet': 'The New York City Council missed an opportunity by seeking to study “member items” rather than end these stipends that pad members’ salaries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A sardonic tip of the cap to Councilman Ruben Wills for getting arrested on Wednesday, the same day the New York City Council convened its first hearing on a package of rule changes meant to increase transparency and accountability. Nothing like a well-timed scandal — alleged theft of public funds — to help drive home a point. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The City Council, Playing by Better Rules', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The City Council, Playing by Better Rules', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'City Council (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption (Institutional)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wills, Ruben W', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-12T01:07:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/521fa5a9-abb2-5d6d-b217-8857149c89b4', 'word_count': 484, 'uri': 'nyt://article/521fa5a9-abb2-5d6d-b217-8857149c89b4'}, {'abstract': 'A White House report on big data has some good ideas, but it falls short of the “clear rules of the road” that were envisioned.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/12/opinion/a-long-way-to-privacy-safeguards.html', 'snippet': 'A White House report on big data has some good ideas, but it falls short of the “clear rules of the road” that were envisioned.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new White House report on “big data” and privacy includes several recommendations that could help protect the personal information of individuals from warrantless searches and abusive marketing practices. But it still does not do enough to advance the goal President Obama set two years ago for “clear rules of the road” on privacy. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Long Way to Privacy Safeguards', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Long Way to Privacy Safeguards', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data-Mining and Database Marketing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronic Communications Privacy Act', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Search and Seizure', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Podesta, John D', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-12T01:09:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/22fe131b-b84e-5ed1-96e8-f4bc8cdb12a0', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22fe131b-b84e-5ed1-96e8-f4bc8cdb12a0'}, {'abstract': 'Despite a host of programs, the New York Police Department struggles to effectively respond to non-English speakers who are in need of translators.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/12/nyregion/language-barrier-continues-to-thwart-victims-of-crimes.html', 'snippet': 'Despite a host of programs, the New York Police Department struggles to effectively respond to non-English speakers who are in need of translators.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York City now has more non-English speakers than ever, according to the Census Bureau: nearly two million. In response to this growing population, the city has assembled a host of programs to help it serve not just those who speak Spanish, Chinese and Russian, but also languages like Pashto, Punjabi, Uzbek and Urdu.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/12/nyregion/12L