', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marjorie Connelly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marjorie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Connelly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4fb1050-ad9e-5cc6-b18d-98adafe41c56', 'word_count': 139, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4fb1050-ad9e-5cc6-b18d-98adafe41c56'}, {'abstract': 'The Haxan Cloak and Robert Henke shared an electronic concert on Saturday as part of the Red Bull Music Academy Festival at Brooklyn Masonic Temple.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/13/arts/music/the-haxan-cloak-and-robert-henke-in-red-bull-festival.html', 'snippet': 'The Haxan Cloak and Robert Henke shared an electronic concert on Saturday as part of the Red Bull Music Academy Festival at Brooklyn Masonic Temple.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The electronic music double bill on Saturday night at the Brooklyn Masonic Temple was a superb clash of aesthetic extremes. The Red Bull Music Academy Festival paired the United States debut of the Haxan Cloak (the English musician Bobby Krlic) with the German musician Robert Henke and his laser project, Lumière. Mr. Henke’s music and light show were crisp, bright, geometric and propulsive, a calibrated barrage. The Haxan Cloak performance was amorphous, ominous and immersive, a transcendent plunge into darkness and overwhelming pressure. Earplugs were available at the door. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Electronic Double Bill to Shiver the Timbers', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Electronic Double Bill to Shiver the Timbers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Henke, Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Haxan Cloak (Music Group)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Red Bull Music Academy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-12T14:44:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a592c51-0089-506a-9478-e48fb87221de', 'word_count': 456, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a592c51-0089-506a-9478-e48fb87221de'}, {'abstract': 'As lawyers for a Texas death row inmate try to stop his execution, even death penalty critics grudgingly agree that Texas kills with efficiency.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/13/us/facing-challenge-to-execution-texas-calls-its-process-the-gold-standard.html', 'snippet': 'As lawyers for a Texas death row inmate try to stop his execution, even death penalty critics grudgingly agree that Texas kills with efficiency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HUNTSVILLE, Tex. — If Texas executes Robert James Campbell as planned on Tuesday, for raping and murdering a woman, it will be the nation’s first execution since Oklahoma’s bungled attempt at lethal injection two weeks ago left a convicted murderer writhing and moaning before he died.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/13/us/JP-TEXAS-1/JP-TEXAS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/13/us/JP-TEXAS-1/JP-TEXAS-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/13/us/JP-TEXAS-1/JP-TEXAS-1-jumbo.jpg'