Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha C. White', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'White', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7c503ad-ffd7-5eba-a5ac-d4f75b23a1f5', 'word_count': 948, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7c503ad-ffd7-5eba-a5ac-d4f75b23a1f5'}, {'abstract': 'More tickets are being sold, and at higher prices, for a musical that had struggled to fill seats earlier in the season.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/12/tony-nominations-continue-to-boost-gentlemans-guide/', 'snippet': 'More tickets are being sold, and at higher prices, for a musical that had struggled to fill seats earlier in the season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More audience members have been willing to pay full price for “A Gentleman’s Guide to Love & Murder” in the two weeks since the show received 10 Tony Award nominations, the most of any on Broadway this season. According to box office data released on Monday, “Gentleman’s Guide” – a tongue-in-cheek operetta about a British man bumping off his rich relatives – had an average paid admission of $93.91 last week, compared to $82.56 for the week in late April immediately preceding the Tony nominations.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/12/theater/12guide/12guide-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/12/theater/12guide/12guide-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/12/theater/12guide/12guide-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/12/theater/12guide/12guide-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1356, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/12/theater/12guide/12guide-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/12/theater/12guide/12guide-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/12/theater/12guide/12guide-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tony Nominations Continue to Boost ‘Gentleman’s Guide’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Gentleman’s Guide’ Still Rides Tony Wave', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tony Awards (Theater Awards)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daly, Tyne', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McNally, Terrence', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-12T20:56:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5843fb8f-4ec6-5254-949d-aa4b1ccd8fec', 'word_count': 289, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5843fb8f-4ec6-5254-949d-aa4b1ccd8fec'}, {'abstract': 'Eliza Griswold will read from “I Am The Beggar of the World,” a book of landai by female Afghan poets, in New York on May 12, 2014. Griswold wrote for the magazine about young Afghan women for whom writing landai served as a vital, and in some cases sole, link to a wider world.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/12/reading-landai-in-new-york/', 'snippet': 'Eliza Griswold will read from “I Am The Beggar of the World,” a book of landai by female Afghan poets, in New York on May 12, 2014. Griswold wrote for the magazine about young Afghan women for whom writing landai served as a vital, and in some cas...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today, Eliza Griswold reads at the Culture Project in New York from ‘‘I Am the Beggar of the World,’’ a book of landai by Afghan women. Griswold’s translations of the landai — two-line folk poems typically about love and grief — are accompanied by photographs by Seamus Murphy, with whom she traveled to Afghanistan in 2012, a trip that also led to an article for the magazine in April of that year. Griswold wrote about young Afghan women for whom writing landai served as a vital, and in some cases sole, link to a wider world. These young women were often confined to their fathers’ homes in the rural villages where they lived; they would sneak calls to a Kabul-based poetry hotline to read their poems and exchange ideas with other Afghan women — parliamentarians, doctors, professors — whose lives they could only imagine. One young woman, Zarmina, stood out. Her poems were distinctive in their language but also in their fearless questioning of God’s will. Zarmina called as frequently as she could, but she lived in fear of being discovered. One day, as Griswold wrote, she was:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/12/magazine/Landays1/Landays1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 