58:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheila Glaser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glaser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa1df339-bff7-5b70-b999-78c8f73ec398', 'word_count': 401, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa1df339-bff7-5b70-b999-78c8f73ec398'}, {'abstract': 'David Finckel, a cellist and co-artistic director of the Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center, says traveling with his instrument always prompts questions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/13/business/im-a-cellist-not-a-teenage-mutant-ninja-turtle.html', 'snippet': 'David Finckel, a cellist and co-artistic director of the Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center, says traveling with his instrument always prompts questions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’VE been traveling most of my adult life. I’m co-artistic director of the Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center, and I’m one of those musicians who actually like to travel. I’ve hardly ever done any dedicated tourism. In fact, I’ve never been to countries in which I didn’t have a concert, so I’ve never seen Egypt or Tibet, two places I really want to visit.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/13/business/flier/flier-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 851, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/13/business/flier/flier-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 851}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/13/business/flier/flier-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 722, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}