cle', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Yaccino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yaccino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7018f0f-bc43-5cbb-9f0c-e0c8c7d18296', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7018f0f-bc43-5cbb-9f0c-e0c8c7d18296'}, {'abstract': 'To the Brits, Pfizer’s $101 billion bid for AstraZeneca is about sciences, not taxes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/13/opinion/nocera-science-vs-taxes.html', 'snippet': 'To the Brits, Pfizer’s $101 billion bid for AstraZeneca is about sciences, not taxes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the United States, most of the controversy over Pfizer’s $101 billion bid for AstraZeneca has been about the potential tax consequences. Last year, Pfizer paid an effective tax rate of about 27 percent. But if it were to succeed in taking over its British rival — and so far, at least, AstraZeneca has shown zero interest in the idea — it would be able to conduct a maneuver called “inversion,” becoming a British company and paying the British corporate tax rate of 20 percent. It would also be able to access cash that resides overseas, which would be taxed at the American rate of 35 percent if it were repatriated into this country.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'l