-plan-for-tracking-commercial-flights.html', 'snippet': 'The issue gained urgency after Malaysia Airlines Flight 370 disappeared in March with 239 people aboard.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONTREAL — Airlines, governments, pilots and aircraft manufacturers all agreed at a conference on Tuesday that a global system for tracking commercial aircraft is badly needed, a United Nations aviation agency said. But the parties sidestepped a key question: whether aircrews should be able to turn off all the equipment that makes them visible.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Groups Back Global Plan for Tracking Commercial Flights', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Groups Back Global Plan for Tracking Public Flights', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Civil Aviation Organization', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Malaysia Airlines Flight 370', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Aviation Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Security and Warning Systems', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-14T02:51:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/87dfda2a-66d3-5ed3-9e19-d12de2305800', 'word_count': 657, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87dfda2a-66d3-5ed3-9e19-d12de2305800'}, {'abstract': 'The prosecutor at the International Criminal Court said she had reopened a preliminary investigation into accusations that British officials were responsible for the abuse of Iraqi prisoners.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/14/world/europe/the-hague-court-to-review-british-war-crime-inquiry.html', 'snippet': 'The prosecutor at the International Criminal Court said she had reopened a preliminary investigation into accusations that British officials were responsible for the abuse of Iraqi prisoners.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The prosecutor at the International Criminal Court, Fatou Bensouda, said Tuesday that she had reopened a preliminary war crimes investigation into accusations that British officials were responsible for the systematic abuse of Iraqi prisoners by Britain’s military from 2003 to 2008. A similar investigation was closed in 2006, but Ms. Bensouda said she would review whether new information she received in January justified reopening the case. Although Iraq has not signed the Rome Statute, which governs the court’s proceedings, Britain has, and its citizens can in theory be prosecuted for crimes committed in Iraq or elsewhere. However, the court, which is based in The Hague, can proceed only if the allegations are not being properly investigated by the British authorities themselves. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Hague: Court to Review British War Crime Inquiry', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Hague: Court to Review British War Crime Inquiry', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Criminal Court', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Castle, Stephen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisoners of War', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, 