lorio Rally Keeps an Eye on the Past', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antique and Classic Cars', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Alfa Romeo Automobiles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Automobile Federation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sicily (Italy)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-14T17:38:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2457f299-6ae8-5c41-908e-09bec85a906f', 'word_count': 646, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2457f299-6ae8-5c41-908e-09bec85a906f'}, {'abstract': 'Prospect, the exhibition founded in New Orleans as a response to Hurricane Katrina, will return for its third iteration.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/14/new-orleans-art-biennial-to-return-in-october/', 'snippet': 'Prospect, the exhibition founded in New Orleans as a response to Hurricane Katrina, will return for its third iteration.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Prospect, the biennial exhibition program founded by the curator Dan Cameron in New Orleans in 2008 as a way to respond to Hurricane Katrina, will hold its third iteration in the city starting Oct. 25 with a lineup of major artists including Theaster Gates, Carrie Mae Weems and Andrea Fraser, as well as historical works by Gauguin and Jean-Michel Basquiat, organizers said on Wednesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Orleans Art Biennial to Return in October', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Katrina (2005)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Orleans (La)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-14T17:43:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af75b89c-a9ef-5583-816e-fad4f2466c33', 'word_count': 269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af75b89c-a9ef-5583-816e-fad4f2466c33'}, {'abstract': 'Socrates Sculpture Park in Long Island City will be given a freestanding educational studio from the Whitney Museum that has been set up for two years in the museum’s sculpture court.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/14/whitney-museum-to-donate-studio-to-queens-sculpture-park/', 'snippet': 'Socrates Sculpture Park in Long Island City will be given a freestanding educational studio from the Whitney Museum that has been set up for two years in the museum’s sculpture court.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As it prepares for a move downtown, the Whitney Museum of American Art will donate its freestanding educational studio to Socrates Sculpture Park in Long Island City, the two organizations are to announce Thursday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Whitney Museum to Donate Studio to Socrates Sculpture Park', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Socrates Sculpture Park', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Whitney Museum of American Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-14T17:47:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51c42463-a987-5e0b-b2d7-04fd1408621d', 'word_count': 136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51c42463-a987-5e0b-b2d7-04fd1408621d'}, {'abstract': 'A roadside poem in Britain is inscribed on a plaque coated with particles of titanium dioxide, which reacts with pollutants to remove them from the air.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/14/roadside-poem-combats-pollution-literally/', 'snippet': 'A roadside poem in Britain is inscribed on a plaque coated with particles of titanium dioxide, which reacts with pollutants to remove them from the air.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Poetry makes nothing happen,” Auden once wrote. But then he never got a chance to read “In Praise of Air,” a poem by Simon Armitage just installed on a building along a busy road at the University of Sheffield, England, and billed as the world’s first bit of air-cleansing verse.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Roadside Poem Combats Pollution — Literally', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Air Pollution', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'England', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-14T17:52:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Cult