 Belief', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-14T22:57:02+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'Daniel Berehulak for The New York Times', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/c9df56ec-23db-5932-bc84-d366937112d2', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/c9df56ec-23db-5932-bc84-d366937112d2'}, {'abstract': 'Twentieth-century classics by Napoleone Martinuzzi and others will be auctioned next week in Chicago.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/15/garden/italian-glass-for-your-bidding.html', 'snippet': 'Twentieth-century classics by Napoleone Martinuzzi and others will be auctioned next week in Chicago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the most comprehensive collections of 20th-century Italian glass is on view in New York just as it is being dispersed. As a preview for a sale next Tuesday in Chicago, Wright auction house has packed its Manhattan showroom at 980 Madison Avenue with glass vessels and sculptures consigned by the photo stylist and art director Dimitri Levas. He’s been acquiring them since the ’70s, focusing on celebrated makers and designers like Venini, A.V.E.M., Gio Ponti and Carlo Scarpa. Many are from the foresighted store Fifty/50. A few belonged to Robert Mapplethorpe, Mr. Levas’s former collaborator. A 1920s globular blue vase by Napoleone Martinuzzi is estimated at $20,000 to $30,000; an Ercole Barovier mosaic cylinder from the same decade, $25,000 to $35,000. Information: 212-585-0200 or wright20.com.\xa0', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/15/garden/15CURREVENTS1/CURR-3-articleLarge.jpg