, 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41e52b14-0813-50f4-8c91-4b5955e83483', 'word_count': 127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41e52b14-0813-50f4-8c91-4b5955e83483'}, {'abstract': 'Umbra’s new Shift collection features jaunty, mostly handmade furnishings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/15/garden/reaching-back-for-its-design-cred.html', 'snippet': 'Umbra’s new Shift collection features jaunty, mostly handmade furnishings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Umbra is going back to its roots. This Toronto-based company, which started 34 years ago selling chic window shades to small, independent design stores, has grown into a $130 million global business. But Les Mandelbaum, Umbra’s chief executive, keenly missed its design cred. “You get rich, but you lose your soul,” he said. So Mr. Mandelbaum and the company’s design director, Matt Carr, came up with Shift, a brand of goods being introduced this weekend at the International Contemporary Furniture Fair in New York that are jaunty, relatively affordable and in many cases handmade. The new line includes whimsical works like Philippe Malouin’s wood folding chair with a built-in hanger ($250 each), Harry Allen’s Coiled stool with a thread-wrapped rattan tractor seat ($250) and Paul Loebach’s Cup lamp, a combination desk lamp and pencil caddy ($180). “These are all things we actually want to take home,” Mr. Carr said. Information: 800-387-5122 or umbrashift.com.\xa0', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/15/garden/15CURRDECOR/CURR-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 475, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/15/garden/15CURRDECOR/C