', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Knoll', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Knoll', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a7ee22d-e6f9-5d1a-9b8d-e45b3e7577cc', 'word_count': 848, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a7ee22d-e6f9-5d1a-9b8d-e45b3e7577cc'}, {'abstract': 'What do “Get Lost,” “Fun,” “Adventures” and “Nice” say about us?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/15/opinion/collins-my-states-prettier-than-yours.html', 'snippet': 'What do “Get Lost,” “Fun,” “Adventures” and “Nice” say about us?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our topic for today is state tourism slogans. Perhaps that’s not what you had in mind. Perhaps you are from North Dakota (“Legendary”) or North Carolina (“Beauty Amplified”) and are already so self-satisfied you see no reason to worry about the subject at all.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/collins-circular/collins-circular-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 601, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/collins-circular/collins-circular-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 601}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/collins-circular/collins-circular-jumbo-v7.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1023, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/collins-circular/collins-circular-superJumbo-v7.jpg', 'height': 1852, 'width': 1850, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_n