-5e48-861c-f1038f75b5c4'}, {'abstract': 'Eric Shinseki may not respond in any detail to questions about what happened at the Phoenix Veterans Affairs Medical Center, where reports of secret waiting lists first emerged.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/15/us/politics/veterans-affairs-secretary-to-face-senate-questions-on-delayed-care.html', 'snippet': 'Eric Shinseki may not respond in any detail to questions about what happened at the Phoenix Veterans Affairs Medical Center, where reports of secret waiting lists first emerged.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — For many years, the Department of Veterans Affairs has been criticized for requiring veterans to wait long periods for medical appointments, and for having no foolproof system for holding hospital administrators accountable for those delays. So last year, the department took a fateful step intended to strengthen its oversight of part of the system: It established a goal for new patients seeking primary care to be seen within 14 days of calling for an appointment, with the date of that request logged into a computer file so that it could be tracked. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/15/us/SHINSEKI-sub/SHINSEKI-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 622, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/15/us/SHINSEKI-sub/SHINSEKI-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 622}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/15/us/SHINSEKI-sub/SHINSEKI-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 988, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/15/us/SHINSEKI-sub/SHINSEKI-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 20