o died and the services for them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A man in a wrinkled sport coat perches on a bar stool. “I was at your daughter’s wake,” he tells the bartender (Evander Duck). Let’s hope the customer (Noel Joseph Allain) likes that sentence. He’ll say it more than 20 times in William Burke’s brief, oblique “the food was terrible,” at the Bushwick Starr.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16FOODISTERRIBLE/FOODISTERRIBLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 416, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16FOODISTERRIBLE/FOODISTERRIBLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 416}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16FOODISTERRIBLE/FOODISTERRIBLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 709, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16FOODISTERRIBLE/FOODISTERRIBLE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1418, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16FOODISTERRIBLE/16FOODISTERRIBLE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16FOODISTERRIBLE/16FOODISTERRIBLE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16FOODISTERRIBLE/16FOODISTERRIBLE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Funeral Buffet and Other Existential Issues', 'kicker': 'Theater Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Funeral Buffet and Other Existential Issues', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bushwick Starr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Easley, Mary Beth', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burke, William', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'the food was terrible (Play)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Simms, Jason', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-15T20:03:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexis Soloski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soloski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/aba18014-c2ae-5143-8aeb-68b7cd983b92', 'word_count': 351, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aba18014-c2ae-5143-8aeb-68b7cd983b92'}, {'abstract': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/15/the-gun-report-may-15-2014/', 'snippet': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Whenever a child opens fire at a school, wounding and killing classmates and teachers, we want to know why. If the shooter commits suicide during the rampage, all we can do is guess.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/15/opinion/reyes/reyes-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 534, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/15/opinion/reyes/reyes-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 534, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/15/opinion/reyes/reyes-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/15/opinion/reyes/reyes-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/15/opinion/reyes/reyes-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gun Report: May 15, 2014', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-05-15T20:08:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Mascia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mascia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9a55d17-2154-59fa-b828-30195e7b4b93', 'word_count': 2119, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9a55d17-2154-59fa-b828-30195e7b4b93'}, {'abstract': 'Jan Svankmajer’s “Alice,” and Ken Loach’s “Black Jack” are released on DVD.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/18/movies/homevideo/jan-svankmajers-alice-released-on-dvd.html', 'snippet': 'Jan Svankmajer’s “Alice,” and Ken Loach’s “Black Jack” are released on DVD.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Alice in Wonderland” has been adapted as an all-star Paramount production (1933), with Cary Grant as the Mock Turtle; as a British puppet animation (1949), eclipsed by the Disney animated musical (1951), later embraced as a head film by the drug culture of the late ’60s; as Dennis Potter’s meditation on the old age of the real Alice (“Dreamchild,” 1985); and as Tim Burton’s 3-D extravaganza (2010) starring Mia Wasikowska as a 19-year-old Alice. None, however, is stranger than “Alice” (1988), made by the Czech animator Jan Svankmajer, newly remastered and reissued on Blu-ray by First Run Features.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18HOBERMAN1/18HOBERMAN1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18HOBERMAN1/18HOBERMAN1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18HOBERMAN1/18HOBERMAN1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 901, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18HOBERMAN1/18HOBERMAN1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1801, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18HOBERMAN1/18HOBERMAN1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18HOBERMAN1/18HOBERMAN1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18HOBERMAN1/18HOBERMAN1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Life Could be a Dream (if Your Name Is Alice)', 'kicker': 'On Video', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Life Could be a Dream (If Your Name Is Alice)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Video Releases (Entertainment)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DVD (Digital Versatile Disc)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Svankmajer, Jan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Loach, Ken', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'ALICE (MOVIE)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Black Jack (Movie)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Video Recordings, Downloads and Streaming', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-15T20:09:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'subsection_name': 'DVD', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. Hoberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d8bcca4-cefe-51f7-99df-10d031babf64', 'word_count': 1253, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d8bcca4-cefe-51f7-99df-10d031babf64'}, {'abstract': 'Two books explore the run-up to World War II in the United States and Japan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/18/books/review/no-end-save-victory-and-japan-1941.html', 'snippet': 'Two books explore the run-up to World War II in the United States and Japan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In February 1933, President-elect Franklin Roosevelt was nearly murdered in Miami by a gunman whose errant fatal shot struck Mayor Anton Cermak of Chicago. Cermak gallantly told Roosevelt, “I’m glad it was me instead of you.” Today’s Americans should not disagree. Had Roosevelt been killed, the 32nd president of the United States would have been his running mate, Speaker John Nance Garner of Texas, a neophyte in foreign and military affairs, isolationist by instinct and deeply rooted in a Congress determined, notwithstanding the growing threats from Hitler and the imperial Japanese, to keep another president from repeating what a majority of its members considered to be Woodrow Wilson’s catastrophic mistake of needlessly dragging the nation into a distant “foreign war.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BESCHLOSS/18BESCHLOSS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 511, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BESCHLOSS/18BESCHLOSS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 511}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BESCHLOSS/18BESCHLOSS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 872, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BESCHLOSS/18BESCHLOSS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1744, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BESCHLOSS/18BESCHLOSS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BESCHLOSS/18BESCHLOSS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BESCHLOSS/18BESCHLOSS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The March to War', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The March to War', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beschloss, Michael R', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kaiser, David E (1947- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hotta, Eri', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-15T20:10:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Beschloss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beschloss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9bd910ab-01a9-56a8-82d2-3cb4432bb194', 'word_count': 1262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9bd910ab-01a9-56a8-82d2-3cb4432bb194'}, {'abstract': 'The Civil Rights Act of 1964 was passed through the efforts of political operatives, liberal activists — and Lyndon Johnson.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/18/books/review/an-idea-whose-time-has-come-and-the-bill-of-the-century.html', 'snippet': 'The Civil Rights Act of 1964 was passed through the efforts of political operatives, liberal activists — and Lyndon Johnson.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the past century, Congress has passed only a handful of truly transformative pieces of legislation. The Social Security Act comes to mind, as do the G.I. Bill, the Marshall Plan, the laws that created a mammoth military and laid down a web of highways, and the reforms that reopened the nation to immigration. Visionary laws — each and every one — meant to achieve policy aims of striking originality. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BOYLE/18BOYLE-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 419, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BOYLE/18BOYLE-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 419}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BOYLE/18BOYLE-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 714, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BOYLE/18BOYLE-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1429, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BOYLE/18BOYLE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BOYLE/18BOYLE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BOYLE/18BOYLE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'All the Way', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'All the Way', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boyle, Kevin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Purdum, Todd S', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil Rights and Liberties', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Risen, Clay', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-15T20:11:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Boyle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boyle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/16b9cd60-09a3-5c0a-8508-71bd9cdd1f8a', 'word_count': 1219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16b9cd60-09a3-5c0a-8508-71bd9cdd1f8a'}, {'abstract': 'Vladimir I. Yakunin, the head of Russian Railways and an associate of Vladimir V. Putin, made the remarks at a forum he hosted in Berlin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/16/world/europe/russian-businessman.html', 'snippet': 'Vladimir I. Yakunin, the head of Russian Railways and an associate of Vladimir V. Putin, made the remarks at a forum he hosted in Berlin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BERLIN — The president of Russian Railways, Vladimir I. Yakunin, a longtime associate of President Vladimir V. Putin, is persona non grata to Washington. Sanctioned by the United States in retaliation for support of Moscow’s annexation of Crimea, he has been banned from entering the country and any assets there have been frozen.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russian Businessman Under Sanctions Fires Broadside at West', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yakunin, Vladimir I', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berlin (Germany)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ukraine', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embargoes and Sanctions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-15T20:13:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison Smale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd87b011-e3bf-5517-aeaf-ee8688a4f740', 'word_count': 560, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd87b011-e3bf-5517-aeaf-ee8688a4f740'}, {'abstract': 'The pitfalls of an idiosyncratic candidate’s unusual presidential path.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/15/rand-pauls-hard-road/', 'snippet': 'The pitfalls of an idiosyncratic candidate’s unusual presidential path.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is fair to say, I think, that Rand Paul is one of the most interesting major contenders for the Republican nomination in 2016, and by extension for the presidency. (That’s why everyone at this newspaper keeps writing about him!) It’s also fair to say that his path to both destinations would be unique, at least by the standards of modern G.O.P. presidential contenders: There are no obvious recent historical models for the kind of coalition Paul would need to put together to win either the nomination or the presidency, and there isn’t even a recent near-miss candidate (his father, for all his grassroots success, was just a “miss,” with nothing near about it) whose almost-success he could try to build upon.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rand Paul’s Hard Road', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Bill', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Rand', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Silicon Valley (Calif)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-15T20:16:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ebd37340-5b19-5c84-861d-425d25f9a42b', 'word_count': 1071, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ebd37340-5b19-5c84-861d-425d25f9a42b'}, {'abstract': 'President Vladimir V. Putin of Russia, in a letter, said that Ukraine’s gas debt had ballooned in the last month.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/16/world/europe/russia-tells-ukraine-cash-in-advance-for-gas.html', 'snippet': 'President Vladimir V. Putin of Russia, in a letter, said that Ukraine’s gas debt had ballooned in the last month.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — Russia said on Thursday that it would stop supplying gas to Ukraine at the end of the month unless the country paid for it in advance, increasing the pressure on Kiev’s financially pressed government as it struggles to contain a growing rebellion in the country’s east.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russia Tells Ukraine: Cash in Advance for Gas', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yatsenyuk, Arseniy P', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gazprom', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kiev (Ukraine)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Putin, Vladimir V', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-15T20:17:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Roth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba5d7ae6-cf83-5ab4-8132-0a358e7abfcc', 'word_count': 464, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba5d7ae6-cf83-5ab4-8132-0a358e7abfcc'}, {'abstract': 'Cédric Klapisch, whose series of films has been tracking the lives of four characters, explores their evolutions as they near 40 in “Chinese Puzzle.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/18/movies/cedric-klapisch-returns-to-4-characters-in-chinese-puzzle.html', 'snippet': 'Cédric Klapisch, whose series of films has been tracking the lives of four characters, explores their evolutions as they near 40 in “Chinese Puzzle.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shortly after arriving in New York from France to study film, Cédric Klapisch, then 23, was interrogated by a roommate: “Where do you think you’ll be in 10 years?” Though he knew he wanted to direct, Mr. Klapisch, now 53, recalled: “I couldn’t really answer that question. I knew that my life was ahead of me, and didn’t know what my life would look like.” ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18MIDNIGHT1/18MIDNIGHT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18MIDNIGHT1/18MIDNIGHT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xl