 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Hynes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hynes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea2f1b2f-7fec-558d-915d-d66612f8eeb5', 'word_count': 995, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea2f1b2f-7fec-558d-915d-d66612f8eeb5'}, {'abstract': '“Rachel, Monique,” Sophie Calle’s installation at the Episcopal Church of the Heavenly Rest, examines her mother’s life and death through photographs, diary entries and a deathbed film.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/16/arts/design/in-rachel-monique-sophie-calle-eulogizes-her-mother.html', 'snippet': '“Rachel, Monique,” Sophie Calle’s installation at the Episcopal Church of the Heavenly Rest, examines her mother’s life and death through photographs, diary entries and a deathbed film.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wandering into the sumptuous neo-Gothic chapel of the Episcopal Church of the Heavenly Rest on the Upper East Side of Manhattan, you might think you’d come upon the lovingly arranged setting for a funeral. You’ve actually walked into a work of art by the French conceptualist Sophie Calle, an installation called “Rachel, Monique.” It’s an elegiac but frustratingly oblique meditation on the life and the death of Ms. Calle’s mother, Monique Sindler. (The Rachel in the title was another name by which she was known.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/SOPHIECALLE1/SOPHIECALLE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/SOPHIECALLE1/SOPHIECALLE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': N