, 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-15T21:16:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jodi Rudoren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jodi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rudoren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/468509a0-c13a-5e9b-9c30-36827b1848c8', 'word_count': 531, 'uri': 'nyt://article/468509a0-c13a-5e9b-9c30-36827b1848c8'}, {'abstract': '“Chinese Puzzle,” the third film in a trilogy, centers on a womanizing Frenchman who follows his ex-wife to New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/16/movies/chinese-puzzle-a-comedy-directed-by-cedric-klapisch.html', 'snippet': '“Chinese Puzzle,” the third film in a trilogy, centers on a womanizing Frenchman who follows his ex-wife to New York.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The next Proust” is the dubious marketing tag applied to Xavier Rousseau (Romain Duris), a flighty, footloose novelist, by his editor in “Chinese Puzzle,” the French filmmaker Cédric Klapisch’s airborne romantic comedy. So what if there is nothing remotely Proustian about Xavier, the womanizing charmer with an idiot grin from two earlier films — “L’Auberge Espagnole” and “Russian Dolls” — also written and directed by Mr. Klapisch. Xavier, like the other returning characters from those movies, is about to turn 40. But in his carefree attitude, he might as well be 25.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/CHINESE-PUZZLE/CHINESE-PUZZLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/CHINESE-PUZZLE/CHINESE-PUZZLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/CHINESE-PUZZLE/CHINESE-PUZZLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/CHINESE-PUZZLE/CHINESE-PUZZLE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/CHINESE-PUZZLE/CHINESE-PUZZLE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/CHINESE-PUZZLE/CHINESE-PUZZLE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/CHINESE-PUZZLE/CHINESE-PUZZLE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Love, Sex, Marriage and Schopenhauer’s Ghost', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Love, Sex, Marriage and Schopenhauer’s Ghost', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klapisch, Cedric', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Duris, Romain', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reilly, Kelly', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tautou, Audrey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Chinese Puzzle (Movie)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-15T21:30:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9518e85e-49cb-50ae-90e8-a60c9e1246eb', 'word_count': 686, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9518e85e-49cb-50ae-90e8-a60c9e1246eb'}, {'abstract': 'The late artist, who created compelling, thought-provoking abstractions from existing imagery, has a new show opening at Petzel Gallery to complement his in-progress exhibition at MoMA.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/15/on-view-robert-heinecken-object-matter-moma-petzel-gallery/', 'snippet': 'The late artist, who created compelling, thought-provoking abstractions from existing imagery, has a new show opening at Petzel Gallery to complement his in-progress exhibition at MoMA.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Robert Heinecken is having a posthumous resurgence. The artist — who founded the UCLA photography department and\xa0famously described himself as a “para-photographer,”\xa0generated photographic images without using a camera\xa0— has an exhibition called “Object Matter” at MoMA through Sept. 7, his first since his death in 2006. A second show, “I Am Involved in Learning to Perceive and Use Light,” opens tonight at Petzel Gallery and runs through June 21.\xa0The title is taken from a 1974 essay by the artist, which the gallery offers in lieu of a press release. In it, he discusses his intentions, methodologies and views on aesthetic value systems. “My basic tenet is relatively simple,” he explains. “To relate the former to the future, the last to the next.” Heinecken carries out this philosophy quite literally in one of the best known strands of his practice, a related series of double exposures made from the 1960s to the 1990s, in which he culled current events and women’s magazines to find individual pages containing two faces that made unforgettable figurative abstractions when overlaid.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Robert Heinecken, Master of the Photographic Juxtaposition', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Heinecken, Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Petzel Gallery', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-15T21:30:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin McGarry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGarry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/68c6d66d-4aff-5eae-a23b-970835bfaa8a', 'word_count': 314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/68c6d66d-4aff-5eae-a23b-970835bfaa8a'}, {'abstract': 'Joanne Tatham’s cabaret show “Soundtrack New York” is an organized social history of the city, with movie references ranging from “On the Town” to “Midnight Cowboy.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/16/arts/music/joanne-tathams-soundtrack-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'Joanne Tatham’s cabaret show “Soundtrack New York” is an organized social history of the city, with movie references ranging from “On the Town” to “Midnight Cowboy.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'A thematically organized social history of New York City told through several decades of movie songs is the concept of Joanne Tatham’s tourist-friendly cabaret show “Soundtrack New York,” heard at the Metropolitan Room on Tuesday evening. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16TATHAM/16TATHAM-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 471, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16TATHAM/16TATHAM-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 471}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16TATHAM/16TATHAM-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 804, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16TATHAM/16TATHAM-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1609, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16TATHAM/16TATHAM-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16TATHAM/16TATHAM-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16TATHAM/16TATHAM-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Life in the City as a Jazzy Medley of Movie Themes and Show Tunes', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Life in the City as a Jazzy Medley of Movie Themes and Show Tunes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Room', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tatham, Joanne', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-15T21:30:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'sectio