in Asbury Park, N.J.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ASBURY PARK, N.J. — “Life’s rotten,” reasoned Off!’s frontman, Keith Morris, small and bothered, crease-faced, dreadlocked, pacing. This was somewhere around the middle of a 50-minute, 22-song set on Monday night at Asbury Lanes, a bar and bowling alley here with low ceilings and focused sound. And this was his version of crowd riling: bad news first, then a suggestion. “So” he continued, “while you’re out there being abused, used, being treated unfairly, have fun and enjoy yourselves.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bringing the Wisdom of Middle Age to Punk', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bringing the Wisdom of Middle Age to Punk', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'OFF! (Music Group)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-15T21:54:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c56d8874-eab4-5377-9dfd-d20baa36006d', 'word_count': 615, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c56d8874-eab4-5377-9dfd-d20baa36006d'}, {'abstract': '“The Immigrant” tells the story of a Polish woman’s arrival in New York in 1921 and the complicated scoundrel who forces her into prostitution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/16/movies/the-immigrant-with-joaquin-phoenix-and-marion-cotillard.html', 'snippet': '“The Immigrant” tells the story of a Polish woman’s arrival in New York in 1921 and the complicated scoundrel who forces her into prostitution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In American popular culture, and in the private lore of millions of American families, the immigrant experience of the late-19th and early 20th centuries is often presented as a chronicle of struggle and triumph, a parable of dreams come true. In “The Immigrant,” James Gray (who directed the film and wrote it with Richard Menello) tries to push through this rosy nostalgia and recapture some of the terror and strangeness of the journey from the Old World to the New. The first shot is of the Statue of Liberty shrouded in harbor mist, and the film unfolds in the gap between the promise that lady embodies and the harsh realities a newcomer encounters once she gets off the boat. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16IMMIGRANT/IMMIGRANT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16IMMIGRANT/IMMIGRANT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16IMMIGRANT/IMMIGRANT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16IMMIGRANT/IMMIGRANT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16IMMIGRANT/IMMIGRANT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16IMMIGRANT/IMMIGRANT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/arts/16IMMIGRANT/IMMIGRANT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'She’s Just Off the Boat. He’s the Wrong Man.', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'She’s Just Off the Boat. He’s the Wrong Man.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gray, James', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cotillard, Marion', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Phoenix, Joaquin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Renner, Jeremy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Immigrant (Movie)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-15T21:54:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2dec48c4-f223-5c10-8955-628b79064313', 'word_count': 825, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2dec48c4-f223-5c10-8955-628b79064313'}, {'abstract': '“Lygia Clark: The Abandonment of Art, 1948-1988” is a comprehensive survey of this influential Brazilian artist and therapist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/16/arts/design/lygia-clarks-many-twists-and-turns-at-moma.html', 'snippet': '“Lygia Clark: The Abandonment of Art, 1948-1988” is a comprehensive survey of this influential Brazilian artist and therapist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Museum of Modern Art’s sprawling survey of the work of the influential Brazilian artist Lygia Clark has a singularly unpromising title: “Lygia Clark: The Abandonment of Art, 1948-1988” signals something of a letdown, especially if you like art. But once you’ve worked your way through this up-and-down massing of some 300 works, equally fitting and less stark alternatives may come to mind, including “the expansion of art” and “the internalization of art.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'See Me. Feel Me. Maybe Drool on Me.', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'See Me. Feel Me. Maybe Drool on Me.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Modern Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clark, Lygia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-15T21:55:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec9001ad-a93c-57d4-afcb-6e77880d0273', 'word_count': 1563, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec9001ad-a93c-57d4-afcb-6e77880d0273'}, {'abstract': 'Professors respond to a Sunday Review article, “Young Minds in Critical Condition.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/16/opinion/critical-thinking-from-socrates-to-the-ivory-tower.html', 'snippet': 'Professors respond to a Sunday Review article, “Young Minds in Critical Condition.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'hea