d protests in several large cities around Brazil this month as labor unions representing teachers, bus drivers and street cleaners seek wage increases. Police unions across the country said they were considering a one-day strike next week. Separately, protests against Brazil’s spending on World Cup stadiums drew thousands into the streets on Thursday in São Paulo and other cities. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Brazil: Troops Deployed to Restore Calm', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Brazil: Troops Deployed to Restore Calm', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Strikes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romero, Simon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Cup 2014 (Soccer)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T03:01:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Simon Romero', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Romero', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7178e4f3-d177-5a6c-9752-20e3ca9e1784', 'word_count': 126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7178e4f3-d177-5a6c-9752-20e3ca9e1784'}, {'abstract': 'Iran’s oil exports declined in April for the second consecutive month, the International Energy Agency reported Thursday, moving much closer to levels permitted under a temporary accord on the Iranian nuclear dispute that is set to expire in July.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/16/world/middleeast/iran-oil-exports-continue-to-decline.html', 'snippet': 'Iran’s oil exports declined in April for the second consecutive month, the International Energy Agency reported Thursday, moving much closer to levels permitted under a temporary accord on the Iranian nuclear dispute that is set to expire in July.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Iran’s oil exports declined in April for the second consecutive month, the International Energy Agency reported Thursday, moving much closer to levels permitted under a temporary accord on the Iranian nuclear dispute that is set to expire in July. Monthly data compiled by the energy agency, a 29-member group of oil importers, showed that Iran’s exports were 1.11 million barrels a day in April, down from 1.29 million barrels a day in March and 1.58 million barrels a day in February. The temporary accord specified that Iran’s monthly oil exports are not to exceed one million barrels a day on average for the six-month duration, and the excesses raised concern among critics of the accord that Iran was violating its terms. Obama administration officials have said they believe that Iran’s May, June and July exports will be lower. Under the accord, some Western economic sanctions were eased in exchange for a freeze on most of Iran’s nuclear activities, for the purpose of giving negotiators more time to reach a permanent agreement. Negotiators for the five permanent members of the United Nations Security Council plus Germany are seeking guarantees that Iran’s nuclear fuel cannot be used to make bombs. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iran: Oil Exports Continue to Decline', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iran: Oil Exports Continue to Decline', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T03:03:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa43f1b8-3f2d-503d-9056-713c100cdaff', 'word_count': 198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa43f1b8-3f2d-503d-9056-713c100cdaff'}, {'abstract': 'The American military has repatriated 10 Pakistani prisoners whom it had held at Bagram Air Base in Afghanistan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/16/world/asia/afghanistan-us-repatriates-pakistanis.html', 'snippet': 'The American military has repatriated 10 Pakistani prisoners whom it had held at Bagram Air Base in Afghanistan.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The American military has repatriated 10 Pakistani prisoners whom it had held at Bagram Air Base in Afghanistan. The transfer of the detainees to Pakistani custody was announced by the Justice Project Pakistan, which represents Pakistani detainees at Bagram. It said the International Committee of the Red Cross had told the men’s families about the move. An American official familiar with the matter confirmed Thursday that the transfers had taken place. The military has already transferred to Afghan control the bulk of Afghan prisoners at Bagram but has retained custody of “third country nationals,” whom the Afghan government does not want to deal with. The transfers reduce the number of those prisoners to 39, according to the official. More than two dozen of them are Pakistani. Some of those just released will be detained in Pakistan, while others will be released but subject to monitoring, the official said. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Afghanistan: U.S. Repatriates Pakistanis', 'kicker': 'World Briefing ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Afghanistan: U.S. Repatriates Pakistanis', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Savage, Charlie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Detainees', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T03:04:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charlie Savage', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charlie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Savage', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2ac05b1-5a5a-5083-adbb-de7d9583a5ae', 'word_count': 148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2ac05b1-5a5a-5083-adbb-de7d9583a5ae'}, {'abstract': 'In his debut, Jacob deGrom recorded the first hit of the season by a Mets pitcher, but the Yankees earned a split of the Subway Series as Chase Whitley and the bullpen combined on a three-hit shutout.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/16/sports/baseball/in-matchup-of-rookies-mets-offense-is-stifled.html', 'snippet': 'In his debut, Jacob deGrom recorded the first hit of the season by a Mets pitcher, but the Yankees earned a split of the Subway Series as Chase Whitley and the bullpen combined on a three-hit shutout.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first 39 games of the baseball season had already taken their toll on the Yankees and the Mets, and the first three games of the Subway Series had been emotional, exhausting affairs. The Mets had battered the Yankees at Yankee Stadium, and Masahiro Tanaka had stifled the Mets at Citi Field. Everyone seemed a bit woozy. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/sports/16mets2/16mets2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 420, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/16/sports/16mets2/16mets2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 420}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/sports/16mets2/16mets2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 716, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/sports/16mets2/16mets2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1433, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/sports/16mets2/16mets2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/16/sports/16mets2/16mets2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/sports/16mets2/16mets2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Rookie Duel, a Met Gets a Loss, and a Hit', 'kicker': 'Yankees 1, Mets 0', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Rookie Duel, a Met Gets a Loss, and a Hit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, '