', 'value': 'Levine, Kristin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New-York Historical Society', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Little Rock (Ark)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T04:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf71f465-129f-5a7d-a396-313cbff2049c', 'word_count': 201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf71f465-129f-5a7d-a396-313cbff2049c'}, {'abstract': 'Perhaps the biggest new idea in exercise research in recent years has been the endurance running hypothesis. But it’s not a completely new idea.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/16/tracking-down-the-deer-runner/', 'snippet': 'Perhaps the biggest new idea in exercise research in recent years has been the endurance running hypothesis. But it’s not a completely new idea.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Perhaps the biggest new idea in exercise research in recent years has been the endurance running hypothesis, the idea that as humans evolved, adaptations in our bones and muscles, along with an impressive ability to regulate our body temperature, made us good endurance runners. As a result, our ancestors were able to literally run down and kill game animals, providing the high-density calories and protein we needed to feed our ever-enlarging brains.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/15/health/15well_deer2/15well_deer2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/15/health/15well_deer2/15well_deer2-a