 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T11:03:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/c706d085-da3f-5396-95cd-e74a03758955', 'word_count': 1020, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c706d085-da3f-5396-95cd-e74a03758955'}, {'abstract': 'Banks continue to stay in business after being convicted of felons. | Pinterest raises more money. | Investors reject Chipotle chiefs’ pay plan. | Detroit’s bankruptcy deadline may be missed.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/05/16/morning-agenda-big-breaks-for-banks/', 'snippet': 'Banks continue to stay in business after being convicted of felons. | Pinterest raises more money. | Investors reject Chipotle chiefs’ pay plan. | Detroit’s bankruptcy deadline may be missed.', 'lead_paragraph': ' BIG BREAKS FOR BANKS \xa0|\xa0 If, as expected, two banking giants ‒ Credit Suisse and BNP Paribas ‒ plead guilty to criminal charges in the next few weeks, they will probably pay large fines and officially be felons, Floyd Norris writes in the High & Low Finance column. But, he asks, does it matter that these will be criminal, not civil, punishments? The issue is not that banks may be “too big to jail,” but that, big or small, banks and other corporations are “too inanimate to jail,” Mr. Norris writes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Breaks for Banks', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T11:13:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstnam