', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2d5bbaa-4c30-5f53-8c3d-b8d69e5d4d49', 'word_count': 589, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2d5bbaa-4c30-5f53-8c3d-b8d69e5d4d49'}, {'abstract': 'Alan Sepinwall and Matt Zoller Seitz will collaborate on a book of essays for Grand Central Publishing about “the most artistically and culturally significant American dramas, comedies, miniseries, variety shows and more.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/16/tv-critics-buddy-up-for-new-book/', 'snippet': 'Alan Sepinwall and Matt Zoller Seitz will collaborate on a book of essays for Grand Central Publishing about “the most artistically and culturally significant American dramas, comedies, miniseries, variety shows and more.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Whether it’s Felix and Oscar, Bert and Ernie, Cagney and Lacey or Simon and Simon, television has consistently provided a home for memorable duos. Now a pair of veteran television critics are partnering up to write a book that will examine various series while chronicling the six-decade history of the medium. Alan Sepinwall, of HitFix.com, and Matt Zoller Seitz, of New York magazine and Vulture.com, will collaborate on a book of essays for Grand Central Publishing about “the most artistically and culturally significant American dramas, comedies, mini-series, variety shows and more,” including hits like “Seinfeld” and “The Sopranos,” as well as cult favorites like “Freaks and Geeks” and “My So-Called Life,” the publisher said in an email. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'TV Critics Buddy Up for New Book', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TV Critics Buddy Up for a New Book', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Seitz, Matt Zoller', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sepinwall, Alan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Grand Central Publishing', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T14:48:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1faab097-f8b9-516a-81ef-eeeeda74d3be', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1faab097-f8b9-516a-81ef-eeeeda74d3be'}, {'abstract': 'Retracing an ancestor’s steps, I found that two centuries changes only so much.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/18/magazine/upstate-and-back-in-time.html', 'snippet': 'Retracing an ancestor’s steps, I found that two centuries changes only so much.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I followed the man off the river and into a labyrinth of creeks behind town, heading deeper into a thickening haze of marsh grass and listing trees. He paddled ahead in his green canoe while I trailed in my rowboat, picking my way around old dredging pipes and doubting that I’d find my way back out of this fractal backwater. Through the trees, worlds emerged: a tiny island of R.V.s, a string of vacation cottages, a far bank of empty-eyed concrete houses.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/magazine/18ives/18ives-articleLar