155, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75cdb28a-58aa-5d91-8b42-9cb7bfb8b61b'}, {'abstract': 'Next Saturday and Sunday, the New York incarnation of the Electric Daisy Carnival takes over MetLife Stadium in East Rutherford, N.J.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/18/arts/music/dance-music-with-a-warning.html', 'snippet': 'Next Saturday and Sunday, the New York incarnation of the Electric Daisy Carnival takes over MetLife Stadium in East Rutherford, N.J.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last August, two deaths from drug overdoses at the annual Electric Zoo dance-music festival on Randalls Island caused the cancellation of the event’s final day, a reminder of the difficulty of adequately overseeing these sprawling, loosely supervised events. Next Saturday and Sunday, the New York incarnation of the Electric Daisy Carnival, the signature brand in American dance music festivals, takes over MetLife Stadium in East Rutherford, N.J., and will face the challenge of keeping tens of thousands of revelers safe. The music will take care of itself: The lineup includes pop-minded D.J.-producers like Calvin Harris and Afrojack; Steve Angello, one-third of the disbanded arena-house champs known as the Swedish House Mafia; the Chicago house legend Green Velvet; the pop aspirants Krewella; the teenage phenom Martin Garrix; and the French abrasionist DJ Snake. The festival’s website warns of “zero tolerance for drug use or possession.” Fingers crossed. (201-559-1515, electricdaisycarnival.com/newyork.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18GUIDE1_SPAN/18GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18GUIDE1_SPAN/18GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18GUIDE1_SPAN/18GUIDE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18GUIDE1_SPAN/18GUIDE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18GUIDE1_SPAN/18GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18GUIDE1_SPAN/18GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18GUIDE1_SPAN/18GUIDE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dance Music, With a Warning', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dance Music, With a Warning', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T16:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/11bbe746-875d-5c0c-bbe1-db7e7f947dc7', 'word_count': 148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11bbe746-875d-5c0c-bbe1-db7e7f947dc7'}, {'abstract': 'The choreographers Mark Morris, Alexei Ratmansky and Christopher Wheeldon are making imaginative dances from challenging sources, from Shostakovich to Shakespeare.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/18/arts/dance/morris-ratmansky-and-wheeldon-make-complex-dances.html', 'snippet': 'The choreographers Mark Morris, Alexei Ratmansky and Christopher Wheeldon are making imaginative dances from challenging sources, from Shostakovich to Shakespeare.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We may be witnessing one sea change or several in full-length dance-theater productions. In the last 12 months, three of the world’s leading choreographers — Mark Morris, Alexei Ratmansky, Christopher Wheeldon — have staged new, important, mold-breaking, all-evening works. All three are flawed. But so are the ballets of “Swan Lake” and “Romeo and Juliet” (no matter which version you watch). The full-length “Swan Lake” took over 70 years before it became a mainstay of the international repertory. At what point does a new production turn into a hit, a war horse or a classic?', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18GENRE1/18GENRE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18GENRE1/18GENRE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18GENRE1/18GENRE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18GENRE1/18GENRE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18GENRE1/18GENRE1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'heigh