f American Punishment,” about what Cole calls our “proclivity for incarceration.” The most enduring literary evocations of imprisonment have been written by those with firsthand experience; books like Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn’s “One Day in the Life of Ivan Denisovich” and Dostoyevsky’s “The House of the Dead.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Openbook/0518-bks-Openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 264, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Openbook/0518-bks-Openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 264}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Openbook/0518-bks-Openbook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 448, 'width': 1020, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Openbook/0518-bks-Openbook-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 448, 'width': 1020, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Openbook/0518-bks-Openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Openbook/0518-bks-Openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Openbook/0518-bks-Openbook-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cell Lit', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cell Lit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T16:24:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8c03e22-a24b-5b1c-bd54-dd3f335ea399', 'word_count': 460, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8c03e22-a24b-5b1c-bd54-dd3f335ea399'}, {'abstract': 'In this novel, a man who can’t stop walking seeks psychiatric help in 19th-century Bordeaux.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/18/books/review/the-man-who-walked-away-by-maud-casey.html', 'snippet': 'In this novel, a man who can’t stop walking seeks psychiatric help in 19th-century Bordeaux.', 'lead_paragraph': 'First, a confession: Words like “liminal” or “luminous” in a novel’s blurbs or flap copy work for me like flashing hazard lights. Generally, I set the book back down on the new releases table and reach for something else. Experience has taught me that novels so described favor style over story, and I’m an old-school reader who values well-wrought plot and highly developed character more than exquisite voice and stylistic gymnastics.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BROOKS/18BROOKS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 533, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BROOKS/18BROOKS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 533}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BROOKS/18BROOKS-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 910, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BROOKS/18BROOKS-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1762, 'width': 1982, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BROOKS/18BROOKS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BROOKS/18BROOKS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BROOKS/18BROOKS-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'One Step at a Time', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'One Step at a Time', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Casey, Maud', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T16:24:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geraldine Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geraldine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0456423d-b313-559a-9ff5-37e03471da9e', 'word_count': 850, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0456423d-b313-559a-9ff5-37e03471da9e'}, {'abstract': 'In Kaui Hart Hemmings’s new novel, a woman struggles with grief over her son’s death in an avalanche.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/18/books/review/the-possibilities-by-kaui-hart-hemmings.html', 'snippet': 'In Kaui Hart Hemmings’s new novel, a woman struggles with grief over her son’s death in an avalanche.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I have a friend, the mother of twin boys, who recently confessed that the only way she had survived those early parenting years was by pretending she was someone else: a long-distance athlete in training, say, while jogging behind a double stroller before work; or a sexy, spaced-out au pair at the playground diddling on her iPhone during lengthy afternoons; or a bearded Bushwick mixologist whenever the clock struck 5. Any of these roles felt more endurable than her own.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18SCHULMAN/18SCHULMAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 814, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18SCHULMAN/18SCHULMAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 814}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18SCHULMAN/18SCHULMAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 755, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18SCHULMAN/18SCHULMAN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1727, 'width': 1273, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18SCHULMAN/18SCHULMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18SCHULMAN/18SCHULMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18SCHULMAN/18SCHULMAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Digging Out', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Digging Out', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hemmings, Kaui Hart', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T16:25:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Helen Schulman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Helen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9db5593c-b7fb-5618-bc41-b538d9ad20b4', 'word_count': 1139, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9db5593c-b7fb-5618-bc41-b538d9ad20b4'}, {'abstract': 'Flaubert’s “Madame Bovary” and Zola’s “Thérèse Raquin” get the star treatment in new audiobooks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/18/books/review/madame-bovary-and-therese-raquin-as-audiobooks.html', 'snippet': 'Flaubert’s “Madame Bovary” and Zola’s “Thérèse Raquin” get the star treatment in new audiobooks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The publication of Gustave Flaubert’s “Madame Bovary” in 1857 and of Émile Zola’s “Thérèse Raquin” 10 years later roused the French literary establishment against two previously unknown authors who had gone beyond the bounds of all that was held to be morally admissible in fiction with their portraits of adulterous women starved by marriage of sex and romance. The state prosecuted Flaubert for gross indecency, despite the terrible punishment he metes out to his wayward heroine. Zola was not subjected to the same ordeal, but critics treated him as a rogue purveying smut in the name of realism. Both novels have appeared in countless editions since the 19th century. Both have been adapted to the screen. Now they are available as audiobooks, read by two gifted English actresses: “Madame Bovary” by Juliet Stevenson and “Thérèse Raquin” by Kate Winslet.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BROWN/18BROWN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BROWN/18BROWN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BROWN/18BROWN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 615, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BROWN/18BROWN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1229, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BROWN/18BROWN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BROWN/18BROWN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18BROWN/18BROWN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Scandalous Women', 'kicker': 'Audiobooks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Scandalous Women', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Audiobooks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Flaubert, Gustave', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zola, Emile', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Winslet, Kate', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stevenson, Juliet', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T16:25:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frederick Brown', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frederick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/33c04db7-2eb6-57e7-af84-568528e7b692', 'word_count': 1140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33c04db7-2eb6-57e7-af84-568528e7b692'}, {'abstract': 'Actors read works by John O’Hara and Truman Capote set in a bygone Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/18/books/review/john-oharas-new-york-stories-and-truman-capotes-breakfast-at-tiffanys.html', 'snippet': 'Actors read works by John O’Hara and Truman Capote set in a bygone Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It would be hard to think of two writers less alike — stylistically and, for that matter, personally — than Truman Capote and John O’Hara, yet they shared many pre\xadoccupations. Both were fascinated by society high and low, by how people climbed or toppled from one rank to the other, and by how sex and money underpinned the entire system. “Breakfast at Tiffany’s,” Capote’s charming 1958 novella about a self-invented cafe society girl and the admiring writer who lives upstairs, is set during World War II. Most of the stories assembled in the recent Penguin anthology of O’Hara’s New York stories were written in either the ’30s or the ’60s, but some are set decades earlier. And yet in the newly released audio recordings of the two books, maybe even more than on the page, the versions of New York that are evoked seem virtually interchangeable: It’s a city of people on the make or else clinging to their former reputations, where everyone drinks too much, and where you can easily wake up in bed next to someone you barely remember meeting.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18MCGRATH/18MCGRATH-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 332, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18MCGRATH/18MCGRATH-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 332}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18MCGRATH/18MCGRATH-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 566, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18MCGRATH/18MCGRATH-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1132, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18MCGRATH/18MCGRATH-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18MCGRATH/18MCGRATH-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18MCGRATH/18MCGRATH-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Talk of the Town', 'kicker': 'Audiobooks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Talk of the Town', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McGrath, Charles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Breakfast at Tiffany's (Book)", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Audiobooks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Hara, John", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Capote, Truman', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hall, Michael C', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T16:26:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles McGrath', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGrath', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9bab5057-c418-5d60-b9ef-83c85bc07b1f', 'word_count': 1135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9bab5057-c418-5d60-b9ef-83c85bc07b1f'}, {'abstract': 'Celebrities, from Rob Lowe to Diane Keaton, narrate the audiobooks of their memoirs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/18/books/review/rob-lowes-love-life-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Celebrities, from Rob Lowe to Diane Keaton, narrate the audiobooks of their memoirs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Let’s be honest: Celebrities don’t usually write great memoirs. But the best ones offer up dish and dirt and some sensationally bizarre yarns.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18SCHWARTZ1-alt/18SCHWARTZ1-alt-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 459, 'width': 464, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18SCHWARTZ1-alt/18SCHWARTZ1-alt-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 459, 'width': 464, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18SCHWARTZ1-alt/18SCHWARTZ1-alt-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18SCHWARTZ1-alt/18SCHWARTZ1-alt-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18SCHWARTZ1-alt/18SCHWARTZ1-alt-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Acting the Part', 'kicker': 'Audiobooks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Acting the Part', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Love Life (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lowe, Rob', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Audiobooks', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perez, Rosie', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keaton, Diane', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greer, Judy', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T16:26:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Schwartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwartz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/35f669d9-0719-5211-9f1d-95d1f9142bbd', 'word_count': 1403, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35f669d9-0719-5211-9f1d-95d1f9142bbd'}, {'abstract': 'Thousands of miles to go? A World War I history (or two) can be an ideal companion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/18/books/review/audiobooks-about-world-war-i.html', 'snippet': 'Thousands of miles to go? A World War I history (or two) can be an ideal companion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In my home state, California, we listen to audiobooks mostly while driving. When stuck in freeway traffic, I sometimes wonder whether the guy in my rearview mirror is secretly absorbed in “Harry Potter,” or if the smiling woman in the next lane is hearing Mr. Darcy woo Elizabeth Bennet. When it comes to immersing yourself in the First World War by audio, however, you’ll need more than a short commute. The war was very long, the books about it tend to be very long, and about this cataclysm that so thoroughly changed our world for the worse, surely you don’t want to listen to merely one book? So I suggest you reserve this listening for some road trip of epic length, like that drive you’ve always wanted to take from Alaska to Tierra del Fuego, or from Rotterdam to Vladivostok. Here are some suggestions for the journey:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Hochschild-sub/0518-bks-Hochschild-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 134, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Hochschild-sub/0518-bks-Hochschild-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 134}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Hochschild-sub/0518-bks-Hochschild-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 229, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Hochschild-sub/0518-bks-Hochschild-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 458, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Hochschild-sub/0518-bks-Hochschild-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Hochschild-sub/0518-bks-Hochschild-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Hochschild-sub/0518-bks-Hochschild-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Long, Long Road Ahead', 'kicker': 'Audiobooks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Long, Long Road Ahead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War I (1914-18)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hochschild, Adam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Audiobooks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T16:26:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Hochschild', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hochschild', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/44a382e7-2324-5d20-a97c-f2b03a5cf825', 'word_count': 1809, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44a382e7-2324-5d20-a97c-f2b03a5cf825'}, {'abstract': 'A collection that reinterprets classic stories from the perspective of women who are emotionally adrift.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/18/books/review/american-innovations-by-rivka-galchen.html', 'snippet': 'A collection that reinterprets classic stories from the perspective of women who are emotionally adrift.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2008, Rivka Galchen published her first novel, “Atmospheric Disturbances,” about a man who believes his wife has been replaced by a replica of herself. Enfolded into the story was also one Dr. Tzvi Gal-Chen, who shares his name (down to the hyphen) with Galchen’s own father, a meteorologist who died in 1994. Combining aspects of her own biography, the physical sciences and psychiatry — Galchen is herself a medical doctor, trained in psychiatry — she created a vertiginous narrative about the nature of love and loss. Even if the book’s momentum ultimately flagged, it showed Galchen to be a bold, inventive and smart young writer.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'i