itual anomie, other people’s use of social media, the perils of said social media, and baseball. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18GROFF/18GROFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 785, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18GROFF/18GROFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 785}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18GROFF/18GROFF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 783, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18GROFF/18GROFF-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1565, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18GROFF/18GROFF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18GROFF/18GROFF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18GROFF/18GROFF-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Doctor Doubt', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Doctor Doubt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Groff, Lauren', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ferris, Joshua', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T16:27:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lauren Groff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lauren', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Groff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1cc2eec-dad1-5e7a-9f1b-fd8881587597', 'word_count': 1152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1cc2eec-dad1-5e7a-9f1b-fd8881587597'}, {'abstract': 'The ubiquity of data gathering has staggering implications for understanding, predicting and influencing human behavior.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/18/books/review/the-naked-future-and-social-physics.html', 'snippet': 'The ubiquity of data gathering has staggering implications for understanding, predicting and influencing human behavior.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “On What We Can Not Do,” a short and pungent essay published a few years ago, the Italian philosopher Giorgio Agamben outlined two ways in which power operates today. There’s the conventional type that seeks to limit our potential for self-\xaddevelopment by restricting material resources and banning certain behaviors. But there’s also a subtler, more insidious type, which limits not what we can do but what we can not do. What’s at stake here is not so much our ability to do things but our capacity not to make use of that very ability.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Morozov/0518-bks-Morozov-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Morozov/0518-bks-Morozov-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 574}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Morozov/0518-bks-Morozov-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 980, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Morozov/0518-bks-Morozov-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1349, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Morozov/0518-bks-Morozov-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Morozov/0518-bks-Morozov-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-Morozov/0518-bks-Morozov-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Every Little Byte Counts', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Every Little Byte Counts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morozov, Evgeny', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pentland, Alex', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data-Mining and Database Marketing', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tucker, Patrick', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T16:28:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evgeny Morozov', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evgeny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morozov', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf90ea9e-c6c5-5c17-9d33-6a6a640660a0', 'word_count': 1289, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf90ea9e-c6c5-5c17-9d33-6a6a640660a0'}, {'abstract': 'A law professor looks at our criminal justice system and sees an “out-of-control punishment regime.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/18/books/review/inferno-by-robert-a-ferguson.html', 'snippet': 'A law professor looks at our criminal justice system and sees an “out-of-control punishment regime.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'As freedom advances, the severity of the penal law decreases. Or so argued Montesquieu, an Enlightenment philosopher whose work profoundly influenced the drafters of our Constitution. Were he alive today, Montesquieu might need to reconsider. The United States may or may not be the leader of the free world, but it is indisputably the world leader in locking up human beings behind bars. We are less than 5 percent of the world’s population, but we warehouse 25 percent of the world’s incarcerated population. Our per capita incarceration rate is seven times greater than France’s, 14 times greater than Japan’s and 24 times greater than India’s. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18COLE/18COLE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 259, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18COLE/18COLE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 259}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18COLE/18COLE-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 441, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18COLE/18COLE-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 883, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18COLE/18COLE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18COLE/18COLE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/18COLE/18COLE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Punitive Damage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Punitive Damage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cole, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ferguson, Robert A', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T16:28:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Cole', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cole', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9616f8a8-7fcc-57aa-a39b-39cccaa2f538', 'word_count': 1271, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9616f8a8-7fcc-57aa-a39b-39cccaa2f538'}, {'abstract': 'In Julia Dahl’s “Invisible City,” a young reporter for a tabloid newspaper investigates a murder.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/18/books/review/julia-dahls-invisible-city-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In Julia Dahl’s “Invisible City,” a young reporter for a tabloid newspaper investigates a murder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first thing we learn about New York tabloid journalism from INVISIBLE CITY (Minotaur, $24.99) is that a body in Gowanus takes precedence over a brothel in Chinatown. Rebekah Roberts, the narrator of Julia Dahl’s first novel, is fresh out of college and considers herself lucky to be working as a stringer for a bottom-feeding tabloid. Amateur sleuths with day jobs on a newspaper’s staff are usually battle-weary veterans, so it’s a treat to hear Rebekah and her friends trade horror stories about fighting for entry-level positions.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-CRIME/0518-bks-CRIME-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 727, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-CRIME/0518-bks-CRIME-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 727}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-CRIME/0518-bks-CRIME-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 845, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/books/review/0518-bks-CRIME/0518-bks-CRIME-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1690, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'cred