: 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Goldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3c90c65-3b35-51fd-a77e-41f8e2511139', 'word_count': 871, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3c90c65-3b35-51fd-a77e-41f8e2511139'}, {'abstract': 'Joseph L. Bruno, the majority leader of the New York Senate until his resignation in 2008, said after the verdict, “This system, it works.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/17/nyregion/joseph-bruno-former-state-senate-leader-is-acquitted.html', 'snippet': 'Joseph L. Bruno, the majority leader of the New York Senate until his resignation in 2008, said after the verdict, “This system, it works.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'ALBANY — Standing in a federal courthouse in a city he long called his political home, Joseph L. Bruno, the once-powerful majority leader of the New York State Senate, was found not guilty on federal fraud charges on Friday. The verdict put an end to a legal battle nearly a decade in the making.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/nyregion/16BRUNOwebsub/16BRUNOwebsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 495, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/16/nyregion/16BRUNOwebsub/16BRUNOwebsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 495}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/nyregion/16BRUNOwebsub/16BRUNOwebsub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 845, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/nyregion/16BRUNOwebsub/16BRUNOwebsub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1691, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/nyregion/16BRUNOwebsub/16BRUNOwebsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/16/nyregion/16BRUNOwebsub/16BRUNOwebsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/16/nyregion/16BRUNOwebsub/16BRUNOwebsub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bruno, Ex-State Senate Chief, Is Acquitted of Fraud in Retrial', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ex-State Senate Chief Acquitted of Fraud in Retrial; ‘This System, It Works,’ He Says', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bruno, Joseph L', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McKinley, Jesse', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption (Institutional)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T17:02:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse McKinley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/efca30dd-cc94-518b-9dd3-90c58c2f066e', 'word_count': 856, 'uri': 'nyt://article/efca30dd-cc94-518b-9dd3-90c58c2f066e'}, {'abstract': 'Jon Pareles and Ben Ratliff discuss Burger Records, the label, store and cultural phenomenon.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/16/popcast-checking-in-on-burger-records/', 'snippet': 'Jon Pareles and Ben Ratliff discuss Burger Records, the label, store and cultural phenomenon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On this week’s Popcast, we look at the scene around Burger Records, based in Fullerton, Calif. — a teen-centered, low-budget, hyper-productive explosion of the new garage rock, though it isn’t exactly the scholarly, record-obsessed, semi-antiquarian kind we’ve seen in the past. Lee Rickard and Sean Bohrman, former bandmates in Thee Makeout Party!, started the Burger Records label in 2007 and opened a record store two years later; in that time they’ve become the center of a scene that’s generating excitement, loyalty and a sense of identity, especially among an Orange County contingent that’s under the legal drinking age and hungry for local culture. Burger’s pace has become manic lately — it released 300 items last year, between cassette-only editions or reissues and original albums on LP and CD — but it seems to be working: Burgerama, its annual two-day festival in Santa Ana, sold 10,000 tickets this year, and its bands (including Cherry Glazerr, Together Pangea, Audacity, the Garden and Gap Dream) have started to move onto the national radar. (Although if all Burger records ever did was to produce the wild, messy, energetic weekly videos for its YouTube channel, BRGRTV, it might have served its purpose on earth.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18BURGERRECORDS1/18BURGERRECORDS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 371, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/18/arts/18BURGERRECORDS1/18BURGERRECORDS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlarge