te Chorale and the Manhattan Girls Chorus performed a collection of choral songs about war at the Intrepid Sea, Air & Space Museum on Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Human fragility is a recurring theme in the music of David Lang, and “battle hymns,” an almost hourlong collection of choral songs about war, is no exception. On Thursday, this often moving but occasionally precious work received its New York premiere, sung by the Collegiate Chorale and the Manhattan Girls Chorus under the direction of James Bagwell in the 12,000-square-foot Hangar 3 inside the Intrepid Sea, Air & Space Museum. The performance also included six singers from the Veteran Artist Program, a nationwide initiative to engage former service members in the arts. Mr. Lang sets repurposed Civil War texts by Stephen Foster, Abraham Lincoln and Maj. Sullivan Ballou in his trademark spare vocal lines and crystalline textures. The result is a portrait of the soldier as sleepwalker, caught in a no man’s land between patriotism and private longing.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/17/arts/17CHORALE/17CHORALE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 209, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/17/arts/17CHORALE/17CHORALE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 209}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/17/arts/17CHORALE/17CHORALE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 356, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/17/arts/17CHORALE/17CHORALE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 713, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/17/arts/17CHORALE/17CHORALE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/17/arts/17CHORALE/17CHORALE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/17/arts/17CHORALE/17CHORALE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Within a Ship’s Walls, Songs of War in High Voices', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Within a Ship’s Walls, Songs of War in High Voices', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'da Fonseca-Wollheim, Corinna', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Intrepid Sea-Air-Space Museum', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Collegiate Chorale', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Manhattan Girls Chorus', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lang, David', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-16T21:21:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corinna da Fonseca-Wollheim', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/42ab5feb-f9b1-570f-86d6-7bea8d3c76f3', 'word_count': 435, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42ab5feb-f9b1-570f-86d6-7bea8d3c76f3'}, {'abstract': '“Frozen” has gone from movie to phenomenon, at least in part because the passion at its heart is one children of both genders can relate to.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/16/parents-respond-to-the-frozen-frenzy/', 'snippet': '“Frozen” has gone from movie to phenomenon, at least in part because the passion at its heart is one children of both genders can relate to.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We have just a touch of “Frozen” fever at our house. Our spring break trip involved three long plane journeys, and my youngest daughter watched “Frozen” in every available language on the seat-back television screen. We are not big singers of “Let it Go”; we prefer the sprightly “Do You Want to Build a Snowman?”— but my older daughter and the girls we visited over break did engage in vigorous debate over whose version of “Let It Go” was better (“vigorous agreement” might be a better phrase for their passionate preference for the Idina Menzel version over the Demi Lovato one). And anyone, anywhere, can get a laugh at any moment by declaring, “Hi, I’m Olaf, and I like warm hugs.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/fashion/18FROZENWEB/18FROZENWEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 251, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/18/fashion/18FROZENWEB/18FROZENWEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 251}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/fashion/18FROZENWEB/18FROZENWEB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 428, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/fashion/18FROZENWEB/18FROZENWEB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 855, 'width': 2045, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 