value': 'Executives and Management (Theory)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Paramore, the Digital Agency', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paramore, Hannah', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-17T14:25:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Bryant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bryant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/79be6588-82cb-5127-8726-041d4b1a5a9d', 'word_count': 1016, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79be6588-82cb-5127-8726-041d4b1a5a9d'}, {'abstract': 'Joseph Atick, a pioneer of facial-recognition systems, is now cautioning against their unfettered use. Never, he says, should they undermine anyone’s choice to remain anonymous.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/18/technology/never-forgetting-a-face.html', 'snippet': 'Joseph Atick, a pioneer of facial-recognition systems, is now cautioning against their unfettered use. Never, he says, should they undermine anyone’s choice to remain anonymous.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joseph J. Atick cased the floor of the Ronald Reagan Building and International Trade Center in Washington as if he owned the place. In a way, he did. He was one of the organizers of the event, a conference and trade show for the biometrics security industry. Perhaps more to the point, a number of the wares on display, like an airport face-scanning checkpoint, could trace their lineage to his work. ', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/18/business/18face-illo/18face-illo-articleLarge.jpg',