ate': '2014-05-17T18:08:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Donadio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Donadio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd702969-9b99-5453-9bdc-6790fa011e09', 'word_count': 685, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd702969-9b99-5453-9bdc-6790fa011e09'}, {'abstract': 'Dan Merzel is scheduled to umpire 142 minor league games in a 152-day stretch and be paid $3,000 a month. But he says it is worth it as he pursues his goal to be an umpire in the major leagues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/18/sports/baseball/on-the-long-road-committed-to-a-goal-of-officiating-in-the-major-leagues.html', 'snippet': 'Dan Merzel is scheduled to umpire 142 minor league games in a 152-day stretch and be paid $3,000 a month. But he says it is worth it as he pursues his goal to be an umpire in the major leagues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TRENTON — A few days each week, before Dan Merzel heads to the ballpark to umpire in the Class AA Eastern League, he tutors high-achieving middle school and high school students participating in a program sponsored by Johns Hopkins. He communicates with them via Skype and email and uses a virtual whiteboard to solve math problems and answer questions.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Johns Hopkins Graduate, and an Umpire', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Johns Hopkins Graduate, and an Umpire', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Casey, Tim', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Officiating (Sports)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minor Leagues', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Eastern League', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Merzel, Dan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-17T18:19:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Casey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Casey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a58c4d17-ccab-5ba0-9a06-a98dc8a70155', 'word_count': 1106, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a58c4d17-ccab-5ba0-9a06-a98dc8a70155'}, {'abstract': 'The Department of Veterans Affairs is facing scrutiny as revelations mount about hospitals concealing the real wait time for patients seeking primary care. The controversy will dominate the Sunday shows conversations.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/17/sunday-breakfast-menu-may-18/', 'snippet': 'The Department of Veterans Affairs is facing scrutiny as revelations mount about hospitals concealing the real wait time for patients seeking primary care. The controversy will dominate the Sunday shows conversations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eric Shinseki, the secretary of veterans affairs, fired the department’s head of health care in response to a growing controversy that veterans hospitals in Arizona, Colorado, Texas, Wyoming and elsewhere were concealing the wait time for patients. Last year, Veterans Affairs set a goal for new patients seeking primary care to be seen within 14 days of requesting an appointment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sunday Breakfast Menu, May 18', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-05-17T18:24:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_de