': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ef691b3-b9d5-5944-8902-beb06fe1a5c2', 'word_count': 480, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ef691b3-b9d5-5944-8902-beb06fe1a5c2'}, {'abstract': '“Hardcore Activity in Progress” featured 15 acts on overlapping stages Friday night at the Knockdown Center in Queens, including Gunplay, Wolf Eyes and Napalm Death.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/19/arts/music/hardcore-activity-in-progress-batters-the-knockdown-center.html', 'snippet': '“Hardcore Activity in Progress” featured 15 acts on overlapping stages Friday night at the Knockdown Center in Queens, including Gunplay, Wolf Eyes and Napalm Death.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Hardcore Activity in Progress,” a one-night, 15-act concert Friday at the Knockdown Center in Maspeth, Queens, represented an idea. The idea was the sound of extremes — going for it, boundary-testing, hardness — across musical languages. So, for instance, there was the noise band Wolf Eyes, the cello improviser Okkyung Lee, the grindcore group Napalm Death, the rapper Gunplay, the post-minimal piano rhapsodist Lubomyr Melnyk, the free-jazz trio the Thing, and the electronic musician Tim Hecker, presented in overlapping sets, in three different parts of a 50,000-square-foot former door-frame factory. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Six Hours at a Festival, the Sound of Extremes Colliding', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For Six Hours at a Festival, the Sound of Extremes Colliding', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Okkyung', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Knockdown Center (Maspeth, Queens)', 'ran