d encountered bodies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/19/world/americas/memo-to-olympic-sailors-in-rio-dont-touch-the-water.html', 'snippet': 'Guanabara Bay, the venue for the 2016 sailing and windsurfing events, is so foul that a biologist called it “a latrine” and a sailor said he had encountered bodies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'RIO DE JANEIRO — Nico Delle Karth, an Austrian sailor preparing for the 2016 Summer Olympics, said it was the foulest place he had ever trained.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Note to Olympic Sailors: Don’t Fall in Rio’s Water', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Note to Olympic Sailors: Don’t Fall in Rio’s Water', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2016)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romero, Simon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rio de Janeiro (Brazil)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clarey, Christopher', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sailboats and Sailing', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sewers and Sewage', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Olympic Committee', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coates, John D', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bitti, Francesco Ricci', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water Pollution', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guanabara Bay (Rio de Janeiro, Brazil)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-18T22:38:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Simon Romero and Christopher Clarey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Romero', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clarey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab25ff7e-e946-5976-938e-85a9425e2bdf', 'word_count': 1251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab25ff7e-e946-5976-938e-85a9425e2bdf'}, {'abstract': 'Instances of wrongful imprisonment have made clear that microscopic hair analysis, a staple of forensics for years, was not as flawless as people had been led to believe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/19/us/dna-analysis-exposes-an-inexact-forensic-science.html', 'snippet': 'Instances of wrongful imprisonment have made clear that microscopic hair analysis, a staple of forensics for years, was not as flawless as people had been led to believe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By now — despite the apparent infallibility of detectives from Sherlock Holmes to Lieutenant Columbo, despite the clinical genius of wizards from Dr. Quincy to Gil Grissom — it should surprise no one that forensic science is not the model of exactitude that popular culture might have us believe. The scientific rigor of entrenched forensic disciplines has been challenged for years. Still, we live in a “C.S.I.” world, and television viewers could be forgiven for assuming that laboratory techniques used to catch bad guys are unassailable. In real life, though, the soundness of criminal analysis is being regularly tested, both in America’s labs and in its courtrooms.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'DNA Analysis Exposes Flaws in an Inexact Forensic Science', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Innocence Project', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forensic Science', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-18T22:38:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/006f60e8-a829-5337-ae51-441d920741c6', 'word_count': 956, 'uri': 'nyt://article/006f60e8-a829-5337-ae51-441d920741c6'}, {'abstract': 'The Pacers were the deeper, more composed team, spreading out the scoring with six players in double figures in upending the defending champion Heat in Game 1.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/19/sports/basketball/pacers-all-around-effort-proves-too-much-for-heat.html', 'snippet': 'The Pacers were the deeper, more composed team, spreading out the scoring with six players in double figures in upending the defending champion Heat in Game 1.', 'lead_paragraph': 'INDIANAPOLIS — Change the narrative. Alter the perception. Convince the basketball-viewing world that the team with the best record this season in the Eastern Conference could actually make it to the N.B.A. finals.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/19/sports/pacers/pacers-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 521, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/19/sports/pacers/pacers-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 521}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': N