er on Predatory Colleges', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'For-Profit Schools', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-19T01:03:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/5cb00ba3-15f0-54b5-b171-efcee77c855c', 'word_count': 336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5cb00ba3-15f0-54b5-b171-efcee77c855c'}, {'abstract': 'The special-interest money unleashed by recent Supreme Court rulings has made judicial elections worse than ever.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/19/opinion/judges-and-justice-for-sale.html', 'snippet': 'The special-interest money unleashed by recent Supreme Court rulings has made judicial elections worse than ever.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Judicial elections have always been a bad idea. The special-interest money unleashed by recent Supreme Court rulings has made them even worse, greatly increasing the influence of political-action committees and supposedly independent groups financed by corporations, unions and other interests with issues before the courts. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Judges and Justice for Sale', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Judges and Justice for Sale', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank':