', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Strikes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-19T10:09:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d812fdb4-cd78-5741-9dca-cbe45eabded1', 'word_count': 438, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d812fdb4-cd78-5741-9dca-cbe45eabded1'}, {'abstract': 'Things that shouldn’t be problems sometimes are.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/19/demography-and-the-bicycle-effect/', 'snippet': 'Things that shouldn’t be problems sometimes are.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Alvin Hansen first proposed the concept of secular stagnation, he emphasized the role of slowing population growth in depressing investment demand (and his warnings were made moot by the postwar baby boom.) Modern discussions return to that emphasis: Japan’s shrinking working-age population looks like an important source of its problems, and slowing population growth in Europe and America are important reasons to believe that we may be entering a similar regime.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Demography and the Bicycle Effect', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-05-19T10:12:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organizatio