he balance of power with media companies as the market for broadband Internet and video shifts,” Michael J. de la Merced and David Gelles write in DealBook. With the acquisition, AT&T becomes the latest telecommunications giant seeking to establish an even greater reach.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'AT&T’s Move for DirecTV', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-05-19T11:57:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71813a29-6445-51ca-a783-8fa053915978', 'word_count': 1949, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71813a29-6445-51ca-a783-8fa053915978'}, {'abstract': 'The Chinese Embassy said 10 of its nationals had gone missing from a camp near the Nigerian border and the identity of the assailants was unknown, but it noted that the area had seen frequent attacks by Boko Haram.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/19/boko-haram-suspected-in-attack-on-chinese-workers-in-cameroon/', 'snippet': 'The Chinese Embassy said 10 of its nationals had gone missing from a camp near the Nigerian border and the identity of the assailants was unknown, but it noted that the area had seen frequent attacks by Boko Haram.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ten Chinese workers in northern Cameroon are missing following an attack last week by gunmen, the Chinese Embassy in the West African country said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Boko Haram Suspected in Attack on Chinese Workers in Cameroon', 'kicker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo'