 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Davidoff Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Davidoff', 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a3cf152-03f0-5760-95f1-ff900aae6975', 'word_count': 884, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a3cf152-03f0-5760-95f1-ff900aae6975'}, {'abstract': 'It would be nice if we could tackle inequality with a growth approach and without some sacrifices from the wealthy, but that path simply doesn’t exist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/20/upshot/to-lift-the-poor-you-cant-avoid-taxing-the-rich.html', 'snippet': 'It would be nice if we could tackle inequality with a growth approach and without some sacrifices from the wealthy, but that path simply doesn’t exist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s tempting to think — and hope — that attacking inequality doesn’t mean we have to hurt those at the top. This idea has become a central response from conservatives to the current debate inspired by Thomas Piketty’s book and proposal for a wealth tax.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Lift the Poor, You Can’t Avoid Taxing the Rich', 'kicker': "Today's Economist", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Tax', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inheritance and Estate Taxes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-19T16:10:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jared Bernstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jared', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bernstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b65afd53-e2a0-5235-b23f-cf54998886e3', 'word_count': 723, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b65afd53-e2a0-5235-b23f-cf54998886e3'}, {'abstract': 'The original Herb Trimpe art for the comic page that introduced Wolverine, one of the most popular heroes of Marvel Comics, sold on Friday for $657,250, part of which will benefit a charity.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/19/first-wolverine-comic-art-is-sold-for-nearly-660000/', 'snippet': 'The original Herb Trimpe art for the comic page that introduced Wolverine, one of the most popular heroes of Marvel Comics, sold on Friday for $657,250, part of which will benefit a charity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The original art for the comic book page that introduced Wolverine, one of the most popular heroes of Marvel Comics, sold on Friday for $657,250 at Heritage Auctions. The superhero is introduced in the final moments of issue no. 180 of “The Incredible Hulk,” from 1974, which finds the green Goliath wreaking havoc in Canada. Wolverine is called in to stop the Hulk’s rampage. As he makes his appearance in the final panel, he proclaims: “If you really want to tangle with someone – why not try your luck against – the Wolverine!” (His first full appearance would be in the following issue of “The Incredible Hulk.”)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/19/arts/19artsbeat-wolverine/19artsbeat-wolverine-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 901, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/19/arts/19artsbeat-wolverine/19artsbeat-wolverine-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 901}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/19/arts/19artsbeat-wolverine/19artsbeat-wolverine-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/19/arts/19artsbeat-wolverine/19artsbeat-wolverine-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1261, 'width': 840, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/19/arts/19artsbeat-wolverine/19artsbeat-wolverine-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/19/arts/19artsbeat-wolverine/19artsbeat-wolverine-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/19/arts/19artsbeat-wolverine/19artsbeat-wolverine-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'First Wolverine Comic Art Is Sold for Nearly $660,000', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trimpe, Herb', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Marvel Comics', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-19T16:17:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Gene Gustines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': 'Gene', 'lastname': 'Gustines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_mat