coln Center juxtaposed Mendelssohn and Brahms for its final program of the season at Alice Tully Hall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Take two of the most popular crudités, vegetables that are neither too similar nor too different. (Zucchini and squash, maybe.) Cut a few slices of each, and serve — careful not to mix them — on the same plate.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In This Romantic Parting, Little Drama or Moodiness', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In This Romantic Parting, Little Drama or Moodiness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woolfe, Zachary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-19T20:48:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/841c2ac7-b0f9-5506-a4e8-c23b28d45d79', 'word_count': 388, 'uri': 'nyt://article/841c2ac7-b0f9-5506-a4e8-c23b28d45d79'}, {'abstract': 'After they diverged from brown bears, polar bears quickly evolved to survive in their frigid environment, researchers say.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/20/science/when-polar-bears-said-goodbye-to-brown.html', 'snippet': 'After they diverged from brown bears, polar bears quickly evolved to survive in their frigid environment, researchers say.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Polar bears diverged from brown bears less than 500,000 years ago, researchers who sequenced the polar bear’s genome reported in the journal Cell. The researchers also discovered several genes related to fatty acid metabolism and cardiovascular function in the polar bear. These genes may explain how the bear subsists on a high-fat diet without suffering from clogged arteries and cardiovascular disease.\xa0', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/science/20FLOA/20FLOA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/20/science/20FLOA/20FLOA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/science/20FLOA/20FLOA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/science/20FLOA/20FLOA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 854, 'width': 1286, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/science/20FLOA/20FLOA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/20/science/20FLOA/20FLOA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/science/20FLOA/20FLOA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Polar Bears Said Goodbye to Brown', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Genetics: When Polar Bears Said Goodbye to Brown', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polar Bears', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cell (Journal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-19T20:48:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0fbd600-2a8c-5885-9fcb-15fd4b7b23af', 'word_count': 61, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0fbd600-2a8c-5885-9fcb-15fd4b7b23af'}, {'abstract': 'Dr. Stanley B. Prusiner offers an entertaining account of the obstacles and skepticism he faced in his discovery of prions, which won him a Nobel Prize in 1997.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/20/science/madness-and-memory-a-memoir-about-discovering-prions.html', 'snippet': 'Dr. Stanley B. Prusiner offers an entertaining account of the obstacles and skepticism he faced in his discovery of prions, which won him a Nobel Prize in 1997.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sweet revenge comes in many delectable forms, among them the receipt of accolades for work long scorned. And then to get to tell the whole story at length and without a single interruption — small wonder that the Nobel laureate Dr. Stanley B. Prusiner, a renowned neurologist at the University of California, San Francisco, writes with a cheerful bounce. Once disparaged, his scientific work is now hailed as visionary, and his memoir takes the reader on a leisurely and immensely readable victory lap from then to now.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/science/20SCIB/20SCIB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 719, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge':