age, looks inviting, its sleek gold awning standing out along a quiet, leafy block of Bleecker Street. Step inside, though, and the antiseptic interior makes you wonder if you’ve walked into a hospital. Descend the stairwell, and it deposits you in the gloomy black-box theater, where MorDance, a fledgling contemporary ballet company directed by Morgan McEwen, presented a triple bill on Saturday.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/20MORDANCE/20MORDANCE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 413, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/20MORDANCE/20MORDANCE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 413}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/20MORDANCE/20MORDANCE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 705, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/20MORDANCE/20MORDANCE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1410, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/20MORDANCE/20MORDANCE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/20MORDANCE/20MORDANCE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/20MORDANCE/20MORDANCE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Troupe and Its Leader, Bodies Willing, Try to Gain a Foothold', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Troupe and Its Leader, Bodies Willing, Try to Gain a Foothold', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MorDance (Dance Co)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sheen Center', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McEwen, Morgan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-19T21:15:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Siobhan Burke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Siobhan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f8ffe70-6342-5506-991c-bddf02cbb1bf', 'word_count': 408, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f8ffe70-6342-5506-991c-bddf02cbb1bf'}, {'abstract': 'In “Lost for Words,” Edward St. Aubyn takes a break from painful autobiographical novels for a thinly veiled satire of the Man Booker Prize.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/20/books/edward-st-aubyns-lost-for-words.html', 'snippet': 'In “Lost for Words,” Edward St. Aubyn takes a break from painful autobiographical novels for a thinly veiled satire of the Man Booker Prize.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In recounting the story of his semi-autobiographical hero, Patrick Melrose, in a series of interlinked novels (“Never Mind,” “Bad News,” “Some Hope,” “Mother’s Milk” and “At Last”), Edward St. Aubyn established himself as a dazzling writer with an utterly distinctive voice. The harrowing story of Patrick’s life — from being raped at 5 by his monstrous father, to his years as a heroin addict, to his achievement of a measure of perspective and peace in middle age — was simultaneously heartbreaking and funny, devastatingly sad and cuttingly acerbic: painful experience transmuted into art through beautifully hammered, aphoristic prose. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/book/book-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 887, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/book/book-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 887}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/book/book-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 693, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/book/book-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1386, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/book/book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/book/book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/book/book-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Skewering Britain’s High-Tone Scribblers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Skewering Britain’s High-Tone Scribblers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'St Aubyn, Edward', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-19T21:15:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michiko Kakutani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michiko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kakutani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1babbace-b927-55d4-a9d5-325b081e8ebb', 'word_count': 901, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1babbace-b927-55d4-a9d5-325b081e8ebb'}, {'abstract': 'The trade group for the industry responds to an article about the hazards of pesticide use.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/20/opinion/benefits-of-pesticides.html', 'snippet': 'The trade group for the industry responds to an article about the hazards of pesticide use.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Benefits of Pesticides', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Benefits of Pesticides', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pesticides', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-19T21:15:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1593677-eeef-58d5-b821-b88321762acf', 'word_count': 194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1593677-eeef-58d5-b821-b88321762acf'}, {'abstract': 'The Bavarian Radio Symphony found a wild side to Brahms’s Second Symphony at Carnegie Hall on Sunday in a program that also included Ligeti and Berg.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/20/arts/music/bavarian-radio-symphony-performs-brahms-and-ligeti.html', 'snippet': 'The Bavarian Radio Symphony found a wild side to Brahms’s Second Symphony at Carnegie Hall on Sunday in a program that also included Ligeti and Berg.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In historical accounts and program notes, you read that of the four Brahms symphonies, the second is the sunny one. After struggling for some 20 years to write his first symphony, a turbulent work that ends in hard-won triumph, Brahms wrote his Symphony No. 2 in D during a few months of summer vacation in 1877. The music, overall, is thought to be genial and, despite some mysterious moments, affirming.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/20SYMPHONY/20SYMPHONY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 425, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/20SYMPHONY/20SYMPHONY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 425}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/20SYMPHONY/20SYMPHONY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 725, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/20SYMPHONY/20SYMPHONY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1449, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/20SYMPHONY/20SYMPHONY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/20SYMPHONY/20SYMPHONY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/20/arts/20SYMPHONY/20SYMPHONY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headli