t', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recalls and Bans of Products', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-20T00:15:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Vlasic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vlasic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b27b2b03-0b4d-558d-aa26-52a42e2ae949', 'word_count': 464, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b27b2b03-0b4d-558d-aa26-52a42e2ae949'}, {'abstract': 'This short documentary chronicles a remote Peruvian village’s complicated path to getting connected to the outside world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/20/opinion/wiring-the-amazon.html', 'snippet': 'This short documentary chronicles a remote Peruvian village’s complicated path to getting connected to the outside world.', 'lead_paragraph': "This Op-Doc video chronicles the four-year struggle to get a remote Peruvian village connected with the outside world. I began filming the project in 2009, while making a feature-length documentary film about the global spread of information technology. I was following the work of One Laptop per Child (O.L.P.C.), a United States-based nonprofit that has designed inexpensive laptops for primary education. The organization is particularly active in Peru, where the government has purchased and distributed hundreds of thousands of O.L.P.C.'s laptops to its poorest communities. One such village is Palestina, deep in the Amazon rain forest. It has only 65 people.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Wiring the Amazon’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'One Laptop Per Child', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Peru', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Palestina (Purus, Peru)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-20T00:16:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Kleiman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kleiman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/2527ff63-9b74-5561-b41b-83bdc9598b73', 'word_count': 368, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2527ff63-9b74-5561-b41b-83bdc9598b73'}, {'abstract': 'When writing biographies of intellectuals, the life is secondary to the thinking.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/19/writing-about-a-life-of-ideas/', 'snippet': 'When writing biographies of intellectuals, the life is secondary to the thinking.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When an intellectual dies, there are no heaps of flowers or public mournings. The odd wistful editorial will be written in upmarket newspapers; colloquia organized in ivy-clad colleges. Few Americans will know, for instance, that Ronald Dworkin, a formidable figure in American liberal philosophy, died last year. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-draft/opinionator-pog-draft-articleLarge.png', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-draft/opinionator-pog-draft-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-draf