 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-21T01:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mireya Navarro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mireya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Navarro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e817182-8305-5a9b-9c44-4343553fb445', 'word_count': 781, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e817182-8305-5a9b-9c44-4343553fb445'}, {'abstract': 'Speaking at an event in New York, William Dudley said the trajectory of economic growth continues to disappoint.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/21/business/economy/new-york-fed-president-sees-inflation-drifting-up.html', 'snippet': 'Speaking at an event in New York, William Dudley said the trajectory of economic growth continues to disappoint.', 'lead_paragraph': 'William Dudley, the president of the New York Federal Reserve, said on Tuesday he expected inflation to drift higher over the remainder of the year, though “excess capacity” in the economy is helping to moderate price pressures. Mr. Dudley’s remarks come as the Fed grapples with several major issues, including when to raise interest rates, and when to start scaling back its more than $4 trillion balance sheet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York Fed President Sees Inflation Drifting Up', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve Bank of New York', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dudley, William C', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-21T01:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9abc38cf-b23c-54c7-b06a-886b615ce980', 'word_count': 400, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9abc38cf-b23c-54c7-b06a-886b615ce980'}, {'abstract': 'It is unjust to dump administrative fees on people caught up in the criminal justice system, often some of the poorest in society.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/21/opinion/pay-up-or-go-to-jail.html', 'snippet': 'It is unjust to dump administrative fees on people caught up in the criminal justice system, often some of the poorest in society.', 'lead_paragraph': 'User fees are a fact of life in America — those inscrutable “administrative” charges tacked on to everything from checking luggage to buying theater tickets to applying for college. For people with the ability to pay, they are an irritation. But such fees are increasingly being levied on people caught up in the criminal justice system, who are overwhelmingly among the poorest members of society.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pay Up or Go to Jail', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pay Up or Go to Jail', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-21T01:05:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca0eacf9-6a30-5f49-95db-75d258a1f413', 'word_count': 564, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca0eacf9-6a30-5f49-95db-75d258a1f413'}, {'abstract': 'Another year, and another draft lottery won by the Cleveland Cavaliers, who had only a 1.7 percent chance of snagging the pick.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/21/sports/basketball/for-third-time-in-4-years-cavs-win-nba-lottery.html', 'snippet': 'Another year, and another draft lottery won by the Cleveland Cavaliers, who had only a 1.7 percent chance of snagging the pick.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a group of teams coming off dreadful seasons, the hours before Tuesday’s N.B.A. draft lottery were a time of rare hope. They all had a crack at the grand prize: the opportunity to select a player like Duke’s Jabari Parker or Kansas’ Andrew Wiggins, elite prospects who are expected to be among the top picks when the draft is held at Barclays Center on June 26.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/21/sports/subNBAcity/subNBAcity-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 815, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/21/sports/subNBAcity/subNBAcity-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 815}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/21/sports/subNBAcity/subNBAcity-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 754, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/21/sports/subNBAcity/subNBAcity-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1507, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'r