ical candidates wouldn’t seem to need to mention, it’s the state in which they are running. And if there is one fact most relevant to voters during these partisan times, it is a candidate’s political party.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/22/upshot/22UP-ADS-copy/22UP-ADS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/22/upshot/22UP-ADS-copy/22UP-ADS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/22/upshot/22UP-ADS-copy/22UP-ADS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/22/upshot/22UP-ADS-copy/22UP-ADS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/22/upshot/22UP-ADS-copy/22UP-ADS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/22/upshot/22UP-ADS-copy/22UP-ADS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/22/upshot/22UP-ADS-copy/22UP-ADS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Candidates Ace Geography in Their Ads', 'kicker': 'Hidden Messages', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Senate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting and Voters', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-21T17:33:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Vavreck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vavreck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26e57fa9-a270-5886-97d5-c8c7db893071', 'word_count': 389, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26e57fa9-a270-5886-97d5-c8c7db893071'}, {'abstract': 'Two new picture books explore the amusing idea of animals having the kind of free will humans enjoy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/21/books/this-is-a-moose-and-froodle.html', 'snippet': 'Two new picture books explore the amusing idea of animals having the kind of free will humans enjoy.', 'lead_paragraph': '“When is a moose not a moose?" could be the first line of an elementary-school joke. In Richard T. Morris’s “This Is a Moose,” illustrated by Tom Lichtenheld, a moose is not a moose when he wants to be an astronaut. And in “Froodle,” written and illustrated by Antoinette Portis, a little brown bird causes revolution — or is it evolution? — among her avian friends when she decides to say something other than “Peep!” Both books explore the amusing idea of animals having the kind of free will humans enjoy. By playing up the contrast between people and wildlife, Morris and Portis subtly encourage children to make their own choices, even when told they can’t.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/21/books/21childrens/21childrens-articleLarg