'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Trip Gabriel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Trip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gabriel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0299f3a6-4697-58ee-a8eb-cfdc9e1bb9ee', 'word_count': 671, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0299f3a6-4697-58ee-a8eb-cfdc9e1bb9ee'}, {'abstract': 'Much of the literary world (not all, but a lot) is enthralled by the Norwegian writer Karl Ove Knausgaard and his multipart novelistic memoir of minutiae, titled “My Struggle.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/22/books/karl-ove-knausgaards-my-struggle-is-a-movement.html', 'snippet': 'Much of the literary world (not all, but a lot) is enthralled by the Norwegian writer Karl Ove Knausgaard and his multipart novelistic memoir of minutiae, titled “My Struggle.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Karl Ove Knausgaard, a handsome, rangy, brooding Norwegian author (and father of four young children) has held much of the literary establishment in thrall ever since his 3,600-page, six-part autobiographical novel, “My Struggle,” started appearing in English in 2012. Direct and unguarded in tone, the books combine a micro-focus on the granular detail of daily life (child care, groceries, quarrels with friends) with earnest meditations on art, death, music and ambition. The novelist Jeffrey Eugenides, calling these two elements “auto-fiction” and “rumination,” said that blending them in the way Mr. Knausgaard does is something “nobody’s done before.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/09/18/arts/18book2-print/22KARLOVE-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 620, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/09/18/arts/18book2-print/22KARLOVE-articleLarg