: 'nyt://article/ab107865-a549-5318-93ee-bc9996ef1cf1', 'word_count': 105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab107865-a549-5318-93ee-bc9996ef1cf1'}, {'abstract': 'A show on Cecil Beaton’s country estates opens at the Salisbury Museum in England.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/22/garden/in-the-houses-where-he-lived.html', 'snippet': 'A show on Cecil Beaton’s country estates opens at the Salisbury Museum in England.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the 1930s until his death in 1980, Cecil Beaton was a pivotal figure in design and fashion, thanks to his photography for Vogue and sets and costumes for “My Fair Lady.” There have been many exhibitions about his work, but none about how he lived. This will change Friday when “Cecil Beaton at Home” opens at the Salisbury Museum in England’s Wiltshire district. The exhibition will be on view through Sept. 19. For Beaton, two homes counted: his Wiltshire country estates Ashcombe, where he lived from 1930 to 1945, and Reddish, which he occupied from 1947 until his death. (The 1950s magazine ad, right, was photographed there.) The show’s curator, Andrew Ginger, has recreated parts of two rooms from those houses, installing Beaton’s Circus Bed, complete with unicorns, sea horses and barley-twist posts. Information: salisburymuseum.org.uk. For a total Beaton immersion, head down the road to “Cecil Beaton at Wilton,” a show of his photos from the 1930s, which runs through Sept. 14. Information: tourism@wiltonhouse.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/22/garden/22CURRSHOWS/22CURRSHOWS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 829, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/22/garden/22CURRSHOWS/22CURRSHOWS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 829}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit':