 who has been critical of the White House’s bailout of Wall Street, conducted a heavy-on-debate interview with former Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner about the 2008 financial crisis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If there was one big takeaway from the former Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner’s appearance on “The Daily Show” on Wednesday night, it’s that he and Jon Stewart do not agree on many things about the financial crisis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/22/business/dbpix-dailyshow/dbpix-dailyshow-articleLarge.png', 'height': 339, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/22/business/dbpix-dailyshow/dbpix-dailyshow-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 339}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/22/business/dbpix-dailyshow/dbpix-dailyshow-jumbo.png', 'height': 540, 'width': 957, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/22/business/dbpix-dailyshow/dbpix-dailyshow-superJumbo.png', 'height': 540, 'width': 957, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/22/business/dbpix-dailyshow/dbpix-dailyshow-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/22/business/dbpix-dailyshow/dbpix-dailyshow-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/22/business/dbpix-dailyshow/dbpix-dailyshow-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Geithner and Stewart Don’t See Eye to Eye on ‘The Daily Show’', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-05-22T14:50:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Abrams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Abrams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d7d1939-d918-5026-a978-71710611f333', 'word_count': 363, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d7d1939-d918-5026-a978-71710611f333'}, {'abstract': 'Celebrants at the “Dadabomb” party on Saturday were taking part in the Zurich Meets New York festival.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/22/inspired-by-dada-a-party-for-the-death-of-privacy/', 'snippet': 'Celebrants at the “Dadabomb” party on Saturday were taking part in the Zurich Meets New York festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first sign that something was up was the tombstones that appeared around the city last week, from Times Square to Chelsea to Wall Street, proclaiming: “RIP Privacy.” It turned out that they were teasers for an unusual night of Dadaism that was held Saturday night, the details of which are now becoming known thanks to a video hosted by a puppet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inspired by Dada, a Party for the Death of Privacy', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Giants Are Small', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-22T14:54:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3cab8b29-313e-5c55-81f5-fe2fcbeaba26', 'word_count': 241, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3cab8b29-313e-5c55-81f5-fe2fcbeaba26'}, {'abstract': 'HBO’s version of Larry Kramer’s play “The Normal Heart” has shortcomings, but it benefits from what film can do for a story written for the stage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/23/arts/television/mark-ruffalo-stars-in-larry-kramers-the-normal-heart.html', 'snippet': 'HBO’s version of Larry Kramer’s play “The Normal Heart” has shortcomings, but it benefits from what film can do for a story written for the stage.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Normal Heart,” Larry Kramer’s impassioned j’accuse about the early years of the AIDS crisis, is an entirely different thing from what it was when first staged as a play at the Public Theater in 1985. Back then, it was, above all, a political statement about continuing indifference toward the gathering storm of AIDS; those strident speeches that the central character, Ned Weeks, delivered onstage could just as appropriately have been shouted on the sidewalks outside City Hall or the White House.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/23/arts/23NORMAL/23NORMAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/23/arts/23NORMAL/23NORMAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/23/arts/23NORMAL/23NORMAL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'wi