imit-nsas-collection-of-phone-data.html', 'snippet': 'Republicans and Democrats agreed to curb the National Security Agency’s bulk phone data collection, but the bipartisanship did not extend to closing the Guantánamo prison or cutting military spending.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — In a rare moment of bipartisan agreement between the White House and Congress on a major national security issue, the House passed legislation on Thursday that aims to end the National Security Agency’s bulk phone records program that had prompted intense domestic debate about privacy and civil liberties. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/23/us/23CONG2/23CONG2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 393, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/23/us/23CONG2/23CONG2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 393}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/23/us/23CONG2/23CONG2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 670, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/23/us/23CONG2/23CONG2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1341, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/23/us/23CONG2/23CONG2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/23/us/23CONG2/23CONG2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/23/us/23CONG2/23CONG2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'House Passes Restraints on Bulk Data Collection', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'House Passes Restraints on N.S.A.’s Data Mining', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Security Agency', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Savage, Charlie', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data-Mining and Database Marketing', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-22T16:32:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Weisman and Charlie Savage', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weisman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Charlie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Savage', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d713043-cb12-5197-8e55-563cba64c0b3', 'word_count': 1002, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d713043-cb12-5197-8e55-563cba64c0b3'}, {'abstract': 'This season’s scores were clearly influenced by numerous musical styles, including country, gospel, jazz and the folk songs of other nations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/25/theater/eclectic-scores-in-the-seasons-broadway-crop.html', 'snippet': 'This season’s scores were clearly influenced by numerous musical styles, including country, gospel, jazz and the folk songs of other nations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The score for the Broadway musical “A Gentleman’s Guide to Love & Murder” has been described as a pastiche of Gilbert and Sullivan operettas. Songs from Jeanine Tesori’s “Violet,” starring the amazing Sutton Foster, like the jaunty “Surprised,” owe an obvious debt to country and gospel.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/arts/25SCORES4/25SCORES4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/25/arts/25SCORES4/25SCORES4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 405}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/arts/25SCORES4/25SCORES4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 692, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/arts/25SCORES4/25SCORES4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1383, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/arts/25SCORES4/25SCORES4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/25/arts/25SCORES4/25SCORES4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/arts/25SCORES4/25SCORES4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pastiche, Parody, Homage and Theft', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pastiche, Parody, Homage and Crimes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tommasini, Anthony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tony Awards (Theater Awards)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-22T16:35:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c29be0e-5da0-590f-9e29-f47f59ea136a', 'word_count': 1540, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c29be0e-5da0-590f-9e29-f47f59ea136a'}, {'abstract': 'Anthony Tommasini on this season’s musical theater songs that stood out and that have potential for independent afterlives.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/22/a-critic-picks-songs-of-the-broadway-season/', 'snippet': 'Anthony Tommasini on this season’s musical theater songs that stood out and that have potential for independent afterlives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As someone who thinks a lot about the musical component of musical theater, I wrote an Arts & Leisure piece offering my impressions of some scores from recent shows in New York. I was particularly interested in the technique of pastiche, in which composers, and lyricists, too, evoke older song styles and genres in their new scores. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Critic Picks Songs of the Broadway Season', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Jason Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tesori, Jeanine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-22T16:41:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d81c3984-3be8-5a13-a411-34bde4bceade', 'word_count': 199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d81c3984-3be8-5a13-a411-34bde4bceade'}, {'abstract': 'For the Million Death Study, researchers are interviewing families that lost loved ones at an untimely age to look for explanations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/23/world/asia/chasing-down-death-india-seeks-answers-on-premature-mortality.html', 'snippet': 'For the Million Death Study, researchers are interviewing families that lost loved ones at an untimely age to look for explanations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MAGADI, India — M. R. Gundappa, 60, died the way most Indians do: with no doctor present, no monitors beeping by his side and no written record. The only person present was his wife, Sushilamma, 48, who spent the day of his death trying to get him admitted to a government hospital where he could be treated for abdominal pain.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/22/world/INDIA/INDIA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/