', 'word_count': 865, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40c26b7e-ea1c-5015-b54f-b742ca4d9fd0'}, {'abstract': 'A Korean ruler known as the king of misfortune faces enemies at every turn in “The Fatal Encounter.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/23/movies/the-fatal-encounter-a-tale-of-the-joseon-dynasty.html', 'snippet': 'A Korean ruler known as the king of misfortune faces enemies at every turn in “The Fatal Encounter.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The title of Lee Jae-kyoo’s “The Fatal Encounter” is a misnomer: The film teems with fatal encounters. It is 1777, in the Joseon dynasty in Korea, and King Jeong-jo (Hyun Bin) — known historically as the king of misfortune — is surrounded by enemies. Two factions, the Noron and Soron, are at each other’s throats, and everyone, it seems, has it in for him. Even his trusted clerk, Sang-chaek (an effectively understated Jung Jae-young), a quiet minder of this philosopher-king’s library, was trained in childhood at a kind of assassin school for slaves. He has positioned himself to strike but discovers that the monarch is a benign ruler and a friend.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/23/arts/23FATAL1/FATAL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/23/arts/23FATAL1/FATAL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/23/arts/23FATAL1/FATAL1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/23/arts/23FATAL1/FATAL1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo'