ct': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/25/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FLORA,  by Gail Godwin. (Bloomsbury, $16.) It’s the summer of 1945, and the still, humid days are fraught with impending consequence in Godwin’s 14th novel. Isolated in a decaying family home in North Carolina, two lonesome souls — motherless, 10-year-old Helen and her hopelessly earnest guardian, Flora — wait out a polio scare while Helen’s father is off doing secret war work in Oak Ridge, Tenn.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-23T14:26:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/b33c9de1-a6b0-5bc2-bb21-9106cdc89427', 'word_count': 475, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b33c9de1-a6b0-5bc2-bb21-9106cdc89427'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/25/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE POSSIBILITIES,  by Kaui Hart Hemmings. (Simon & Schuster, $25.) Hemmings’s first novel since “The Descendants” is about learning to move forward, as a Colorado woman struggles with grief over her son’s death in an avalanche.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-23T14:26:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/2be39078-18d5-513f-be17-b591cee73223', 'word_count': 272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2be39078-18d5-513f-be17-b591cee73223'}, {'abstract': 'A 40-something rock singer looks to revive a career she had long abandoned.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/25/books/review/wonderland-by-stacey-derasmo.html', 'snippet': 'A 40-something rock singer looks to revive a career she had long abandoned.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Much like smell (it’s all very well to say that something smells “like an orange,” but how do you describe the smell of an orange?), music is notoriously difficult to evoke on the page. The fact that language has musical attributes — rhythm, melody, tone — isn’t much help when you’re trying to express Bartok or Captain Beefheart in words. Even when music has lyrics, its essence is antilanguage, or at least in another language — and an inability to translate explains why so many album and concert reviews are unreadable. Thus Stacey D’Erasmo’s most impressive accomplishment in her fourth novel, “Wonderland,” is to write about music well.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/0525-bks-Shriver/0525-bks-Shriver-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 639, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/0525-bks-Shriver/0525-bks-Shriver-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 639}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/0525-bks-Shriver/0525-bks-Shriver-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 962, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/0525-bks-Shriver/0525-bks-Shriver-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/0525-bks-Shriver/0525-bks-Shriver-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/0525-bks-Shriver/0525-bks-Shriver-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/0525-bks-Shriver/0525-bks-Shriver-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Road Again', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On the Road Again', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shriver, Lionel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "D'Erasmo, Stacey", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-23T14:27:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lionel Shriver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lionel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shriver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e512ec20-fedf-5047-8c10-504c231e8dc8', 'word_count': 1204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e512ec20-fedf-5047-8c10-504c231e8dc8'}, {'abstract': 'In a change of pace, Edward St. Aubyn’s novel satirizes the awarding of a British literary prize.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/25/books/review/lost-for-words-by-edward-st-aubyn.html', 'snippet': 'In a change of pace, Edward St. Aubyn’s novel satirizes the awarding of a British literary prize.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Edward St. Aubyn is that thing beloved by heritage series from the BBC, a man of ancient and aristocratic pedigree. He writes of an upper-class world so central to the English novel of the 19th and early 20th centuries, it has shaped the modern imagination: You might think we all sprang from big houses with handmade wallpaper and quietly smoldering servants. St. Aubyn cuts through the fantasy of privilege, however, with lacerating precision; once you have read his Melrose cycle of novels, there is no going back. These books — “Never Mind,” “Bad News,” “Some Hope,” “Mother’s Milk” and “At Last” — set a marker in the English tradition by changing our view of what preceded them. The narrator, Patrick Melrose, is raped at the age of 5 by his sadistic, patrician father and later partially disinherited by his cold and foolish mother. After Melrose, the aristocratic novel, from Richardson’s swooning “Pamela” to the affectless cruelties of Waugh, can never look the same again. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/25ENRIGHT/25ENRIGHT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 709, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/25ENRIGHT/25ENRIGHT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 709}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/25ENRIGHT/25ENRIGHT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 868, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/25ENRIGHT/25ENRIGHT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1991, 'width': 1686, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/25ENRIGHT/25ENRIGHT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/25ENRIGHT/25ENRIGHT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/25ENRIGHT/25ENRIGHT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'By the Booker', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'By the Booker', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'St Aubyn, Edward', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-23T14:27:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anne Enright', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Enright', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d18ca178-9de5-50fd-85a7-a368cde4c410', 'word_count': 1202, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d18ca178-9de5-50fd-85a7-a368cde4c410'}, {'abstract': 'Some humiliations experienced by writers are more in-your-face than others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/25/books/review/please-turn-to-the-chapter-on-obscurity.html', 'snippet': 'Some humiliations experienced by writers are more in-your-face than others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The woman at the counter in Barnes & Noble had silver hair in a bun and a smile like breakfast. I was with a friend, for whom I was buying a copy of my latest book. I do this often — buy my own books for friends, then wonder why I’m broke. “May I take a moment to sign it?” I asked the smiling woman. “Did you write it?” she asked. “No,” I said. “I just like signing books.” Unmoved, she asked, “Is this your first book?” I shook my head no, and continued inscribing. “It must be fun to write books,” she said. “Superfun,” I said.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/0525-bks-Rosenblatt/0525-bks-Rosenblatt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 310, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/0525-bks-Rosenblatt/0525-bks-Rosenblatt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 310}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/0525-bks-Rosenblatt/0525-bks-Rosenblatt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 529, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/0525-bks-Rosenblatt/0525-bks-Rosenblatt-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1058, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 's