, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koch, Charles G', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schulman, Daniel', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-23T14:34:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Lemann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lemann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3013cfcf-6728-52a4-80a2-22d273c3a3f2', 'word_count': 1442, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3013cfcf-6728-52a4-80a2-22d273c3a3f2'}, {'abstract': 'Behind the scenes at a fictional restaurant with a sadistic chef.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/25/books/review/chop-chop-by-simon-wroe.html', 'snippet': 'Behind the scenes at a fictional restaurant with a sadistic chef.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At its best, food is a sensory pleasure that also fosters less tangible joys. At its worst, to paraphrase one of the many vivid characters in Simon Wroe’s first novel, “Chop Chop,” watching someone eat is like watching a body convert food to waste right in front of your eyes. The character phrases it less delicately, but many of the book’s funniest moments — and they are plentiful — are also its most unprintable.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/05325-bks-Wildgen/05325-bks-Wildgen-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 250, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/05325-bks-Wildgen/05325-bks-Wildgen-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 250}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'u