its relationship to an often censored history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the night of June 3-4, 1989, when the Chinese Army was slaughtering demonstrators in Tiananmen Square, Wang Nan, a young student, was shot in the head. As he lay dying at the side of the road, soldiers threatened to kill anyone, even some young doctors, who tried to help him. In the morning, finally dead, he was buried in a shallow grave nearby. A few days later, the smell of Wang Nan’s body was so great that it was dug up and moved to a hospital. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/25MIRSKY/25MIRSKY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 511, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/25MIRSKY/25MIRSKY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 511}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/25MIRSKY/25MIRSKY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 871, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/25MIRSKY/25MIRSKY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1743, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/25MIRSKY/25MIRSKY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/25MIRSKY/25MIRSKY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/25MIRSKY/25MIRSKY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Inconvenient Past', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Inconvenient Past', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Osnos, Evan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lim, Louisa', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tiananmen Square (Beijing)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-23T14:43:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Mirsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mirsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6197cb55-056b-5de8-94e9-f43f531fe562', 'word_count': 1242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6197cb55-056b-5de8-94e9-f43f531fe562'}, {'abstract': 'What happens when a young American journalist falls in love with a Libyan émigré.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/25/books/review/my-accidental-jihad-by-krista-bremer.html', 'snippet': 'What happens when a young American journalist falls in love with a Libyan émigré.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s a scene in “My Accidental Jihad” in which Krista Bremer visits her husband’s relatives in Libya and sees a wheezing, skeletal old woman lying on a hospital bed in their living room.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Foreign Affair', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Foreign Affair', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Druckerman, Pamela', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bremer, Krista', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-23T14:43:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pamela Druckerman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pamela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Druckerman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d88aa77-a49f-5320-b6d6-0e6db96a6413', 'word_count': 664, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d88aa77-a49f-5320-b6d6-0e6db96a6413'}, {'abstract': 'Jeffery Deaver, whose thriller “The Skin Collector” is No. 8 on the hardcover fiction list, breeds show dogs and once passed up dinner with Angelina Jolie to tend to a dog’s matted coat.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/01/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Jeffery Deaver, whose thriller “The Skin Collector” is No. 8 on the hardcover fiction list, breeds show dogs and once passed up dinner with Angelina Jolie to tend to a dog’s matted coat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dog Days: Jeffery Deaver’s new Lincoln Rhyme thriller, “The Skin Collector,” makes its debut on the hardcover fiction list at No. 8. Part of the perverse allure of this series — which now numbers some dozen books — has always come from the demented nature of its villains, and “The Skin Collector” is no exception: It features a \xadserial-killing tattoo artist who makes his own ink out of a slow-acting and painful poison. But a person can spend only so many hours a day inventing gruesome murder schemes, and one of the ways Deaver likes to unwind is by breeding show dogs with a business partner near his North Carolina home. “I’m not as creepy as the things I write about,” he told The Associated Press in 2007. That doesn’t mean you can’t question his priorities — he once passed up dinner with Angelina Jolie to tend to a dog’s matted coat. But Deaver knows what he’s doing: His briards have won Best of Breed more than once at the Westminster Kennel Club show in New York. “Your heart’s pounding,” he told a reporter in 2005, comparing the experience to that of a father watching a Little League game. “You’ll still love the dog just as much if he doesn’t win. But I can’t help it. I hope he wins.” Given the elaborate plot twists and psychological head games in his novels, is Deaver ever tempted to mess with his dogs’ minds, just a little? “I couldn’t possibly do that,” he told The Sun Herald newspaper in Australia in 2006. “The dogs are much cleverer than me.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/25list-deaver/25list-deaver-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/25list-deaver/25list-deaver-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 454, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/25list-deaver/25list-deaver-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/25list-deaver/25list-deaver-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/books/review/25list-deaver/25list-deaver-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 