me': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sports Utility Vehicles and Light Trucks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-23T18:37:33+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Automobiles', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'Tom Voelk/Driven', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': 'Voelk', 'lastname': 'Driven', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/af1dae72-4d33-56e5-971a-8a57c9ca029d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/af1dae72-4d33-56e5-971a-8a57c9ca029d'}, {'abstract': 'Ben Ratliff and Jon Caramanica discuss the increasing overlap between country music and rap.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/23/popcast-country-embraces-hip-hop/', 'snippet': 'Ben Ratliff and Jon Caramanica discuss the increasing overlap between country music and rap.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On this week’s Popcast: the slow and circuitous loosening of modern country music. The perpetual story of the country music industry, our critic Jon Caramanica explains, is always how well it polices its own borders, how it maintain its core identity and resists assimilation into the rest of popular culture. But his cover story in Arts and Leisure this weekend analyzes how things may be decisively shifting right now, especially around rapping in country songs. The power of hip-hop reached critical mass across the country a long time ago; there are, he writes, “a generation of stars — mostly in their mid-late 30s, old enough to have grown up with hip-hop as a fact of life, even in the small towns they hail from, who think nothing of sprinkling a little bit of it into their music.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/arts/25nashville-blog/25JPNASHV