+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom Voelk', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Voelk', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c84f2f58-77f4-5050-a716-2452a7716f00', 'word_count': 626, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c84f2f58-77f4-5050-a716-2452a7716f00'}, {'abstract': 'A recent survey suggests that endorsements matter most when ideology and issues don’t distinguish rival candidates.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/24/upshot/when-do-political-endorsements-matter.html', 'snippet': 'A recent survey suggests that endorsements matter most when ideology and issues don’t distinguish rival candidates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Voters are generally quick to acknowledge to pollsters that candidates’ stances on issues influence their votes much more than candidates’ personal attributes. However, the impact of political endorsements, pollsters have found, is more ambiguous.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/24/upshot/24UP-Endorse/24UP-Endorse-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/24/upshot/24UP-Endorse/24UP-Endorse-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/24/upshot/24UP-Endorse/24UP-Endorse-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/24/upshot/24UP-Endorse/24UP-Endorse-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop