view-of-exposed-eadweard-muybridge-and-the-study-of-motion-in-huntington.html', 'snippet': 'An exhibition at the Heckscher Museum of Art in Huntington shows some of an influential 19th-century photographer’s prints from his project “Animal Locomotion.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the prospectus accompanying “Animal Locomotion” (1887), an 11-volume collection of 20,000 photographs documenting human and animal movement, Eadweard Muybridge described how he became interested in the subject in the 1870s and was convinced that he could “demonstrate many novel facts, not only interesting to the casual observer, but of indisputable value to the Artist and to the Scientist.” ', 'print_section': 'LI', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/nyregion/25ARTSLI_SPAN/5ARTSLI1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 528, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/25/nyregion/25ARTSLI_SPAN/5ARTSLI1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 528}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/nyregion/25ARTSLI_SPAN/5ARTSLI1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 901, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/nyregion/25ARTSLI_SPAN/5ARTSLI1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1803, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/nyregion/25ARTSLI_SPAN/5ARTSLI1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/25/nyregion/25ARTSLI_SPAN/5ARTSLI1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 't