reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ab7ea0e-7b67-54c8-b026-4f8fb9d29a10', 'word_count': 940, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ab7ea0e-7b67-54c8-b026-4f8fb9d29a10'}, {'abstract': 'It will take a concerted effort to ensure that drug treatments are tested in groups that reflect the patients who will be using them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/24/opinion/gender-imbalance-in-the-lab.html', 'snippet': 'It will take a concerted effort to ensure that drug treatments are tested in groups that reflect the patients who will be using them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Federal health officials are taking steps to correct a longstanding gender imbalance in laboratory research on potential drug treatments. For the past two decades, federal law has required that women be included in clinical studies funded by the National Institutes of Health in recognition of the fact that drugs often have vastly different effects in men and women. As a result, more than half of the participants in N.I.H.-funded clinical research are women. But there has been no similar requirement to eliminate the sex bias from more basic laboratory research that can lead up to a clinical trial or doom a drug to the discard pile if it seems not to work.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gender Imbalance in the Lab', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gender Imbalance in the Lab', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gender', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Clinical Trials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Institutes of Health', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-24T01:17:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/345b567d-85cc-5260-be74-6e9d0f8b6c93', 'word_count': 345, 'uri': 'nyt://article/345b567d-85cc-5260-be74-6e9d0f8b6c93'}, {'abstract': 'A bid to buy Britain’s AstraZeneca is in large part an all-too-common corporate exercise in tax avoidance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/24/opinion/pfizers-ploy-and-the-porous-tax-laws.html', 'snippet': 'A bid to buy Britain’s AstraZeneca is in large part an all-too-common corporate exercise in tax avoidance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pfizer’s $119 billion offer to buy AstraZeneca, the British drug company, is driven largely by its desire to cut its tax bill. It is a ploy made possible by porous tax laws in the United States that encourage corporate tax avoidance and by laws in Britain and elsewhere that abet it.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pfizer’s Ploy and the Porous Tax Laws', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pfizer’s Ploy and the Porous Tax Laws', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporate Taxes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AstraZeneca PLC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pfizer Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Shelters', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-24T01:19:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4a8ba64-6d1b-58ea-9224-1681f1c956bf', 'word_count': 580, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4a8ba64-6d1b-58ea-9224-1681f1c956bf'}, {'abstract': 'In 1950, Jacqueline Bouvier began an extraordinary correspondence with an Irish priest in which she revealed her private thoughts as she dated, then married, then witnessed the assassination of the president of the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/24/us/jackie-kennedys-letters-taken-off-the-auction-block.html', 'snippet': 'In 1950, Jacqueline Bouvier began an extraordinary correspondence with an Irish priest in which she revealed her private thoughts as she dated, then married, then witnessed the assassination of the president of the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — It was an unlikely friendship, between a shy, 21-year-old American socialite and a 73-year-old Irish priest. But in 1950, they began an extraordinary correspondence in which the American revealed her private thoughts as she dated, then married, then witnessed the assassination of the president of the United States. Now the fate of their letters, which had been headed for the auction block, is in question.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/24/us/LETTERS/LETTERS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 842, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/24/us/LETTERS/LETTERS-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 842}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/24/us/LETTERS/LETTERS-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width