, 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-05-24T04:48:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95d1f9ac-142f-5dcb-b9e8-09cce630e0f4', 'word_count': 449, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95d1f9ac-142f-5dcb-b9e8-09cce630e0f4'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/24/arts/television/whats-on-saturday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '10 P.M. (Cinemax) THE WORLD’S END (2013) In the summer of 1990, five British teenagers set out on a pub crawl in the town of Newton Haven, vomiting, fighting and having drunken sex along what is known as the Golden Mile. But they failed to make it through the 12 requisite watering holes, the last of which was called the World’s End. Twenty years later, they give it a second try in this comedy directed by Edgar Wright, the final installment in his Cornetto Trilogy, following “Shaun of the Dead” and “Hot Fuzz.” Nick Frost, Simon Pegg and Paddy Considine, along with Martin Freeman and Eddie Marsan, play the Golden Milers. Four have traded their wild youth for adult respectability; the fifth decidedly has not. Then the robots show up, and these men just may be humanity’s only hope for survival. “The Cornetto Trilogy is named after a popular ice cream treat, and the buzz of ‘The World’s End’ is more like an antic sugar high than a reeling, drunken stupor,” A. O. Scott wrote in The New York Times. “There are no headaches, dry mouth or crushing shame at the end — no ‘Hangover,’ in other words. I’ll drink to that.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Saturday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Saturday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-05-24T05:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/55762904-7468-5640-8078-ed0c7cdbcbee', 'word_count': 740, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55762904-7468-5640-8078-ed0c7cdbcbee'}, {'abstract': 'The invitation by Narendra Modi, India’s new prime minister, indicated a possible upturn in relations between the two longtime rivals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/25/world/asia/in-possible-thaw-pakistani-premier-to-attend-modis-swearing-in.html', 'snippet': 'The invitation by Narendra Modi, India’s new prime minister, indicated a possible upturn in relations between the two longtime rivals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW DELHI — Ending days of hesitation, Pakistan’s prime minister, Nawaz Sharif, on Saturday accepted an Indian invitation to attend the swearing-in of the new prime minister, Narendra Modi, offering a fresh opportunity to revitalize the moribund peace process between the two countries, which have had particularly frosty relations since early 2013.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/24/world/asia/25INDIA/25INDIA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 381, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/24/world/asia/25INDIA/25INDIA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 381}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/24/world/asia/25INDIA/25INDIA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 651, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumb