w.nytimes.com/2014/05/25/sunday-review/deterrence-revisited.html', 'snippet': 'Do our adversaries feel free to ignore the rules of global order?', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — THE graduation exercises at West Point have long been a moment for American presidents to offer midcourse adjustments — or something more radical — in their strategies for dealing with foreign adversaries. A dozen years ago, George W. Bush offered the first taste of his “pre-emption” strategy from the bluffs over the Hudson River, reviving a 165-year-old concept to warn anyone who might threaten the United States that Washington could act first to neutralize them. It was more than a rhetorical exercise: 10 months later American forces were on their way to Baghdad. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/sunday-review/0525sanger/0525sanger-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/25/sunday-review/0525sanger/0525sanger-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 395}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/sunday-review/0525sanger/0525sanger-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 674, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/sunday-review/0525sanger/0525sanger-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1347, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/sunday-review/0525sanger/0525sanger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/25/sunday-review/0525sanger/0525sanger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/sunday-review/0525sanger/0525sanger-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Deterrence Revisited', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Deterrence Revisited', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drones (Pilotless Planes)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL QAEDA', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Commencement Speeches', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-24T18:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David E. Sanger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Sanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/aad820a6-b624-5d88-977e-27ff5189f4ef', 'word_count': 1038, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aad820a6-b624-5d88-977e-27ff5189f4ef'}, {'abstract': 'Applications from transgender students and a Title IX ruling are forcing women’s colleges to reconsider admissions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/25/opinion/sunday/who-are-womens-colleges-for.html', 'snippet': 'Applications from transgender students and a Title IX ruling are forcing women’s colleges to reconsider admissions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR hundreds of years, universities excluded women. Denied access to these institutions, they created their own. “Attempt great things,” the founder of Mount Holyoke, Mary Lyon, told her students. “Accomplish great things.” These schools, including the elite Seven Sisters — Mount Holyoke, Barnard, Bryn Mawr, Radcliffe, Smith, Vassar and Wellesley — were where the nation’s most promising young women went to do just that. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/sunday-review/25transgender/25transgender-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 422, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/25/sunday-review/25transgender/25transgender-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 422}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/sunday-review/25transgender/25transgender-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/sunday-review/25transgender/25transgender-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1440, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/sunday-review/25transgender/25transgender-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/25/sunday-review/25transgender/25transgender-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/25/sunday-review/25transgender/25transgender-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 15