ws_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kirk Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kirk', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60b19969-6ce6-52aa-8ba7-c5676036a46d', 'word_count': 916, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60b19969-6ce6-52aa-8ba7-c5676036a46d'}, {'abstract': 'Think mapping data is new? The Union Army was doing it in Louisiana 150 years ago.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/25/a-mysterious-map-of-louisiana/', 'snippet': 'Think mapping data is new? The Union Army was doing it in Louisiana 150 years ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'These days the intersection of cartography and Big Data is all the rage: Using information from the 2010 census, countless news outfits, including The New York Times, have created tools allowing readers to make customized maps of everything from trends in ethnic and racial composition to the dynamics of housing development. Indeed, we have come to expect that any large body of data will be visualized through maps and infographics. Such tools help to transform information into knowledge, and at their best allow us to see patterns that might otherwise be lost.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/27/opinion/27louisiana-zoom/27louisiana-zoom-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 412, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/27/opinion/27louisiana-zoom/27louisiana-zoom-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 412, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/27/opini