inal': 'By Rachel L. Swarns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Swarns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/760bcdf1-6794-5d60-aa33-6c652ce8a266', 'word_count': 790, 'uri': 'nyt://article/760bcdf1-6794-5d60-aa33-6c652ce8a266'}, {'abstract': 'A spat over the shooting of a talk show host, followed by the government’s reversal of a ban on the station, has exposed tensions between Prime Minister Nawaz Sharif and top officials.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/26/world/asia/press-battle-in-pakistan-feeds-into-larger-conflict-government-vs-military.html', 'snippet': 'A spat over the shooting of a talk show host, followed by the government’s reversal of a ban on the station, has exposed tensions between Prime Minister Nawaz Sharif and top officials.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — For a moment, it seemed that a victor had emerged from Pakistan’s news media wars. After a month of mud-slinging between rival television stations, officials from the government media regulator announced last week that Geo News, the country’s most popular news channel, would immediately be closed.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/26/world/PAKISTAN/PAKISTAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/26/world/PAKISTAN/PAKISTAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/26/world/PAKISTAN/PAKISTAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None