: 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-27T04:12:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Keh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f355caeb-c5c0-5391-8627-289824eda996', 'word_count': 1054, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f355caeb-c5c0-5391-8627-289824eda996'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/27/arts/television/whats-on-tuesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (HBO) THE HEAT (2013) An uppity F.B.I. hotshot (Sandra Bullock) is assigned to work with a loose-cannon Boston detective (Melissa McCarthy, above left, with Ms. Bullock) — the better to temper personality traits that her boss (Demian Bichir) believes are standing in the way of a promotion at the agency — in this comedy directed by Paul Feig (“Bridesmaids”). And voilà! A female version of Felix and Oscar, or Bateman and Galifianakis, is born. “The story is sloppy and thin, many of the jokes are strained or tired, and the level of violence is a bit jarring,” A. O. Scott wrote in The New York Times. “But the volatile chemistry between Ms. McCarthy and Ms. Bullock is something to behold, and carries ‘The Heat’ through its lazy conception and slapdash execution.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Tuesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Tuesday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-05-27T04:13:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Tele