 Garner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dwight', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf83e244-a0e6-5c73-9cff-ad219a5878cb', 'word_count': 1131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf83e244-a0e6-5c73-9cff-ad219a5878cb'}, {'abstract': 'The status of the Rangers’ Derek Stepan, whose jaw was broken on a late hit in Game 3 and who missed Game 4, remained a game-time decision for Game 5 against the Canadiens on Tuesday night.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/28/sports/hockey/rangers-derek-stepan-practices-before-game-5.html', 'snippet': 'The status of the Rangers’ Derek Stepan, whose jaw was broken on a late hit in Game 3 and who missed Game 4, remained a game-time decision for Game 5 against the Canadiens on Tuesday night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONTREAL — Derek Stepan, wearing a full plexiglass face shield to protect his broken jaw, returned to practice with the Rangers on Tuesday morning at Bell Centre before Game 5 of the N.H.L.’s Eastern Conference finals. Stepan then returned to the ice after the team’s medical staff gave him the go-ahead to play in the game.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stepan Returns to Lineup And Has Immediate Effect ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stepan Returns to Lineup And Has Immediate Effect ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Rangers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Montreal Canadiens', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stepan, Derek', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Price, Carey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-27T18:34:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Z. Klein and Ben Shpigel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': 'Z.', 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shpigel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ed1d264-c86b-5594-92a5-032d52da9783', 'word_count': 857, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ed1d264-c86b-5594-92a5-032d52da9783'}, {'abstract': 'We recently added read-only comments to our iPhone and iPad apps. We have been striving to add as much test coverage as we can to these pods, and the comments pod was no exception. We’ve detailed the approach we took while writing tests for the comments pod.', 'web_url': 'https://open.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/27/unit-testing-with-block-captures/', 'snippet': 'We recently added read-only comments to our iPhone and iPad apps. We have been striving to add as much test coverage as we can to these pods, and the comments pod was no exception. We’ve detailed the approach we took while writing tests for the co...', 'lead_paragraph': 'We recently added read-only comments to our iPhone and iPad apps. We have started writing features that can be shared across our apps as CocoaPods, and the comments feature was an excellent candidate for a pod. Going forward, all of our iOS apps will be able to use this feature. We have been striving to add as much test coverage as we can to these pods, and the comments pod was no exception. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Unit Testing With Block Captures', 'kicker': 'Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-05-27T18:36:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mohit Pandey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mohit', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pandey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/12e320a6-903d-5562-a7ae-2aeabf440a26', 'word_count': 1077, 'uri': 'nyt://article/12e320a6-903d-5562-a7ae-2aeabf440a26'}, {'abstract': 'A book review kicks up a storm about the role of the press in a democracy.', 'web_url': 'https://publiceditor.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/27/kinsley-greenwald-and-government-secrets/', 'snippet': 'A book review kicks up a storm about the role of the press in a democracy.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Updated, 9:22 p.m. Michael Kinsley’s review of Glenn Greenwald’s new book, “No Place to Hide” hasn’t even appeared in the printed Book Review yet – that won’t happen until June 8 – but it’s already infuriated a lot of people. After the review was published online last week, many commenters and readers (and Mr. Greenwald himself) attacked the review, which was not only negative about the book but also expressed a belief that many journalists find appalling: that news organizations should simply defer to the government when it comes to deciding what the public has a right to know about its secret activities. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kinsley, Greenwald and Government Secrets', 'kicker': 'The Public Editor&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-05-27T18:45:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaret Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_mater