 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c03366d3-ff2d-51d3-8a73-3a9f0a486e0d', 'word_count': 954, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c03366d3-ff2d-51d3-8a73-3a9f0a486e0d'}, {'abstract': 'How online dating explains the truth about the digital economy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/01/magazine/who-wants-free-love-anyway.html', 'snippet': 'How online dating explains the truth about the digital economy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Misty Terrell turned 28, she happened to see an ad for a special deal on the dating site eHarmony\xad and decided it was time to get serious about her love life. Terrell felt pretty optimistic. The site claims responsibility for 542 marriages a day through its “scientific approach” to finding soul mates: an exhaustive questionnaire, the trademarked “29 dimensions of compatibility” algorithm and its clinical labs where psychologists spend hours analyzing couple interactions. For this sort of comprehensive matchmaking, the company charges $60 a month, which is far more than most dating sites, but perhaps something of a bargain when it comes to finding true love. Terrell signed up to receive five potential matches a day for six months.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/magazine/01economy/mag-01Economy-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 689, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/01/magazine/01economy/mag-01Economy-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 689}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/magazine/01economy/mag-01Economy-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 893, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'ju