name': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c9f4f28-064b-5ff9-945d-ccd0e4c544c8', 'word_count': 616, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c9f4f28-064b-5ff9-945d-ccd0e4c544c8'}, {'abstract': '“Watch Dogs,” an adventure game from Ubisoft, revolves around a protagonist looking for revenge in a cityscape that resembles Chicago.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/28/arts/video-games/watch-dogs-an-adventure-game-from-ubisoft.html', 'snippet': '“Watch Dogs,” an adventure game from Ubisoft, revolves around a protagonist looking for revenge in a cityscape that resembles Chicago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the pantheon of lawbreaking folk heroes in American culture, alongside the mobster and the Wild West gunslinger, we can add the computer hacker. Matthew Broderick’s character in the 1983 movie “WarGames” may have been Patient Zero for this strain, followed by figures from William Gibson’s cyberpunk novels, “The Matrix” and a parade of forgettable 1990s Hollywood thrillers. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/28/arts/28DOGS/28DOGS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 351, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/28/arts/28DOGS/28DOGS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 351}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/28/arts/28DOGS/28DOGS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/28/arts/28DOGS/28DOGS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1199, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo