, 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cacciola', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9150de5d-d5da-541b-9d3d-7170bdc2fd56', 'word_count': 1321, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9150de5d-d5da-541b-9d3d-7170bdc2fd56'}, {'abstract': 'Betting on breakthroughs, or even positive developments, is always perilous in the Middle East, but Francis exhibited a deft touch during his visit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/28/world/middleeast/for-middle-east-region-of-religious-conflict-pope-suggests-a-respite-in-prayer.html', 'snippet': 'Betting on breakthroughs, or even positive developments, is always perilous in the Middle East, but Francis exhibited a deft touch during his visit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERUSALEM — Pope Francis returned home from his three-day sojourn in the Holy Land with the typical bag of ceremonial gifts, including, from the children of Bethlehem’s refugee camps, a mock-up of an identification card in the name of Jesus that lists family members as Mohandas K. Gandhi, Nelson Mandela, Yasir Arafat and Martin Luther King Jr. But the pope also carried with him a promise from the Israeli and Palestinian presidents to come soon to the Vatican for a joint peace prayer, an unprecedented effort to lend a spiritual lift to a desperate political stalemate.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/28/world/SUB-MIDEAST/SUB-MIDEAST-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/28/world/SUB-MIDEAST/SUB-MIDEAST-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/28/world/SUB-MIDEAST/SUB-MIDEAST-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/28/world/SUB-MIDEAST/SUB-MIDEAST-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/28/world/SUB-MIDEAST/SUB-MIDEAST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/28/world/SUB-MIDEAST/SUB-MIDEAST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/28/world/SUB-MIDEAST/SUB-MIDEAST-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Middle East, Region of Religious Conflict, Pope Suggests a Respite in Prayer', 'kicker': 'Memo From Jerusalem', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For Region of Religious Conflict, Pope Suggests a Respite in Prayer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Francis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rudoren, Jodi', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Middle East', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Palestinian Authority', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABBAS, MAHMOUD', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Peres, Shimon', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Netanyahu, Benjamin', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-28T00:56:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jodi Rudoren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jodi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rudoren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0a6bce1-509b-56ab-858b-3ed3454a9104', 'word_count': 1173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0a6bce1-509b-56ab-858b-3ed3454a9104'}, {'abstract': 'Double-digit increases cannot go on forever, and many economists are using words like “sustainable” and “stable” to describe the slowdown, saying the market is becoming healthier.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/28/business/economy/home-prices-start-easing-to-the-relief-of-experts.html', 'snippet': 'Double-digit increases cannot go on forever, and many economists are using words like “sustainable” and “stable” to describe the slowdown, saying the market is becoming healthier.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A steep gain in home prices in many markets that helped lift millions of Americans out of the red on their mortgages is now markedly slowing, with new data from the Standard & Poor’s/Case-Shiller national home price index on Tuesday showing that the annual growth in prices had eased in March to 10.3 percent, from the previous year’s increase of 11.4 percent.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Home Prices Start Easing, to the Relief of Experts', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Home Prices Rising Less, to the Relief of Experts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dewan, Shaila', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Standard & Poor's Case-Shiller Home Price Index", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-28T00:58:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shaila Dewan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shaila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dewan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd4da79a-9eed-5488-81a6-a0e52992f979', 'word_count': 856, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd4da79a-9eed-5488-81a6-a0e52992f979'}, {'abstract': 'A long line of academic research, comparing otherwise similar people, finds that those who graduate from college do better.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/28/upshot/the-value-of-college-its-not-just-correlation.html', 'snippet': 'A long line of academic research, comparing otherwise similar people, finds that those who graduate from college do better.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Comparing the earnings of college graduates and nongraduates is an imperfect way of measuring the value of a degree, as I noted in my Tuesday column. Hypothetically, college graduates could earn more because they’re innately smarter — and would have earned just as much if they had never attended college or even any school. As a reader named Shawn posted in the comments section of the column, “The useful comparison is not between those who do and do not attend college but, rather, between equally intelligent students who do and do not choose to attend college.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/28/upshot/28up-Correlate/28up-Correlate-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/28/upshot/28up-Correlate/28up-Correlate-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/28/upshot/28up-Correlate/28up-Correlate-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/28/upshot/28up-Correlate/28up-Correlate-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/28/upshot/28up-Correlate/28up-Correlate-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/28/upshot/28up-Correlate/28up-Correlate-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/28/upshot/28up-Correlate/28up-Correlate-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Value of College: It’s Not Just Correlation', 'kicker': 'Dollars and Diplomas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADMISSIONS STANDARDS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-28T01:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ca98ac8-e1d0-5984-951b-a18f6c0d575c', 'word_count': 635, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ca98ac8-e1d0-5984-951b-a18f6c0d575c'}, {'abstract': 'A government spokesman did not rule out some type of discussion with people in contact with Boko Haram to try to win the freedom of hundreds of kidnapped schoolgirls.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/28/world/africa/nigeria-denies-formal-talks-on-captive-schoolgirls.html', 'snippet': 'A government spokesman did not rule out some type of discussion with people in contact with Boko Haram to try to win the freedom of hundreds of kidnapped schoolgirls.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DAKAR, Senegal — With reports swirling in the Nigerian media of a possible negotiation to free hundreds of schoolgirls kidnapped by Islamist extremists, and the country’s top armed forces commander ruling out force to do so, the government’s chief spokesman again denied Tuesday that any “formal, official” talks were underway with the extremist group Boko Haram. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nigeria Denies Formal Talks on Captive Schoolgirls', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nigeria Denies Formal Talks on Captive Schoolgirls', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nigeria', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kidnapping', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boko Haram', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jonathan, Goodluck', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-28T01:05:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Nossiter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nossiter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b454a910-8d7a-5ee4-9979-8cca418b38f6', 'word_count': 798, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b454a910-8d7a-5ee4-9979-8cca418b38f6'}, {'abstract': 'Classes were halted at the University of California, Santa Barbara, on Tuesday after the rampage last week that left six students dead and more than a dozen people injured.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/28/us/after-california-attack-students-pause-and-seek-closure.html', 'snippet': 'Classes were halted at the University of California, Santa Barbara, on Tuesday after the rampage last week that left six students dead and more than a dozen people injured.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISLA VISTA, Calif. — Across the street from the IV Deli Mart, one of the places where Elliot O. Rodger opened fire on Friday night before killing himself, people wrote mournful and inspiring messages in chalk on Tuesday. On the campus of the University of California, Santa Barbara, students stood by a bike path holding “Free Hugs” signs and shouting “Have a great day!” to cyclists and skateboarders. Flags at all 10 University of California campuses remained lowered. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/28/us/SHOOTING-sub/SHOOTING-sub-articleLarge-v2.