al media.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/28/us/police-use-high-tech-lures-to-reel-in-bike-thieves.html', 'snippet': 'To fight a rash of thefts, the police leave bicycles equipped with GPS devices in public places, then track down thieves and post their portraits on social media.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO — Officer Matt Friedman fights crime with modern tools: Twitter, which he uses to publicize pictures of suspects and convicted criminals, and a GPS device, which he uses to track down stolen property.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Police Use High-Tech Lures to Reel in Bike Thieves', 'kicker': 'San Francisco Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Police Use High-Tech Lures to Reel in Bike Thieves', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Richtel, Matt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bicycles and Bicycling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (San Francisco, Calif)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Positioning System', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-28T02:13:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Richtel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Richtel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f9f96e5-0b6f-5d0c-ba03-48814ab18302', 'word_count': 828, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f9f96e5-0b6f-5d0c-ba03-48814ab18302'}, {'abstract': 'Solicitor General Donald Verrilli Jr. recently asked the Supreme Court not to intervene in a lawsuit in which American victims of terrorist attacks in Israel are accusing Jordan’s Arab Bank of providing services to terrorists.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/28/us/amid-split-on-bank-case-us-seeks-a-middle-path.html', 'snippet': 'Solicitor General Donald Verrilli Jr. recently asked the Supreme Court not to intervene in a lawsuit in which American victims of terrorist attacks in Israel are accusing Jordan’s Arab Bank of providing services to terrorists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Solicitor General Donald B. Verrilli Jr. faced a legal and bureaucratic dilemma this year: Obama administration officials could not agree about what he should tell the Supreme Court about a lawsuit in which American victims of terrorist attacks in Israel are suing Arab Bank, the largest financial institution in Jordan.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Amid Split on Bank Case, U.S. Seeks a Middle Path', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Amid Split on Bank Case, U.S. Seeks a Middle Path', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Savage, Charlie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Arab Bank', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Verrilli, Donald B Jr', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court (US)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jordan', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-28T02:26:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charlie Savage', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charlie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Savage', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64e764d6-e40f-5736-ba15-da5117b9bc92', 'word_count': 491, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64e764d6-e40f-5736-ba15-da5117b9bc92'}, {'abstract': 'The State Department cited the fighting among militias in urging Americans against traveling to Libya and asking those already there to leave.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/28/world/africa/libya-state-dept-urges-americans-to-leave.html', 'snippet': 'The State Department cited the fighting among militias in urging Americans against traveling to Libya and asking those already there to leave.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The State Department issued a travel warning\xa0on Tuesday\xa0urging Americans against traveling to Libya and asking those already there to leave. The department cited the continuing fighting among militias, including an attack on the Parliament building 10 days ago; the Tripoli government’s inability to stop the violence; and repeated threats against American interests. The United States Embassy, the State Department said, “is only able to offer very limited emergency services to U.S. citizens in Libya.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Libya: State Dept. Urges Americans to Leave', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Libya: State Dept. Urges Americans to Leave', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kenny, Steve', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'State Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel Warnings', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-28T02:26:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Kenny', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kenny', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/075d149e-080f-56a3-8690-fa23e1c67a91', 'word_count': 74, 'uri': 'nyt://article/075d149e-080f-56a3-8690-fa23e1c67a91'}, {'abstract': 'The United Nations Security Council passed a resolution on Tuesday that shifts its peacekeeping operations in South Sudan toward safeguarding civilians.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/28/world/africa/south-sudan-un-shifts-focus-of-its-mandate.html', 'snippet': 'The United Nations Security Council passed a resolution on Tuesday that shifts its peacekeeping operations in South Sudan toward safeguarding civilians.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The United Nations Security Council unanimously passed a resolution on Tuesday that shifts its peacekeeping operations in South Sudan away from nation-building and toward safeguarding civilians threatened by ethnic violence and political instability. The resolution, which extended the mission until Nov. 30, retains its size but authorizes “all necessary means” to protect civilians, to police rights abuses, to ensure aid delivery and to enforce cease-fires among rival factions in the country, which became independent in 2011. Thousands have been killed, and more than 1.3 million people have fled their homes since December, when fighting erupted between loyalists to President Salva Kiir, an ethnic Dinka, and his former vice president, Riek Machar, a Nuer. A recent United Nations report described atrocities “on a massive scale” by both sides. The United Nations refugee agency reported Tuesday that 76,000 South Sudanese had fled fighting since the latest cease-fire was signed on May 9. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'South Sudan: U.N. Shifts Focus of Its Mandate', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'South Sudan: U.N. Shifts Focus of Its Mandate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Sudan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Security Council (UN)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kiir Mayardit, Salva', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Machar, Riek', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-28T02:29:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/de767b8c-2795-556b-916d-09ff1de7f9d8', 'word_count': 150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de767b8c-2795-556b-916d-09ff1de7f9d8'}, {'abstract': 'One death in a village near the border with Guinea has been confirmed by a laboratory as Ebola, and four other deaths are considered suspect.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/28/world/africa/sierra-leone-ebola-virus-spreads-from-guinea.html', 'snippet': 'One death in a village near the border with Guinea has been confirmed by a laboratory as Ebola, and four other deaths are considered suspect.', 'lead_paragraph': ' West Africa’s Ebola outbreak appears to have spread to Sierra Leone, the World Health Organization said Monday. One death in a village near the border with Guinea has been confirmed by a laboratory as Ebola, and four other deaths are considered suspect. Sierra Leone health officials told the Voice of America that the confirmed case was a woman who had just returned from the funeral of an Ebola victim in Guinea; the suspect cases included her relatives. The outbreak began in Guinea in March and spread to Liberia; of the 259 cases reported to the W.H.O., 175 have been fatal. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sierra Leone: Ebola Virus Spreads From Guinea', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sierra Leone: Ebola Virus Spreads From Guinea', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sierra Leone', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McNeil, Donald G Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guinea', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ebola Virus', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Health Organization', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Voice of America', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-28T02:31:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Donald G. McNeil Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Donald'