lar weekly program will begin distributing itself in July when its contract with Public Radio International ends.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Five suitors came calling, including satellite radio company SiriusXM, but in the end public radio’s “This American Life” decided to go it alone. The popular weekly program is notifying stations on Wednesday that it will distribute the show itself beginning in July when its distribution contract with Public Radio International ends.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spurning Suitors, ‘This American Life’ Opts for Self-Distribution', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘American Life’ Will Distribute Itself to Stations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jensen, Elizabeth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radio', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public Broadcasting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Public Radio', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sirius XM Radio Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Radio Exchange, The', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Radio International', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'This American Life (Radio Program)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-28T15:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f14520c-c8fb-54f9-b202-03a939389b5e', 'word_count': 369, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f14520c-c8fb-54f9-b202-03a939389b5e'}, {'abstract': 'President Roosevelt reassured the nation with a prospective gift to the 1-year-old son of a pilot who sacrificed his life on the Pacific war’s third day.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/29/upshot/after-pearl-harbor-fdr-showed-confidence-that-us-would-exist-in-1956.html', 'snippet': 'President Roosevelt reassured the nation with a prospective gift to the 1-year-old son of a pilot who sacrificed his life on the Pacific war’s third day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is Captain Colin P. Kelly Jr., who was idolized from coast to coast as one of America’s first World War II heroes in the immediate aftermath of the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbor. As Life Magazine reported in December 1941, “Kelly dove his plane straight into the Japanese battleship, Haruna, released a stick of high explosives almost into the mouths of flaming guns, and then vanished himself in the mighty explosion that ushered the 29,000-ton ship to the bottom of the sea.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/29/upshot/29UP-Hero/29UP-Hero-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 713, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/29/upshot/29UP-Hero/29UP-Hero-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 713}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/29/upshot/29UP-Hero/29UP-Hero-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 862, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/29/upshot/29UP-Hero/29UP-Hero-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1724, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': '