ike room service and nail salons, more associated with hotels than health care.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hospitals today offer even general amenities for patients, like room service and nail salons, more associated with hotels than health care.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is This a Hospital or a Hotel?', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hotels and Travel Lodgings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-28T22:28:02+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sunday Review', 'section_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/751b00eb-cb21-532d-9613-93daa8381c8f', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/751b00eb-cb21-532d-9613-93daa8381c8f'}, {'abstract': 'How the Union took advantage of the slaves it was fighting to set free.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/28/worse-than-jeff-davis/', 'snippet': 'How the Union took advantage of the slaves it was fighting to set free.', 'lead_paragraph': 'That “Twelve Years a Slave” won the Oscar for best picture is a stunning achievement in Hollywood cinematic history. Solomon Northup’s autobiography, upon which the movie was based, provides a powerful insider’s account and critique of one of the nation’s founding institutions, while the film rendition disrupts the long tradition of derisive and romantic portrayals of slavery in motion pictures.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-disunion/opinionator-pog-disunion-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': '