ed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Harold Baer Jr., a federal judge in New York whose expansive interpretation of civil liberties drew intense criticism from Republicans and Democrats alike and became a partisan issue in President Bill Clinton’s re-election campaign in 1996, died on Tuesday in Stony Brook, N.Y. He was 81.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/29/nyregion/Baer-Obit/Baer-Obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 816, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/29/nyregion/Baer-Obit/Baer-Obit-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 816}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/29/nyregion/Baer-Obit/Baer-Obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 754, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/29/nyregion/Baer-Obit/Baer-Obit-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1984, 'width': 1459, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/29/nyregion/Baer-Obit/Baer-Obit-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/29/nyregion/Baer-Obit/Baer-Obit-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/29/nyregion/Baer-Obit/Baer-Obit-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Harold Baer Jr., Judge Whose Civil Liberties Rulings Drew Fire, Dies at 81', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Harold Baer Jr., Judge Widely Assailed Over Civil Liberties Decisions, Dies at 81', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baer, Harold Jr (1933-2014)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil Rights and Liberties', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-29T04:48:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph P. Fried', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': 'P.', 'lastname': 'Fried', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/f30068e3-8f71-5f01-809c-fe581df3b00a', 'word_count': 1221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f30068e3-8f71-5f01-809c-fe581df3b00a'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/29/arts/television/whats-on-thursday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '6:05 P.M. (Starz Cinema) BEFORE MIDNIGHT (2013) Nine years after rekindling a romance (“Before Sunset”) in Paris that began nine years before that (“Before Sunrise”) in Vienna, Celine (Julie Delpy) and Jesse (Ethan Hawke, above with Ms. Delpy) are now in Greece, at the end of a summer vacation with their twin daughters (Jennifer and Charlotte Prior) and Hank (Seamus Davey-Fitzpatrick), the son he left behind in Chicago. Jesse wonders if they could move from Paris to the United States to spend more time with Hank. Celine sees his question as a demand to make it happen. And so they will fight about this — and the meaning of love, the nature of time and the difference between men and women — throughout the course of a day. “\xa0‘Before Sunrise,’ ‘Before Sunset’ and ‘Before Midnight’ are modest, charming movies that together add up to the great romantic epic of a generation defined, in the popular mind and our therapists’ offices, by hedged bets, easy ironies and perpetual confusion,” A. O. Scott wrote in The New York Times of this trilogy from the director Richard Linklater. This final installment, he added, “is a wonderful paradox: a movie passionately committed to the ideal of imperfection that is itself very close to perfect.” “Before Sunset” (2004) will be shown at 8:15 a.m. on Cinemax.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Thursday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Thursday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-05-29T05:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/4635038d-11f8-51ba-967a-a6ea4e780f51', 'word_count': 748, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4635038d-11f8-51ba-967a-a6ea4e780f51'}, {'abstract': 'Raymond DeGiorgio, an engineer at General Motors, is at the center of accusations that the company covered up a deadly defect. In these clips from a deposition in April 2013, he answers questions about the faulty ignition switches.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/business/100000002908050/gm-engineer-speaks-in-house-inquiry.html', 'snippet': 'Raymond DeGiorgio, an engineer at General Motors, is at the center of accusations that the company covered up a deadly defect. In these clips from a deposition in April 2013, he answers questions about the faulty ignition switches.', '