', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Life Beyond the Cancer Clinic', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-29T15:10:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mikkael A. Sekeres, M.d', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mikkael', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Sekeres', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f664a3ad-08c4-52a2-824a-250dcdab5ff3', 'word_count': 906, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f664a3ad-08c4-52a2-824a-250dcdab5ff3'}, {'abstract': 'Quarterly output is a prime example of an economic indicator that is important, yet notoriously subject to a lot of later revision.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/30/upshot/with-gdp-its-important-to-distinguish-signal-from-noise.html', 'snippet': 'Quarterly output is a prime example of an economic indicator that is important, yet notoriously subject to a lot of later revision.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An economic report issued this morning provides a good example of the hazards facing election forecasters. The Bureau of Economic Analysis reported that in the first quarter of this year, Gross Domestic Product, a broad indicator of the health of the economy, shrank at an annual rate of 1 percent. Even worse, an alternative and more accurate measure, called Gross Domestic Income, shrank at an annual rate of 2.3 percent. If that persisted, we’d call it a sharp recession.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Deceptive Dip in G.D.P. Points to Perils of Election Forecasting', 'kicker': 'Everyday Economics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gross Domestic Product', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recession and Depression', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bureau of Economic Analysis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Statistics', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-29T15:19:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin Wolfers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wolfers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd52ee4a-d2a5-572b-b497-c6629d32f11a', 'word_count': 534, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd52ee4a-d2a5-572b-b497-c6629d32f11a'}, {'abstract': 'Pollsters find that “global warming” is more familiar and conjures stronger emotional responses.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/30/upshot/climate-change-or-global-warming-tough-choice-for-pollsters.html', 'snippet': 'Pollsters find that “global warming” is more familiar and conjures stronger emotional responses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many scientists have come to prefer the term “climate change” over “global warming.” Climate change is a broader term that encompasses not only the warming of the planet in recent years but also the rise of severe storms, droughts and damaging winds. The choice of terms has important implications for polling because Americans give different answers to questions depending on which is used.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/30/upshot/30up-pollster/30up-pollster-articleLarge.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/30/upshot/30up-pollster/30up-pollster-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/30/upshot/30up-pollster/30up-pollster-jumbo.png', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/30/upshot/30up-pollster/30up-pollster-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/30/upshot/30up-pollster/30up-pollster-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/30/upshot/30up-pollster/30up-pollster-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/30/upshot/30up-pollster/30up-pollster-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Americans More Worried About ‘Warming’ Than ‘Climate Change’', 'kicker': 'Ask a Pollster', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Luntz, Frank I', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-29T15:22:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allison Kopicki', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kopicki', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3267c88a-7d39-5fd8-8a1d-904847d4cc13', 'word_count': 536, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3267c88a-7d39-5fd8-8a1d-904847d4cc13'}, {'abstract': 'London Wine Week includes tastes, classes and dinners.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/29/a-wine-week-in-london/', 'snippet': 'London Wine Week includes tastes, classes and dinners.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Paris, the capital of wine-loving France, wraps up its beer week festival, London, more known for ales and pubs, is uncorking its first wine week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Wine Week in London', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ramsay, Gordon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-29T15:25:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susanne Fowler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fowler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41591d41-b3a9-5bc6-aec9-c51e1c03a7e0', 'word_count': 325, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41591d41-b3a9-5bc6-aec9-c51e1c03a7e0'}, {'abstract': 'Producers aim to get Ms. Moss to play Heidi in a Broadway revival of Wendy Wasserstein play, which won a Tony and a Pulitzer Prize.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/29/elisabeth-moss-in-talks-to-star-in-the-heidi-chronicles-on-broadway/', 'snippet': 'Producers aim to get Ms. Moss to play Heidi in a Broadway revival of Wendy Wasserstein play, which won a Tony and a Pulitzer Prize.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sunday’s finale of the 2014 episodes of “Mad Men,” the copy chief Peggy Olson was single (but making eyes at the repairman), childless (yet loving to the neighbor boy, Julio) and newly 30 (though only Don knew). Her concerns and conflicts called to mind the life choices of Heidi Holland, the brilliant, lonely art historian in “The Heidi Chronicles,” the beloved Wendy Wasserstein play that won the Pulitzer Prize and the Tony Award in 1989, with Joan Allen as Heidi. If a group of Broadway producers have their way, Peggy and Heidi will have something else in common: the actress Elisabeth Moss.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/30/arts/29artsbeat-moss1/29artsbeat-moss1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 884, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/30/arts/29artsbeat-moss1/29artsbeat-moss1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 884}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/30/arts/29artsbeat-moss1/29artsbeat-moss1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 695, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/30/arts/29artsbeat-moss1/29artsbeat-moss1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1390, 'subType'