riter talks about why he revisited footage shot with his friend, the photojournalist Tim Hetherington, who was later killed in Libya.', 'lead_paragraph': '\xa0', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sebastian Junger on His Companion Film to ‘Restrepo’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hetherington, Tim', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Junger, Sebastian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Korengal Valley (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-29T15:58:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7473c59c-bb2c-51cd-9591-e1657526162f', 'word_count': 906, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7473c59c-bb2c-51cd-9591-e1657526162f'}, {'abstract': 'Businesses are planting themselves in existing buildings in San Francisco, while residents are getting little relief from the high housing costs, and new construction faces obstructions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/01/arts/design/san-francisco-repurposes-the-old-for-the-future.html', 'snippet': 'Businesses are planting themselves in existing buildings in San Francisco, while residents are getting little relief from the high housing costs, and new construction faces obstructions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO — It has gone widely unremarked, maybe because it’s so obvious to people here, that tech firms in San Francisco have not (yet) been moving into new buildings; they’ve been taking over old ones. Twitter has leased a onetime furniture mart on Market Street, and AirBnB has renovated a century-old industrial warehouse south of Market. Yelp occupies part of 140 New Montgomery downtown, the magnificent Art Deco former Pacific Telephone tower from the 1920s, lovingly revamped by Cathy Simon, an architect with Perkins & Will, and the developers Wilson Meany Sullivan.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Urban Renewal, No Bulldozer', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Urban Renewal, No Bulldozer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kimmelman, Michael', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate (Commercial)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restoration and Renovation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Renting and Leasing (Real Estate)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Relocation of Business', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Building (Construction)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Area Planning and Renewal', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Silicon Valley (Calif)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mission District (San Francisco, Calif)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Benchmark Capital', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Spotify', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Zendesk Inc', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yelp', 'rank': 18, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-29T16:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Kimmelman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kimmelman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/52f008c1-6fcf-52d6-a99c-e5701abe81a9', 'word_count': 1992, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52f008c1-6fcf-52d6-a99c-e5701abe81a9'}, {'abstract': 'Three Stockholm girls set out to make a name for themselves in the late punk period in “We Are the Best!,” from Lukas Moodysson.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/30/movies/we-are-the-best-from-lukas-moodysson.html', 'snippet': 'Three Stockholm girls set out to make a name for themselves in the late punk period in “We Are the Best!,” from Lukas Moodysson.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is hardly a shortage of movies about rock ’n’ roll, but there are few as perfect — which is to say as ragged, as silly, as touching or as true — as “We Are the Best!,” Lukas Moodysson’s sweet and rambunctious new film. The setting is Stockholm in 1982, hardly a legendary place or moment in the annals of modern music, but that’s kind of the point. Rock ’n’ roll glory is wherever you find it, and whatever you make of it.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/30/arts/30weare-image/30weare-image-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 394, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/30/arts/30weare-image/30weare-image-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 394}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo',