, 'pub_date': '2014-05-29T20:37:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/43dab2b2-9b7a-5a19-ac47-3d5196f91522', 'word_count': 827, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43dab2b2-9b7a-5a19-ac47-3d5196f91522'}, {'abstract': 'In “The Third Plate: Field Notes on the Future of Food,” the chef Dan Barber calls for a new organizing principle for cuisine that goes beyond “farm to table.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/30/books/the-third-plate-by-dan-barber.html', 'snippet': 'In “The Third Plate: Field Notes on the Future of Food,” the chef Dan Barber calls for a new organizing principle for cuisine that goes beyond “farm to table.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not long ago, Dan Barber, the chef and a co-owner of Blue Hill in Manhattan, and the animating force behind the Stone Barns Center for Food and Agriculture in Westchester County, came up with a dish he calls Rotation Risotto. It’s a manifesto on a plate, a tricky play on the Italian classic that uses, instead of rice, a medley of lesser-known grains: rye, barley, buckwheat and millet.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/30/arts/30JPBOOKBARBER/30JPBOOKBARBER-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 853, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/30/arts/30JPBOOKBARBER/30JPBOOKBARBER-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 853}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/30/arts/30JPBOOKBARBE