N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Emanuel, Rahm', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-30T01:08:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b5dfda1-b906-5ff2-afaf-a5456625ef67', 'word_count': 521, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b5dfda1-b906-5ff2-afaf-a5456625ef67'}, {'abstract': 'In one book, Willie Randolph provides insights into colleagues; another book captures the character of North Brother Island; and another book traces the evolution of public markets that served a seaport city in 1800 into a radically different system of demand and supply.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/01/nyregion/brooklyn-yankee-lonely-island-food-city.html', 'snippet': 'In one book, Willie Randolph provides insights into colleagues; another book captures the character of North Brother Island; and another book traces the evolution of public markets that served a seaport city in 1800 into a radically different syst...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Willie Randolph played for almost as many New York professional sports teams as Gladys Gooding (the organist for the Dodgers at Ebbets Field and the Rangers and Knicks at Madison Square Garden). A graduate of Brooklyn’s Samuel J. Tilden High School, Randolph spent most of his playing career with the Yankees, capped it with the Mets, returned to coach for the Yankees and then managed the Mets. ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': N