t Europe’s most beguiling city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cole Porter isn’t the only American to have fallen in love with Paris in the springtime, which may explain why four books about Europe’s most beguiling city have appeared all at once. By pure serendipity, their four authors have independently succeeded in producing a chronological quartet.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01SEYMOUR/01SEYMOUR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01SEYMOUR/01SEYMOUR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01SEYMOUR/01SEYMOUR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 686, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01SEYMOUR/01SEYMOUR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1340, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01SEYMOUR/01SEYMOUR-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01SEYMOUR/01SEYMOUR-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01SEYMOUR/01SEYMOUR-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'They Love Paris', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'They Love Paris', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DeJean, Joan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baxter, John', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mazzeo, Tilar J', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McAuliffe, Mary', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-30T14:35:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Miranda Seymour', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Miranda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seymour', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f25d704c-178c-574f-a540-fcc1538c6f13', 'word_count': 2023, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f25d704c-178c-574f-a540-fcc1538c6f13'}, {'abstract': 'In “Hyde,” Daniel Levine tells Robert Louis Stevenson’s “The Strange Case of Dr. Jekyll and Mr. Hyde” from the monster’s perspective.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/01/books/review/hyde-by-daniel-levine.html', 'snippet': 'In “Hyde,” Daniel Levine tells Robert Louis Stevenson’s “The Strange Case of Dr. Jekyll and Mr. Hyde” from the monster’s perspective.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Robert Louis Stevenson’s “The Strange Case of Dr. Jekyll and Mr. Hyde” is an oblique and artful Gothic tale framed as a detective story. The truth seeker is Jekyll’s lawyer, Utterson, the book’s most prominent character. Jekyll — the \xadgentleman who dabbles in chemical self-\xadtransformation — appears only \xadintermittently, never fully speaking for himself until the end, when he discloses the details of the disastrous experiments that unleashed his primitive alter ego. The novel isn’t a conventional horror story, lingering on the macabre for its own sake, but an allegory of the divided self, perhaps also a meditation on addiction. Stevenson dramatizes human duality but doesn’t analyze its causes, treating it as pervasive and fundamental. For him, the Jekyll-Hyde split is the split in all of us, between the animals we evolved from and the angels we aspire to be.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01KIRN/01KIRN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 346, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01KIRN/01KIRN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 346}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01KIRN/01KIRN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 590, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01KIRN/01KIRN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1180, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01KIRN/01KIRN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01KIRN/01KIRN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01KIRN/01KIRN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Dark Side', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Dark Side', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kirn, Walter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Levine, Daniel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-30T14:36:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Walter Kirn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Walter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kirn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e7fd6aa-7049-5ced-baf0-1653277ed342', 'word_count': 1040, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e7fd6aa-7049-5ced-baf0-1653277ed342'}, {'abstract': 'Brittney Griner, the second-leading career scorer at the top level of women’s college basketball, writes about her life as an athlete and a gay woman.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/01/books/review/in-my-skin-by-brittney-griner.html', 'snippet': 'Brittney Griner, the second-leading career scorer at the top level of women’s college basketball, writes about her life as an athlete and a gay woman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Brittney Griner was a senior in high school, a 6-foot-8 center on her way to an all-\xadAmerican basketball career at Baylor University, she feared telling her father that she was a lesbian. His explosive, curse-filled response upon finding out, Griner writes in a painful, searing and ultimately liberating memoir, “In My Skin: My Life on and Off the Basketball Court,” was exactly what she had dreaded.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01LONGMAN/01LONGMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 826, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01LONGMAN/01LONGMAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 826}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01LONGMAN/01LONGMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 744, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01LONGMAN/01LONGMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1487, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01LONGMAN/01LONGMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01LONGMAN/01LONGMAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01LONGMAN/01LONGMAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Life Out Loud', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Life Out Loud', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Griner, Brittney', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hovey, Sue', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-30T14:36:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeré Longman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeré', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Longman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad9a1022-e8f5-51d7-91af-4bef7c00ad74', 'word_count': 648, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad9a1022-e8f5-51d7-91af-4bef7c00ad74'}, {'abstract': 'New books include Jeff VanderMeer’s “Authority,” the second novel in a trilogy about an ecological danger zone.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/01/books/review/jeff-vandermeers-authority-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New books include Jeff VanderMeer’s “Authority,” the second novel in a trilogy about an ecological danger zone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Annihilation,” the first book of Jeff VanderMeer’s Southern Reach trilogy, readers were introduced to Area X, a stretch of coastal wetland whose conspiracy-theory name is the icing atop a mille-feuille of layered, slow-building, deliciously creepy horror. Twelve teams of \xadresearchers, stripped of names to somehow protect them from the identity-warping effects of the danger zone, have traveled into Area X; “Annihilation” followed “the biologist” as she seemingly became the only survivor to actually understand what was happening.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01BKSSCIFI/0601-bks-SCI-FI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 456, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01BKSSCIFI/0601-bks-SCI-FI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 456}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01BKSSCIFI/0601-bks-SCI-FI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 778, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01BKSSCIFI/0601-bks-SCI-FI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1556, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01BKSSCIFI/01BKSSCIFI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01BKSSCIFI/01BKSSCIFI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01BKSSCIFI/01BKSSCIFI-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Science Fiction', 'kicker': 'Summer Reading', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Science Fiction', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'VanderMeer, Jeff', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walton, Jo', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beckett, Chris', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brennan, Marie', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Price, Daniel', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-30T14:38:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By N. K. Jemisin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'N.', 'middlename': 'K.', 'lastname': 'Jemisin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a52111c-ca17-53b3-91ff-48b4af5c2791', 'word_count': 2430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a52111c-ca17-53b3-91ff-48b4af5c2791'}, {'abstract': 'New thrillers include Mo Hayder’s “Wolf” and Jo Nesbo’s “The Son.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/01/books/review/mo-hayders-wolf-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New thrillers include Mo Hayder’s “Wolf” and Jo Nesbo’s “The Son.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'I had the wrong idea about beach books. It came from “Goodbye, Columbus,” where Neil Klugman tells Brenda Patimkin that his cousin Doris always has “War and Peace” by the pool: “That’s how I know it’s the summer, when Doris is reading ‘War and Peace.’ ” Pool and beach being the same to me back then — an idiot kid forced to sit outside with no TV, uncomfortable with my shirt off as my grandfather paraded around to show off he still had a body — I didn’t see that Philip Roth was mocking Doris, and so for years protected myself from the water and sand I resented and feared by lugging along a classic. This is how I read “Swann’s Way” at the Mamaroneck Beach Club as a guest of Uncle Marty and Aunt Lee. It took many visits. Here I would like to thank them personally.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/0616THRILLER1SUB/0616THRILLER1SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/0616THRILLER1SUB/0616THRILLER1SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/0616THRILLER1SUB/0616THRILLER1SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 666, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name