ed', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d483d4b6-1fd6-5c3f-9eba-52a32536e873', 'word_count': 2605, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d483d4b6-1fd6-5c3f-9eba-52a32536e873'}, {'abstract': 'Win, lose or draw, professional players cherish support from their fans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/31/sports/soccer/success-is-in-the-eye-of-the-beholder.html', 'snippet': 'Win, lose or draw, professional players cherish support from their fans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I smile and sign autographs no matter what. Most fans don’t seem to care much if we won or lost. They don’t know if I had the game of my life or a complete shocker. They say, “Great game! You were awesome!” Sometimes they even tell me I was wonderful when I didn’t play a single minute. I thank them whether I feel on top of the world or have a lump of disappointment in my throat.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/31/sports/soccer/31yael1/31yael1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1066, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/31/sports/soccer/31yael1/31yael1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1066}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/31/sports/soccer/31yael1/31yael1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 577, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/31/sports/soccer/31yael1/31yael1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1153, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/31/sports/soccer/31yael1/31yael1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/05/31/sports/soccer/31yael1/31yael1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/31/sports/soccer/31yael1/31yael1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Success Is in the Eye of the Beholder', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': "National Women's Soccer League (US)", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington Spirit (Soccer Team)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-30T14:38:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Yael Averbuch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Yael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Averbuch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0842fec-daac-5a12-ba73-74175c1ec4da', 'word_count': 654, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0842fec-daac-5a12-ba73-74175c1ec4da'}, {'abstract': 'New travel books include John Waters’s “Carsick,” in which the director hitchhikes from one of his homes in Baltimore to another of his homes in San Francisco.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/01/books/review/john-waterss-carsick-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New travel books include John Waters’s “Carsick,” in which the director hitchhikes from one of his homes in Baltimore to another of his homes in San Francisco.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paul Bowles observed that an important difference between a tourist and a traveler “is that the former accepts his own civilization without question; not so the traveler, who compares it with the others, and rejects those elements he finds not to his liking.” This difference also separates writers about travels from “travel writers.” Is the voyaging writer telling us canny stories from a romping trip or using his peregrinations to relocate his place in the world? Sometimes it’s difficult to tell. D. H. Lawrence knocked off travel essays about places in which he had arrived only a few hours before, but he was writing about himself and his intuitions. Bowles, for his part, lived for years in Morocco but traveled through the desert with a suitcase filled with his favorite neckties. Do we read either man for profound and meticulously earned insights about Sardinia or the Sahara, or for their ability to convey something about themselves as they pass through those places? Most writers are “passing through,” and we expect no more of them. It’s part of their charm, if they have any. And part of the sometimes dubious premise of travel writing itself.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/0601BKSTRAVEL/0601BKSTRAVEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 452, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/0601BKSTRAVEL/0601BKSTRAVEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 452}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/0601BKSTRAVEL/0601BKSTRAVEL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 772, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/0601BKSTRAVEL/0601BKSTRAVEL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1376, 'width': 1825, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/0601BKSTRAVEL/0601BKSTRAVEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/0601BKSTRAVEL/0601BKSTRAVEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/0601BKSTRAVEL/0601BKSTRAVEL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Travel', 'kicker': 'Summer Reading', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Travel', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waters, John (1946- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sinclair, Iain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Butcher, Tim', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hoare, Philip', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bewes, Diccon', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Wanderer (Movie)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Carsick: John Waters Hitchhikes Across America (Book)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-30T14:38:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lawrence Osborne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lawrence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Osborne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a82f42ee-6af6-5dfb-91c7-96e20468da5f', 'word_count': 2450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a82f42ee-6af6-5dfb-91c7-96e20468da5f'}, {'abstract': 'New books from inside and around the world of pop music, including the journalist Lisa Robinson’s memoir “There Goes Gravity.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/01/books/review/lisa-robinsons-there-goes-gravity-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New books from inside and around the world of pop music, including the journalist Lisa Robinson’s memoir “There Goes Gravity.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'All rock biographies/memoirs agree on one point. As Gregg Allman tells it in “One Way Out,” an exhaustive oral history of the Allman Brothers Band by Alan Paul, escaping the workaday lot of “a shock-absorber washer-jammer in Detroit . . . is why I became a musician in the first place.” Or as Joey Ramone sang: “It’s not my place in the 9-to-5 world.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/16POPMUSICSUB/0616POPMUSICSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 413, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/16POPMUSICSUB/0616POPMUSICSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 413}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/16POPMUSICSUB/0616POPMUSICSUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 704, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/16POPMUSICSUB/0616POPMUSICSUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1287, 'width': 1872, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/16POPMUSICSUB/16POPMUSICSUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/16POPMUSICSUB/16POPMUSICSUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/16POPMUSICSUB/16POPMUSICSUB-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pop Music', 'kicker': 'Summer Reading', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pop Music', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-30T14:39:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Howard Hampton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Howard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hampton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f64072b1-006b-54d4-8c03-e8f37ee457c0', 'word_count': 2680, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f64072b1-006b-54d4-8c03-e8f37ee457c0'}, {'abstract': 'New books about the science of why we laugh, the references of Monty Python, the art of writing comedy and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/01/books/review/mike-sackss-poking-a-dead-frog-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New books about the science of why we laugh, the references of Monty Python, the art of writing comedy and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Writing seriously about humor is a tricky proposition. If you approach the subject too analytically, you risk losing sight of what made it worth writing about in the first place. If you try to leaven your analysis with a little humor of your own, there’s a good chance you’ll end up reminding your readers why making people laugh is best left to the people who do it for a living.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/16HUMORSUB/16HUMORSUB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 354, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/16HUMORSUB/16HUMORSUB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 354}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/16HUMORSUB/16HUMORSUB-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 603, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/16HUMORSUB/16HUMORSUB-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1119, 'width': 1899, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/16HUMORSUB/16HUMORSUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/16HUMORSUB/16HUMORSUB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/16HUMORSUB/16HUMORSUB-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Humor', 'kicker': 'Summer Reading', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Humor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keepnews, Peter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-30T14:39:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Keepnews', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keepnews', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e8d86b1-14a1-5d5a-86bf-c57036fdc57f', 'word_count': 2472, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e8d86b1-14a1-5d5a-86bf-c57036fdc57f'}, {'abstract': 'An uncle and two cousins were among those accused of beating to death a pregnant woman who married against her family’s wishes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/31/world/asia/honor-killing-of-pakistani-woman.html', 'snippet': 'An uncle and two cousins were among those accused of beating to death a pregnant woman who married against her family’s wishes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — The murder case of Farzana Parveen, it seemed, could hardly have turned more tragic or gruesome: a 25-year-old pregnant woman, bludgeoned to death with a brick by family members on a busy street, for having married the man she loved.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/31/world/31PAKISTAN-2/31PAKISTAN-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 425, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/31/