'Women and Girls', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-30T14:40:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Declan Walsh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Declan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Walsh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4cfd83bb-df5a-5582-9735-1055da2e8625', 'word_count': 1089, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4cfd83bb-df5a-5582-9735-1055da2e8625'}, {'abstract': 'Richard Barrios surveys the extensive world of movie musicals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/01/books/review/dangerous-rhythm-by-richard-barrios.html', 'snippet': 'Richard Barrios surveys the extensive world of movie musicals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'All movies are musical, film being as reliant on rhythm and tempo as sonatas are. Only a few movies are musicals, though, and the fewness isn’t going away. Live-action musicals are nearly as endangered a movie species as westerns. Whether you think those few are too many or nowhere near enough will go a long way toward determining your response to Richard Barrios’s “Dangerous Rhythm: Why Movie Musicals Matter.” The subtitle indicates which side Barrios comes down on. The fact of his having written the exhaustively researched and exuberantly argued “A Song in the Dark: The Birth of the Musical Film” (1995) should settle any doubts.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01FEENEY/01FEENEY-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 648, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01FEENEY/01FEENEY-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 648}}, {'rank': 0, 'subt