'word_count': 1291, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56946f3f-ea11-5d96-80c3-e32d5ba8e246'}, {'abstract': 'Two new books explore the possibilities of street photography.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/01/books/review/unfamiliar-streets-and-touching-strangers.html', 'snippet': 'Two new books explore the possibilities of street photography.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The flâneur, that feature of the 19th-century urban landscape, stood apart from the city as a wide-eyed wanderer. But when he had a camera in his hands, he became the archetypal street photographer, a curious observer who captured his environment in what Henri Cartier-Bresson called its "decisive moments”: spontaneous, serendipitous, intuitive, singular. In “Unfamiliar Streets: The Photographs of Richard Avedon, Charles Moore, Martha Rosler, and Philip-Lorca diCorcia,” the art historian Katherine A. Bussard explores the genre of street photography, beginning with this dominant definition and then expanding it. The photographers she investigates, whose work here spans the 1940s through 2001, manipulate the viewer’s imagination of the city, or change the way viewers act within it. According to Bussard, accurately representing experiences of city streets “involves addressing issues of gender, commerce, race, collectivity, class, dispossession, sexuality and spectacle.” In other words, street photography isn’t only the magic of capturing a well-composed slice of life; it’s the more complex task of recapitulating or intensifying the economic, class and gender tensions of the city street within an image. The image should galvanize the viewer or, ideally, alter street life as it’s lived simply by the fact of the image having been made.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01ALTMAN2/01ALTMAN2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 748, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01ALTMAN2/01ALTMAN2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 748}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01ALTMAN2/01ALTMAN2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 822, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01ALTMAN2/01ALTMAN2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1644, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01ALTMAN2/01ALTMAN2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01ALTMAN2/01ALTMAN2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01ALTMAN2/01ALTMAN2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Street Life', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Street Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Renaldi, Richard', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bussard, Katherine A', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-30T14:41:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anna Altman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Altman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0cc8d2bc-8b9e-575b-8f3f-42e6079fdae4', 'word_count': 988, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0cc8d2bc-8b9e-575b-8f3f-42e6079fdae4'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of stories, essays, poems and reminiscences from the creator of the public radio show “A Prairie Home Companion.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/01/books/review/the-keillor-reader-by-garrison-keillor.html', 'snippet': 'A collection of stories, essays, poems and reminiscences from the creator of the public radio show “A Prairie Home Companion.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some writers define themselves by contrasting their talents or sensibility with those of another writer. “Well, I’m no Joyce Carol Oates,” they’ll tell you, false modesty oozing from their pores like musk. “I publish a book every eight years.” Or, “You wouldn’t confuse me with Thomas Merton,” they’ll say, referring to the Trappist monk and mystic. “Kneeling bags my nylons.” The best of these self-conscious comparisons may come from James Thurber, who was once asked to review Salvador Dalí’s autobiography. Comparing his own golden-hued Columbus, Ohio, upbringing with young Dalí’s chiaroscuro of ermine capes and necrophilia, Thurber wrote, “The naked truth about me is to the naked truth about Salvador Dalí as an old ukulele in the attic is to a piano in a tree, and I mean a piano with breasts.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01SUBALLFORD/01SUBALLFORD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 311, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01SUBALLFORD/01SUBALLFORD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 311}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01SUBALLFORD/01SUBALLFORD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 531, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01SUBALLFORD/01SUBALLFORD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1062, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01SUBALLFORD/01SUBALLFORD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01SUBALLFORD/01SUBALLFORD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01SUBALLFORD/01SUBALLFORD-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Minnesota Nice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Minnesota Nice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Alford, Henry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keillor, Garrison', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-30T14:45:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Henry Alford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Henry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b95ca2a9-42d3-5418-8907-f3d46bdebcef', 'word_count': 1199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b95ca2a9-42d3-5418-8907-f3d46bdebcef'}, {'abstract': 'The plucky heroine of the food writer Ruth Reichl’s first novel goes on working for a magazine after it folds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/01/books/review/ruth-reichls-delicious.html', 'snippet': 'The plucky heroine of the food writer Ruth Reichl’s first novel goes on working for a magazine after it folds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ruth Reichl is known for many things, among them her best-selling memoirs, including “Tender at the Bone” and “Comfort Me With Apples,” as well as her years of reviewing restaurants for both The Los Angeles Times and The New York Times, and as the editor in chief of the seemingly immortal Gourmet magazine — until it folded. With the arrival of Twitter, Reichl added another genre to her résumé: haiku-like paeans to breakfast in any weather: “Sunny in Charlotte. Returned to rain. Gray New York morning. Big bowl of ginger strewn congee. Chiles. Soy. Scallion. So consoled.” The next day, it was still raining, but once again food came to the rescue: “Gray drizzle. Umbrellas up. Sidewalks sloppy. Rye bread, still warm. Herring: pungent, rich. Sour cream. Onions. Solace in the city.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01CHRISTIANSON/01CHRISTIANSON-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 1172, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01CHRISTIANSON/01CHRISTIANSON-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1172}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01CHRISTIANSON/01CHRISTIANSON-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 525, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01CHRISTIANSON/01CHRISTIANSON-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1049, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01CHRISTIANSON/01CHRISTIANSON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01CHRISTIANSON/01CHRISTIANSON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01CHRISTIANSON/01CHRISTIANSON-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Appetites', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Appetites', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reichl, Ruth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-30T14:45:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Christensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Christensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3e3b90c-8170-580b-a8a5-3c84883275e2', 'word_count': 892, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3e3b90c-8170-580b-a8a5-3c84883275e2'}, {'abstract': 'In their new memoirs, Colson Whitehead and Brooks Haxton use poker to expand our sense of how human beings work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/01/books/review/the-noble-hustle-and-fading-hearts-on-the-river.html', 'snippet': 'In their new memoirs, Colson Whitehead and Brooks Haxton use poker to expand our sense of how human beings work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Art is the deliberate transformed by the accidental. If you’re a writer, you brew a pot of coffee, adjust the lights, go over your notes and get started, though you know that something — a phone call, a memory, a random image — is likely to barge in and change everything. Instead of “art,” of course, you could say “science,” “making gumbo” and even “romance”: You show up at Veronica’s apartment with your hair slicked back and a bouquet in your hand, and who answers the door? Betty.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01KIRBY/01KIRBY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 888, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01KIRBY/01KIRBY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 888}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01KIRBY/01KIRBY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 692, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01KIRBY/01KIRBY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1384, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01KIRBY/01KIRBY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01KIRBY/01KIRBY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01KIRBY/01KIRBY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Five-Card Studs', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Five-Card Studs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Whitehead, Colson', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haxton, Brooks', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poker (Card Game)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-30T14:45:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Kirby', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kirby', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc58a926-47f4-5be1-b71e-233ad5c555b6', 'word_count': 1218, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc58a926-47f4-5be1-b71e-233ad5c555b6'}, {'abstract': 'In their new books, Nelson George and Questlove chart the rise and reign of the African-American dance show “Soul Train.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/01/books/review/the-hippest-trip-in-america-and-soul-train.html', 'snippet': 'In their new books, Nelson George and Questlove chart the rise and reign of the African-American dance show “Soul Train.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I was growing up in Chicago, there was no rowdier time in our house than Friday night, when my mother, my three siblings and I stayed up to watch “Red Hot and Blues” on Channel 26, Chicago’s first UHF station. Part advertisement and part dance spectacle, it was our chance to celebrate the end of the week, listen and dance to our favorite R & B songs and howl at the cheesy commercials delivered by pitchmen selling used cars and ugly furniture: “You’ll do well to see Dell’s — Dell’s Furniture.” (Even hearing the voice was enough to send us all into fits of laughter.) Just as attractive as the music was the lineup of shabbily dressed kids from the West Side who gathered in front of the camera to dance — a great counterpart to us shabbily dressed kids from the South Side. The show was the highlight of our week.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '40', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01MCNATT/01MCNATT-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 404, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01MCNATT/01MCNATT-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 404}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01MCNATT/01MCNATT-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 689, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01MCNATT/01MCNATT-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1379, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01MCNATT/01MCNATT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/06/01/books/review/01MCNATT/01MCNATT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 