}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/96543f0a-1e1b-5542-b355-48d946805f62', 'word_count': 1087, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96543f0a-1e1b-5542-b355-48d946805f62'}, {'abstract': 'Off-season work was common for baseball stars in an earlier era, but a holdout by Koufax and his Dodgers teammate Don Drysdale helped change that.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/05/31/upshot/how-sandy-koufaxs-motel-helped-lead-to-baseballs-big-money-era.html', 'snippet': 'Off-season work was common for baseball stars in an earlier era, but a holdout by Koufax and his Dodgers teammate Don Drysdale helped change that.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1962, the star Los Angeles Dodgers pitcher Sandy Koufax invested in a West Hollywood motor inn, which was renamed “Sandy Koufax’s Tropicana Motel.” Down Santa Monica Boulevard from the famed Troubadour club, these “74 luxurious air-conditioned rooms” — rented at “popular prices” — came to lodge some of the biggest musical acts of the period: Alice Cooper, Bob Marley, the Mamas and the Papas, Led Zeppelin, Janis Joplin, Jim Morrison and the Doors. “I don’t know which made me more excited,” said one guest, “to be in Sandy’s motel or to be in a room right beside Sly Stone, from Sly and the Family Stone.”', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/31/upshot/31UP-Koufax/31UP-Koufax-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 385, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/31/upshot/31UP-Koufax/31UP-Koufax-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 385}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/31/upshot/31UP-Koufax/31UP-Koufax-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 657, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype':