-8262-5328-9d3e-85f8498a2cc2', 'word_count': 1264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8500e402-8262-5328-9d3e-85f8498a2cc2'}, {'abstract': 'Jem Roberts’s biography of Adams, coming this fall, will include previously unpublished excerpts from the writer’s archive at Cambridge.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/30/douglas-adams-biography-to-include-unseen-hitchhikers-guide-material/', 'snippet': 'Jem Roberts’s biography of Adams, coming this fall, will include previously unpublished excerpts from the writer’s archive at Cambridge.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are very few things that Douglas Adams did not touch on in his “Hitchhiker’s Guide to the Galaxy” novels, a peculiar blend of science fiction and comedy that began life as a BBC radio series and later had life on television and film as well. Apart from disquisitions on the destruction of Earth, as well as its creation (with special attention to fjords), space travel, alien life forms, depressive robots, celestial bureaucracy, animals that beg you to slice off and eat bits of them, the crucial role of mice and the universe’s loudest rock band, the book asks and answers (well, kind of) the Big Question: What is the meaning of “life, the universe and everything?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Douglas Adams Biography to Include Unseen ‘Hitchhiker’s Guide’ Material', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ADAMS, DOUGLAS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-05-30T17:02:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e42b36fd-7fa1-54d3-b10f-c185deb078c7', 'word_count': 570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e42b36fd-7fa1-54d3-b10f-c185deb078c7'}, {'abstract': '“Boxed,” a book which grew out of an exhibition by the artist at Paul Kasmin Gallery earlier this year, is the follow up to his previous cultural-study-cum-art-project, “Nailed,” which told the story of nail adornment.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/30/dzine-visual-history-and-art-of-boxing-book-carlos-rolon/', 'snippet': '“Boxed,” a book which grew out of an exhibition by the artist at Paul Kasmin Gallery earlier this year, is the follow up to his previous cultural-study-cum-art-project, “Nailed,” which told the story of nail adornment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The artist Carlos Rolón, better known as Dzine, has long been a boxing fan, if not a practitioner. “My father took me for boxing lessons when I was 13,” he says. “I was more scared then excited. I developed a hernia.” The injury didn’t dampen his passion, which has led him to produce “Boxed: A Visual History and the Art of Boxing,” an image-driven compendium of the sport, from the Grecian Olympiads to bare-knuckle fighting to today’s megabucks stadium battles.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/30/t-magazine/30boxed-vanmeter-slide-P7GW/30boxed-vanmeter-slide-P7GW-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 721, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/05/30/t-magazine/30boxed-vanmeter-slide-P7GW/30boxed-vanmeter-slide-P7GW-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 721}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/05/30/t-magazine/30boxed-vanmeter-slide-P7GW/30boxed-vanmeter-slide-P7GW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 853, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 