, 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c79706ce-7cd9-50a4-9999-c46572d46274', 'word_count': 1290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c79706ce-7cd9-50a4-9999-c46572d46274'}, {'abstract': 'The two most poetic Shakespeare ballets will be performed this month at Lincoln Center: George Balanchine’s “A Midsummer Night’s Dream” and Frederick Ashton’s “The Dream.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/01/arts/dance/at-lincoln-center-ballets-by-balanchine-and-ashton.html', 'snippet': 'The two most poetic Shakespeare ballets will be performed this month at Lincoln Center: George Balanchine’s “A Midsummer Night’s Dream” and Frederick Ashton’s “The Dream.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'This month, the two most poetic and endlessly rewatchable Shakespeare ballets are to be performed on two sides of Lincoln Center: George Balanchine’s two-act “A Midsummer Night’s Dream” (1962) from New York City Ballet at the David H. Koch Theater (opening on Tuesday) and Frederick Ashton’s one-act “The Dream” (1964) from American Ballet Theater at the Metropolitan Opera House (starting June 30). You might suppose this is to commemorate the 450th anniversary of Shakespeare’s birth this year, but no. The same juxtaposition happened in 2010 and 2012. It’s just the happiest symptom of Lincoln Center’s Ballet Madness each spring, whereby America’s two leading companies play on different sides of the same plaza. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/arts/01DREAM1/01DREAM1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/01/arts/01DREAM1/01DREAM1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/arts/01DRE