one, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c77301a8-1950-500a-b67e-42f57a76cd57', 'word_count': 888, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c77301a8-1950-500a-b67e-42f57a76cd57'}, {'abstract': 'Paolo Pellegrin, a photojournalist who has been documenting efforts to uncover mass graves in Srebrenica for the past three years, talks about what it was like photographing the annual Srebrenica memorial ceremony and burial of the dead.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/05/30/under-cover-paolo-pellegrin-on-photographing-srebrenica-20-years-after-the-genocide/', 'snippet': 'Paolo Pellegrin, a photojournalist who has been documenting efforts to uncover mass graves in Srebrenica for the past three years, talks about what it was like photographing the annual Srebrenica memorial ceremony and burial of the dead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The June 1 cover story by Scott Anderson about efforts to uncover mass graves in Srebrenica, Bosnia, is accompanied by a series of haunting photographs by Paolo Pellegrin, who has been documenting the process for the past three years. Some of his pictures capture the scale of the genocide; others examine in extreme close-up the people affected by the tragedy — the man searching for remains, the Dutch soldier with PTSD who can’t seem to leave Bosnia. Pellegrin recently shared with me what it was like photographing the annual Srebrenica memorial ceremony and burial of the dead.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/06/01/magazine/01cover_text/01cover_text-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/06/01/magazine/01cover_text/01cover_text-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 730}}, {'rank': 0, '